(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e2b.97f8817620.js"]={"18":[{"id":"sdl18_q01","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the organization of the cerebellar cortex, deep nuclei, functional zones, and peduncles.","batch":1,"stem":"Which cell layer of the cerebellar cortex contains the neurons whose axons form the sole output of the cortex itself?","choices":{"A":"The molecular layer, containing stellate cells, basket cells, and parallel fibers","B":"The granular layer, containing granule cells and mossy fiber terminals","C":"The subcortical white matter, containing all afferent and efferent cortical fibers","D":"The Purkinje cell layer, positioned at the molecular-granular layer interface","E":"The glomerular layer, a purely histologic term not used in cerebellar cortex anatomy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The Purkinje cell layer sits at the interface of the outer molecular layer and inner granular layer, and its large Purkinje cell somata are the only neurons whose axons leave the cerebellar cortex, projecting inhibitory output to the cerebellar and vestibular nuclei. The molecular layer instead contains interneurons and afferent parallel and climbing fibers rather than the cortical output cells, the granular layer contains the granule cells that relay mossy fiber input rather than generating cortical output, the subcortical white matter carries fibers rather than containing cell bodies, and glomerular layer is not a recognized term in cerebellar histology.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Purkinje cells are inhibitory, GABAergic, and provide the exclusive output pathway from cerebellar cortex to the deep nuclei, a frequently tested cellular fact. COMLEX: Recognizing Purkinje cell degeneration as the shared final pathway in many cerebellar ataxias helps connect diverse etiologies to one common histologic finding.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — identifying the major external and internal landmarks of the pons, the same landmark-based organizational approach applied here to cerebellar cortex, deep nuclei, functional zones, and peduncles","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q02","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the organization of the cerebellar cortex, deep nuclei, functional zones, and peduncles.","batch":1,"stem":"Purkinje cells of the medial vermal cortex send their axons primarily to which deep cerebellar nucleus?","choices":{"A":"The red nucleus, a midbrain structure that lies entirely outside the cerebellum","B":"The dentate nucleus, the most lateral and largest of the deep cerebellar nuclei","C":"The globose nucleus, part of the interposed nuclear group flanking the vermis","D":"The emboliform nucleus, the anterior member of the interposed group","E":"The fastigial nucleus, the most medial of the four deep cerebellar nuclei"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The cerebellar cortex and nuclei are organized as parallel medial-to-lateral zones, and Purkinje cells of the medial vermis project to the underlying fastigial nucleus, the most medial of the four deep nuclei. Purkinje cells of the lateral hemisphere instead project to the dentate nucleus rather than the vermal fastigial nucleus, the globose and emboliform nuclei together form the interposed group that instead receives projections from the intermediate, paravermal zone, and the red nucleus is a midbrain relay structure that receives cerebellar output secondarily rather than being a deep cerebellar nucleus itself.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The medial-to-lateral sequence, fastigial, globose, emboliform, dentate, mirrors the vermis-intermediate-hemisphere cortical zones and is a classic mnemonic pairing tested on exams. First Aid mnemonic for the lateral-to-medial order: don't eat greasy foods, dentate, emboliform, globose, fastigial. COMLEX: Knowing that vermal Purkinje output converges on the fastigial nucleus explains why vermal lesions produce midline, truncal deficits rather than limb findings.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — identifying the major external and internal landmarks of the pons, the same landmark-based organizational approach applied here to cerebellar cortex, deep nuclei, functional zones, and peduncles","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Purkinje cells of the lateral ","C":"Purkinje cells of the lateral "}},{"id":"sdl18_q03","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the organization of the cerebellar cortex, deep nuclei, functional zones, and peduncles.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the three cerebellar peduncles is described as purely afferent, carrying only crossed fibers into the cerebellum?","choices":{"A":"The superior cerebellar peduncle, carrying mostly efferent fibers from the deep nuclei","B":"The inferior cerebellar peduncle, carrying both afferent and vestibular reciprocal fibers","C":"The middle cerebellar peduncle, carrying crossed pontocerebellar fibers exclusively","D":"The restiform body, a component that also carries reciprocal vestibulocerebellar fibers","E":"The brachium conjunctivum, a peduncle whose axons originate from deep nuclei"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The middle cerebellar peduncle, or brachium pontis, is purely afferent, carrying only crossed pontocerebellar fibers that arise from the contralateral basilar pontine nuclei and enter the cerebellum after crossing the midline. The superior cerebellar peduncle is instead primarily efferent, originating from the dentate, emboliform, and globose nuclei en route to the red nucleus and thalamus, the inferior cerebellar peduncle carries mixed afferent input including reciprocal vestibulocerebellar connections rather than being purely afferent from one source, the restiform body is simply the larger afferent component of the inferior peduncle rather than a separate purely afferent structure, and the brachium conjunctivum is simply another name for the superior peduncle, which is efferent rather than afferent.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The middle cerebellar peduncle as the sole purely afferent peduncle, carrying crossed corticopontocerebellar fibers, explains why cerebral hemisphere lesions cause contralateral hemiparesis with cerebellar signs on the same side as the weakness. COMLEX: This crossed relay is a favorite conceptual pearl for why a stroke patient's ataxia localizes to the same side as their weakness rather than the opposite side.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — identifying the major external and internal landmarks of the pons, the same landmark-based organizational approach applied here to cerebellar cortex, deep nuclei, functional zones, and peduncles","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q04","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Predict the clinical effects of lesions in the cerebellar vermis, intermediate zone, lateral hemisphere, flocculonodular lobe, and deep nuclei.","batch":1,"stem":"A lesion confined to the cerebellar vermis most characteristically produces which clinical pattern?","choices":{"A":"Ipsilateral limb dysmetria with a pronounced action or intention tremor","B":"Positional vertigo with nystagmus and impaired smooth pursuit","C":"Dysdiadochokinesia with past-pointing on finger-to-nose testing bilaterally","D":"Slowly progressive symmetric proximal limb weakness without any sensory loss","E":"Truncal ataxia with a wide-based gait and a tendency to fall backward"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The vermis projects to the fastigial nucleus and governs midline, axial control, so its damage produces truncal ataxia, titubation, and a wide-based unsteady gait with falling in any direction, often backward. Ipsilateral limb dysmetria with intention tremor instead reflects intermediate or lateral zone damage rather than vermal, positional vertigo with nystagmus instead reflects flocculonodular rather than vermal damage, dysdiadochokinesia with past-pointing again reflects the intermediate or lateral zones rather than the vermis, and symmetric proximal weakness without sensory loss describes a myopathic pattern unrelated to cerebellar localization.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Truncal ataxia and a wide-based, staggering gait from vermal disease is a classic exam finding in alcoholic cerebellar degeneration, which preferentially damages the anterior vermis. COMLEX: A wide-based gait that worsens with eyes open just as much as closed, unlike sensory ataxia, points toward a cerebellar rather than dorsal column cause.","crossRef":"📎 Recognizing cerebellar and other named gait patterns on the neurologic examination, the clinical correlate of the zone-to-deficit mapping developed here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q05","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Predict the clinical effects of lesions in the cerebellar vermis, intermediate zone, lateral hemisphere, flocculonodular lobe, and deep nuclei.","batch":1,"stem":"Damage isolated to the flocculonodular lobe of the cerebellum is expected to produce which combination of findings?","choices":{"A":"Ipsilateral limb ataxia with dysmetria and a coarse intention tremor on reaching","B":"Vertigo, positional nystagmus, and truncal instability from vestibular loss","C":"Contralateral spastic hemiparesis with an associated ipsilateral abducens palsy","D":"Isolated hand clumsiness with dysdiadochokinesia sparing all axial function","E":"Progressive dysarthria and dysphagia without any limb or truncal involvement"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The flocculonodular lobe is the vestibulocerebellum, richly interconnected with the vestibular nuclei through the inferior cerebellar peduncle, so its damage produces vestibulocerebellar dysfunction: severe disequilibrium, vertigo, positional nystagmus, truncal ataxia, and impaired tracking eye movements. Ipsilateral limb ataxia with a coarse intention tremor instead reflects intermediate or lateral hemisphere damage rather than flocculonodular, contralateral hemiparesis with an abducens palsy describes a medial pontine rather than cerebellar syndrome, isolated hand clumsiness sparing axial function again localizes laterally rather than to the flocculonodular lobe, and isolated dysarthria and dysphagia without limb or truncal signs suggests a lower cranial nerve or bulbar process rather than flocculonodular disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The flocculonodular lobe's dedicated vestibular connections make it the classic site of pediatric medulloblastoma, presenting with truncal ataxia and early vertigo or nystagmus rather than limb clumsiness. COMLEX: Positional nystagmus combined with a wide-based, unsteady gait but preserved limb coordination should prompt evaluation of the flocculonodular lobe rather than the hemispheres.","crossRef":"📎 Recognizing cerebellar and other named gait patterns on the neurologic examination, the clinical correlate of the zone-to-deficit mapping developed here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q06","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Predict the clinical effects of lesions in the cerebellar vermis, intermediate zone, lateral hemisphere, flocculonodular lobe, and deep nuclei.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with a lesion restricted to the intermediate, or paravermal, zone of the cerebellum is most likely to show which finding?","choices":{"A":"Ipsilateral distal limb ataxia with dysmetria localized to that same limb","B":"Bilateral truncal titubation with an unsteady, wide-based stance while standing","C":"Contralateral loss of pain and temperature sensation over the trunk and limbs","D":"Bilateral horizontal nystagmus with impaired vestibulo-ocular reflex suppression","E":"Progressive bilateral hand weakness sparing coordination and reflex arcs entirely"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The intermediate zone projects to the interposed nuclei, globose and emboliform, and governs distal limb execution, so its damage produces ipsilateral limb ataxia and dysmetria confined largely to that limb rather than axial or bilateral findings. Bilateral truncal titubation instead reflects vermal rather than intermediate zone disease, contralateral pain and temperature loss describes an anterolateral system lesion unrelated to cerebellar zones, bilateral nystagmus with impaired vestibulo-ocular suppression instead reflects flocculonodular rather than intermediate zone damage, and bilateral hand weakness sparing coordination describes a motor rather than cerebellar process entirely.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ipsilateral, distal, limb-restricted ataxia from intermediate zone damage bridges the fully axial vermal syndrome and the more complex lateral hemisphere syndrome on the medial-to-lateral spectrum of cerebellar organization. COMLEX: Testing heel-to-shin coordination isolates intermediate zone function, distinct from testing truncal stability, which isolates vermal function.","crossRef":"📎 Recognizing cerebellar and other named gait patterns on the neurologic examination, the clinical correlate of the zone-to-deficit mapping developed here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q07","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Differentiate AICA, basilar paramedian, and SCA-related patterns involving the pons and cerebellum.","batch":1,"stem":"Long circumferential branches of the basilar artery supply the pontine tegmentum. Beginning at about the level of the principal sensory and motor trigeminal nuclei, which artery supplements the supply to the rostral pontine tegmentum?","choices":{"A":"The anterior inferior cerebellar artery","B":"The superior cerebellar artery","C":"The posterior inferior cerebellar artery","D":"The posterior cerebral artery","E":"The anterior spinal artery"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Internal pontine areas are served by basilar branches: paramedian branches to the medial basilar pons, short circumferential branches to its lateral part, and long circumferential branches to the tegmentum plus a wedge of the middle cerebellar peduncle. At caudal levels, around the facial colliculus, the long circumferential supply is supplemented by the anterior inferior cerebellar artery; rostrally, beginning at about the principal sensory and motor trigeminal nuclei, it is supplemented by the superior cerebellar artery. The posterior inferior cerebellar artery serves the lateral medulla and inferior cerebellum, the posterior cerebral artery serves the midbrain and occipital lobe, and the anterior spinal artery serves the medial medulla.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pontine tegmentum: AICA supplements caudally, SCA supplements rostrally; the cranial nerve involved points to the level and the vessel. COMLEX: An SCA infarct with contralateral pain loss and ipsilateral ataxia reflects this rostral tegmental supply.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 4 — distinguishing medial and lateral pontine syndromes, extended here to the cerebellar arteries that supplement tegmental supply at each level","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"At caudal levels, around the f"}},{"id":"sdl18_q08","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Differentiate AICA, basilar paramedian, and SCA-related patterns involving the pons and cerebellum.","batch":1,"stem":"Which artery supplies the more lateral areas of the inferior cerebellar surface, parts of the middle cerebellar peduncle, a small caudal region of the dentate nucleus, and the choroid plexus lying in the subarachnoid space at the cerebellopontine angle?","choices":{"A":"The superior cerebellar artery","B":"The posterior inferior cerebellar artery","C":"The posterior cerebral artery","D":"The anterior inferior cerebellar artery","E":"The vertebral artery directly"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Branches of the anterior inferior cerebellar artery distribute to the more lateral areas of the inferior cerebellar surface and also serve parts of the middle cerebellar peduncle, a small caudal region of the dentate nucleus, and the choroid plexus in the subarachnoid space at the cerebellopontine angle. The superior cerebellar artery serves the superior cerebellar surface, the fastigial, globose, and emboliform nuclei, and most of the dentate. The posterior inferior cerebellar artery serves the medial inferior surface, the nodulus, and the choroid plexus of the fourth ventricle. The posterior cerebral artery supplies the midbrain and occipital lobe, and direct vertebral branches supply the anterolateral medulla.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cerebellar arterial map: SCA superior surface and most deep nuclei; AICA lateral inferior surface, part of the MCP, and the angle; PICA medial inferior surface and nodulus. COMLEX: A lateral inferior cerebellar infarct with hearing loss and facial weakness points to the AICA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 4 — distinguishing medial from lateral pontine syndromes, extended here to the AICA's cerebellar territory","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q09","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Differentiate AICA, basilar paramedian, and SCA-related patterns involving the pons and cerebellum.","batch":1,"stem":"Occlusion of the superior cerebellar artery is expected to produce severe, long-lasting ipsilateral cerebellar signs primarily because its territory includes which structures?","choices":{"A":"Only the superior cerebellar cortex, sparing all four deep cerebellar nuclei entirely","B":"The inferior cerebellar surface together with the nodulus and the choroid plexus","C":"The superior cerebellar surface, the superior peduncle, and most deep nuclei","D":"The middle cerebellar peduncle together with the lateral cochlear and vestibular nuclei","E":"The medial vermis together with the fastigial nucleus and the posterior inferior surface"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The superior cerebellar artery supplies the superior cerebellar surface, the superior cerebellar peduncle, the rostral lateral pontine tegmentum, and most of the deep cerebellar nuclei, so damage to this deep nuclear territory produces severe and persistent, rather than transient, ipsilateral cerebellar signs, together with contralateral body and facial pain and temperature loss and ipsilateral Horner syndrome, while sparing corticospinal motor strength. The territory does not spare all four deep nuclei, since SCA supplies most of them, the inferior cerebellar surface with the nodulus and choroid plexus instead describes posterior inferior cerebellar artery territory, the middle cerebellar peduncle with the lateral cochlear and vestibular nuclei instead describes anterior inferior cerebellar artery territory, and the medial vermis with the fastigial nucleus and posterior inferior surface again describes posterior inferior cerebellar artery territory rather than SCA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SCA infarcts cause disproportionately severe and durable cerebellar deficits compared with cortex-only lesions, because the artery directly supplies the deep nuclei rather than cortex alone, a mechanistic distinction worth memorizing. COMLEX: The combination of ipsilateral Horner syndrome with contralateral facial and body pain and temperature loss helps localize a posterior circulation stroke specifically to SCA territory.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 4 — distinguishing medial pontine syndromes from lateral pontine syndromes, extended here in the same lecture to add the superior cerebellar artery and the cerebellar signs of each named vascular syndrome","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The territory does not spare a"}},{"id":"sdl18_q10","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the organization of the cerebellar cortex, deep nuclei, functional zones, and peduncles.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher injects an anterograde tracer into the basilar pontine nuclei and follows the labeled axons into the cerebellar cortex. These afferents arise outside the cerebellum and are not climbing fibers. In which cortical layer, and on which cells, do most of these fibers end?","choices":{"A":"In the molecular layer, wrapping around individual Purkinje cell dendrites","B":"In the granular layer, as mossy fibers synapsing with granule cells","C":"In the Purkinje cell layer, directly on Purkinje cell somata","D":"In the subcortical white matter, on Golgi cell bodies","E":"In the molecular layer, on basket and stellate cell bodies"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Mossy fibers originate from cells in many nuclei of the brainstem and spinal cord, including the basilar pontine nuclei, and end in the granular layer, which contains granule cells, Golgi cells, and the mossy fiber terminals; granule cell axons then form the parallel fibers of the molecular layer. Climbing fibers, which come from the contralateral inferior olive, are the afferents that wrap around Purkinje cell dendrites in the molecular layer. Purkinje somata form a single layer but are not the main target of mossy fibers. Golgi cells sit in the granular layer, not the white matter, and basket and stellate cells are molecular layer interneurons.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Two cerebellar afferent types: climbing fibers (inferior olive, molecular layer, Purkinje dendrites) and mossy fibers (all other sources, granular layer, granule cells). COMLEX: Purkinje cells are the sole output of the cortex and are inhibitory to the deep nuclei.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 3 — pontocerebellar fibers passing through the pons, followed here to their mossy fiber endings in the cerebellar cortex","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q11","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the organization of the cerebellar cortex, deep nuclei, functional zones, and peduncles.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher studying cerebellar circuitry traces a fiber type that originates from a single well-defined brainstem nucleus, crosses the midline, ascends into the cerebellar white matter, and wraps extensively around individual Purkinje cell dendrites in the molecular layer, producing a powerful all-or-none excitatory response. Which fiber type and origin does this describe?","choices":{"A":"Mossy fibers, which arise from pontine, spinal, and vestibular sources","B":"Parallel fibers, which arise locally from granule cells within the granular layer","C":"Climbing fibers, which arise from the contralateral inferior olivary nucleus","D":"Purkinje cell axons, which arise from Purkinje somata at the cortical output layer","E":"Golgi cell axons, which arise from inhibitory interneurons within the granular layer"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Climbing fibers arise from the contralateral inferior olivary nucleus, cross the midline, and ascend into the cerebellar white matter to wrap around individual Purkinje cell dendrites in the molecular layer, producing a powerful, all-or-none complex spike, a distinctive one-to-one relationship unlike any other cerebellar afferent. Mossy fibers instead arise from multiple brainstem and spinal sources and synapse on granule cells within the granular layer rather than directly on Purkinje dendrites, parallel fibers are the axons of granule cells themselves rather than an independent brainstem-derived afferent, Purkinje cell axons are cortical efferents traveling to the deep nuclei rather than an afferent fiber type, and Golgi cell axons are local inhibitory interneuron fibers confined within the cortex rather than a long, crossed brainstem projection.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The one-climbing-fiber-to-one-Purkinje-cell relationship from the contralateral inferior olive is a classic distinguishing fact from the many-to-one mossy fiber to granule cell relationship. COMLEX: Olivary degeneration disrupting climbing fiber input is implicated in certain hereditary ataxias, linking a specific afferent pathway to a recognizable clinical syndrome.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 8 — predicting clinical findings from inferior olive lesions in the medulla, the same nucleus whose crossed climbing fiber output is traced here into the cerebellar cortex","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q12","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the organization of the cerebellar cortex, deep nuclei, functional zones, and peduncles.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man undergoes MRI after a posterior fossa mass is found to compress the cerebellar white matter near the roof of the fourth ventricle. The radiologist notes the mass abuts a peduncle that arises predominantly from the dentate, emboliform, and globose nuclei and carries their output rostrally toward the red nucleus and ventrolateral thalamus. Which peduncle is most likely compressed?","choices":{"A":"The superior cerebellar peduncle, the primary efferent pathway of the deep nuclei","B":"The middle cerebellar peduncle, the primary afferent pathway from the pontine nuclei","C":"The inferior cerebellar peduncle, containing the restiform body and vestibular fibers","D":"The juxtarestiform body, a purely reciprocal vestibulocerebellar connection alone","E":"The corticospinal tract, a descending motor pathway unrelated to cerebellar output"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The superior cerebellar peduncle, or brachium conjunctivum, originates predominantly from the dentate, emboliform, and globose nuclei and forms the lateral wall of the fourth ventricle before ascending to the red nucleus and ventrolateral and ventral anterior thalamic nuclei, making it the deep nuclei's primary efferent route. The middle cerebellar peduncle instead is a purely afferent pathway from the contralateral pontine nuclei rather than an efferent nuclear pathway, the inferior cerebellar peduncle carries the restiform body's afferent fibers and vestibular connections rather than the described efferent output, the juxtarestiform body is only a component of the inferior peduncle carrying reciprocal vestibular fibers rather than the full described pathway, and the corticospinal tract is an unrelated descending motor pathway that does not arise from cerebellar nuclei at all.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The superior cerebellar peduncle as the deep nuclei's chief efferent route toward the red nucleus and thalamus is essential for understanding why deep nuclear or peduncular lesions cause more severe, lasting deficits than isolated cortical lesions. COMLEX: Localizing a posterior fossa mass to the superior peduncle predicts a worse, more persistent coordination deficit than a mass confined to cerebellar cortex alone.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 1 — identifying the major external and internal landmarks of the pons, since the superior cerebellar peduncle forms the lateral wall of the fourth ventricle at the level covered in that objective","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q13","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Predict the clinical effects of lesions in the cerebellar vermis, intermediate zone, lateral hemisphere, flocculonodular lobe, and deep nuclei.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman develops sudden ipsilateral limb ataxia and dysmetria after a small cerebellar cortical infarct. Six weeks later, her coordination has nearly returned to baseline. A colleague asks how her prognosis would differ if the infarct had instead destroyed the underlying deep cerebellar nucleus and superior peduncle rather than cortex alone. Which statement best answers this question?","choices":{"A":"Deep nuclear damage would actually recover faster than the isolated cortical lesion","B":"Deep nuclear damage produces deficits identical in severity and course to cortical","C":"Deep nuclear damage would produce contralateral rather than ipsilateral findings","D":"Deep nuclear damage would produce vertigo and nystagmus rather than any limb ataxia","E":"Deep nuclear or peduncular damage produces more severe and far more persistent deficits"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Isolated cortical lesions often show partial functional recovery over weeks because remaining cortical and nuclear circuitry can partially compensate, whereas lesions of the deep cerebellar nuclei or the superior cerebellar peduncle destroy the final common output pathway itself, producing deficits that are more severe and persist for months, years, or permanently. Deep nuclear damage does not recover faster, since it in fact recovers more slowly and less completely than cortical damage, the severity and time course are not identical, since deep nuclear damage is characteristically worse and more lasting, deep nuclear damage does not shift the laterality of findings, since cerebellar signs remain ipsilateral to the lesion regardless of cortical versus nuclear location, and deep nuclear damage does not specifically convert limb ataxia into a vestibular presentation, since that pattern instead reflects flocculonodular rather than deep nuclear involvement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The distinction between transient cortical cerebellar deficits and severe, persistent deep nuclear or peduncular deficits is a high-yield prognostic concept for cerebellar stroke and tumor vignettes. COMLEX: Counseling a patient on expected recovery after a cerebellar infarct should depend on whether imaging shows cortex-only or deep nuclear involvement.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — distinguishing medial from lateral pontine syndromes by which specific structures are damaged, the same structure-to-severity logic applied here to cortical versus deep nuclear cerebellar damage","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Deep nuclear damage does not r","D":"Deep nuclear damage does not r"}},{"id":"sdl18_q14","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Predict the clinical effects of lesions in the cerebellar vermis, intermediate zone, lateral hemisphere, flocculonodular lobe, and deep nuclei.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old right-handed man presents after a small right cerebellar hemisphere infarct. On examination he overshoots when reaching for a target with his right hand, shows rapid alternating hand movements that are slow and irregular, and cannot suppress a limb's oscillation after a sudden push is released. Which cerebellar zone best explains this constellation of findings?","choices":{"A":"The vermal zone, which governs midline truncal posture and stance rather than the limb","B":"The flocculonodular lobe, which governs vestibular reflexes rather than limb movement","C":"The intermediate zone alone, which governs distal execution but not movement planning","D":"The lateral hemisphere zone, which governs planning and timing of complex movement","E":"The dorsal spinocerebellar pathway, which relays proprioception rather than motor timing"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The lateral hemisphere zone projects to the dentate nucleus and is responsible for planning and timing of complex voluntary movement, and its damage produces ipsilateral dysmetria, or past-pointing, dysdiadochokinesia on rapid alternating movement testing, and an abnormal rebound phenomenon, exactly the pattern described. The vermal zone instead governs axial, truncal posture rather than the fine, planned limb movements described, the flocculonodular lobe governs vestibular reflex function rather than limb dysmetria or rebound, the intermediate zone alone governs simpler distal limb execution without the higher-order planning and timing deficits captured by dysdiadochokinesia and rebound, and the dorsal spinocerebellar pathway is a proprioceptive afferent route rather than the zone responsible for generating this motor output pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Dysmetria, dysdiadochokinesia, and an abnormal rebound phenomenon together localize precisely to the lateral cerebellar hemisphere and dentate nucleus, a classic bedside triad. COMLEX: Finger-to-nose and rapid alternating movement testing are the standard bedside maneuvers used to elicit this lateral hemisphere pattern during the neurologic examination.","crossRef":"📖 Recognizing cerebellar gait and limb-coordination patterns on the neurologic examination, the bedside correlate of localizing dysmetria and dysdiadochokinesia to the lateral hemisphere zone","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q15","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Predict the clinical effects of lesions in the cerebellar vermis, intermediate zone, lateral hemisphere, flocculonodular lobe, and deep nuclei.","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-year-old girl presents with several weeks of unsteady, wide-based walking and new episodic vertigo with positional nystagmus, but her parents note her hand coordination and drawing remain completely normal. Imaging reveals a midline posterior fossa mass compressing the roof of the fourth ventricle. Which cerebellar structure does this presentation best localize to?","choices":{"A":"The flocculonodular lobe, given preserved limb coordination with prominent vertigo","B":"The lateral hemisphere, given the combination of vertigo and truncal gait instability","C":"The intermediate zone, given the child's preserved hand coordination on drawing tasks","D":"The dentate nucleus specifically, given the severity and persistence of her symptoms","E":"The superior cerebellar peduncle, given the midline location of the compressing mass"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Preserved limb coordination together with prominent vertigo, positional nystagmus, and truncal gait instability localizes to the flocculonodular lobe, the vestibulocerebellum, a pattern classically seen with pediatric midline posterior fossa tumors such as medulloblastoma that compress this structure and the fourth ventricle roof while sparing the hemispheres. The lateral hemisphere would instead be expected to produce ipsilateral limb dysmetria and dysdiadochokinesia, which are explicitly absent here, the intermediate zone similarly governs limb execution rather than vestibular symptoms and would not explain preserved drawing alongside vertigo, the dentate nucleus is a lateral hemisphere structure whose damage would again produce limb, not vestibular, findings, and the superior cerebellar peduncle's midline location near the fourth ventricle roof is not itself diagnostic, since peduncular compression would be expected to add limb or motor findings rather than the pure vestibulocerebellar picture described.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A pediatric midline posterior fossa mass causing truncal ataxia and vertigo with spared limb coordination is the classic presentation of medulloblastoma arising from the flocculonodular region. COMLEX: Distinguishing preserved fine motor coordination from truncal and vestibular symptoms helps localize pediatric posterior fossa tumors before imaging is obtained.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 2 — localizing the vestibulocochlear nerve nucleus in the pons, the brainstem partner structure whose fibers reciprocally connect with the flocculonodular lobe implicated in this vignette","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q16","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Differentiate AICA, basilar paramedian, and SCA-related patterns involving the pons and cerebellum.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman has sudden severe vertigo, nausea, nystagmus, and truncal ataxia with inability to stand unaided, but normal limb strength, facial sensation, swallowing, hearing, and facial movement. MRI shows an infarct confined to the medial inferior surface of the right cerebellum including the nodulus, with the medulla spared. Which vessel occlusion best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Right superior cerebellar artery, supplying the superior cerebellar surface","B":"Right anterior inferior cerebellar artery, supplying the lateral inferior cerebellar surface","C":"Paramedian basilar branches, supplying the medial basilar pons","D":"Right posterior inferior cerebellar artery, through its medial cerebellar branches","E":"Right vertebral artery, through its direct anterolateral medullary branches"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The posterior inferior cerebellar artery branches to the more medial regions of the inferior cerebellar surface and to the nodulus, and also supplies the choroid plexus of the fourth ventricle and the posterolateral medulla. When only its cerebellar branches are affected, infarction of the nodulus and medial inferior cerebellum produces vertigo, nystagmus, and truncal ataxia without the brainstem signs of a lateral medullary syndrome. The superior cerebellar artery serves the superior cerebellar surface and most deep nuclei. The anterior inferior cerebellar artery serves the more lateral inferior surface and caudal lateral pons, often with hearing loss and facial weakness. Paramedian basilar occlusion causes hemiparesis, and direct vertebral branches supply the medulla, which is spared.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cerebellar surfaces: superior from SCA, lateral inferior from AICA, medial inferior and nodulus from PICA. COMLEX: An isolated cerebellar infarct can mimic peripheral vertigo; inability to stand and absence of features of a peripheral cause should prompt urgent imaging.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — predicting deficits caused by PICA, AICA, and SCA lesions, applied here to PICA's cerebellar territory without medullary involvement","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q17","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Differentiate AICA, basilar paramedian, and SCA-related patterns involving the pons and cerebellum.","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman presents with acute onset of severe left-sided limb ataxia, dysmetria, and dysdiadochokinesia along with a left-sided miotic pupil and mild ptosis. She also reports decreased pain and temperature sensation over the right side of her body and face, but her strength remains intact bilaterally. Which vessel occlusion best explains this constellation?","choices":{"A":"Left anterior inferior cerebellar artery, explaining facial and hearing deficits","B":"Left superior cerebellar artery, explaining the severe deep nuclear cerebellar signs","C":"Left paramedian basilar branches, explaining corticospinal and abducens findings","D":"Right posterior inferior cerebellar artery, explaining a crossed presentation","E":"Left posterior spinal artery, explaining loss of proprioception in both lower limbs"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Severe, deep nuclear cerebellar signs, ipsilateral Horner syndrome from descending sympathetic fiber involvement, and contralateral body and facial pain and temperature loss with preserved strength together describe left superior cerebellar artery occlusion, since SCA supplies the superior cerebellar surface, superior peduncle, and most of the deep cerebellar nuclei along with the rostral lateral pontine tegmentum. Left AICA occlusion would instead be expected to add facial weakness and hearing loss rather than the Horner syndrome and facial pain loss described, left paramedian basilar branch occlusion would instead produce contralateral hemiparesis and an ipsilateral abducens palsy rather than sparing strength, right PICA occlusion would misassign the laterality of the deficits and would instead produce medullary rather than cerebellar deep nuclear signs, and the posterior spinal artery supplies the spinal cord dorsal columns rather than the brainstem or cerebellum and would not explain any of these cranial findings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Severe deep nuclear cerebellar signs with ipsilateral Horner syndrome and contralateral face-plus-body pain and temperature loss is the classic SCA pattern, distinguished from AICA by the absence of facial weakness or hearing loss. COMLEX: The presence of Horner syndrome alongside severe, persistent limb ataxia should prompt consideration of SCA territory rather than the more caudal AICA or paramedian territories.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — distinguishing medial from lateral pontine syndromes, extended here in the same lecture to differentiate this rostral SCA pattern from the caudal AICA and paramedian patterns using cerebellar and Horner findings","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Left AICA occlusion would inst","D":"Left AICA occlusion would inst","E":"Left AICA occlusion would inst"}},{"id":"sdl18_q18","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Differentiate AICA, basilar paramedian, and SCA-related patterns involving the pons and cerebellum.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man develops sudden right-sided lateral rectus weakness with an inability to abduct the right eye, together with left-sided weakness of the face, arm, and leg. He denies vertigo, hearing changes, or limb incoordination, and sensory testing is normal. Which vessel territory best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Anterior inferior cerebellar artery, sparing the medial basilar corticospinal fibers","B":"Superior cerebellar artery, sparing the deep cerebellar nuclei and superior peduncle","C":"Paramedian branches of the basilar artery, sparing the lateral tegmentum","D":"Posterior inferior cerebellar artery, sparing the anterior spinal artery territory","E":"Long circumferential branches alone, sparing the abducens nerve fascicle entirely"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ipsilateral abducens palsy with contralateral hemiparesis of the face, arm, and leg, and the absence of vertigo, hearing loss, limb ataxia, or sensory loss, describes the medial pontine syndrome from paramedian basilar artery branch occlusion, since these branches supply the medial basilar pons containing the corticospinal fibers and the abducens fascicle while sparing the lateral tegmentum entirely. AICA occlusion would instead spare the medial corticospinal fibers while damaging the lateral tegmentum, producing facial weakness, hearing loss, and ataxia rather than sparing them, superior cerebellar artery occlusion would instead damage the deep cerebellar nuclei and superior peduncle, producing severe ataxia rather than sparing coordination, posterior inferior cerebellar artery occlusion is a medullary rather than pontine event and would not produce this pattern at all, and long circumferential branches alone supply only the lateral basilar pons rather than the full paramedian territory responsible for this abducens and corticospinal pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Isolated ipsilateral abducens palsy with contralateral hemiparesis, sparing hearing, vertigo, ataxia, and sensation, is the textbook medial pontine, Foville-type, presentation from paramedian basilar occlusion. COMLEX: Absence of vertigo, hearing loss, and limb ataxia in a patient with new eye movement and contralateral weakness findings helps a clinician exclude AICA or SCA territory in favor of this medial paramedian pattern.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — distinguishing medial from lateral pontine syndromes, the direct same-lecture continuation whose medial pontine, paramedian basilar artery framework is applied here alongside the cerebellar vascular syndromes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"AICA occlusion would instead s","E":"AICA occlusion would instead s"}},{"id":"sdl18_bloom_q01","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Differentiate AICA, basilar paramedian, and SCA-related patterns involving the pons and cerebellum.","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman with hypertension presents with a 2-hour history of sudden-onset severe vertigo, slurred speech, and imbalance.\n- BP 172/96, HR 88, afebrile\n- Dysarthria; voice and swallowing normal\n- Left-sided limb and gait ataxia with dysmetria on finger-to-nose and heel-to-shin testing\n- Left ptosis and miosis\n- Decreased pain and temperature sensation over the right face, trunk, and limbs\n- Hearing intact bilaterally; facial strength full and symmetric\nThis patient's condition is most likely caused by ischemia in a region supplied by the ___","choices":{"A":"anterior inferior cerebellar artery","B":"superior cerebellar artery","C":"posterior inferior cerebellar artery","D":"paramedian basilar branches","E":"posterior spinal artery"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ipsilateral limb and gait ataxia with dysarthria and ipsilateral Horner signs, combined with contralateral pain and temperature loss over the face and body (the trigeminothalamic and spinothalamic fibers have both crossed by rostral pontine levels), with preserved hearing and no bulbar signs, localizes to the superior cerebellar artery territory at the rostral pons–cerebellum junction. The anterior inferior cerebellar artery would also produce ipsilateral hearing loss and facial weakness, which are absent. The posterior inferior cerebellar artery supplies the lateral medulla and would add hoarseness and dysphagia from nucleus ambiguus involvement, with ipsilateral rather than contralateral facial sensory loss. Paramedian basilar branches supply the medial pons and would produce hemiparesis rather than ataxia. The posterior spinal arteries supply the dorsal spinal cord rather than the posterior fossa.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Preserved hearing separates SCA from AICA infarction, and absent bulbar signs with contralateral rather than ipsilateral facial pain/temperature loss separates it from PICA. COMLEX: Ataxia with crossed face-and-body pain/temperature loss and no facial weakness, hearing loss, or hoarseness should raise SCA territory.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 Bloom → Obj 4 — AICA lateral pontine syndrome with ipsilateral hearing loss, contrasted here with the hearing-sparing SCA pattern","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl18_bloom_q02","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Predict the clinical effects of lesions in the cerebellar vermis, intermediate zone, lateral hemisphere, flocculonodular lobe, and deep nuclei.","batch":3,"stem":"An 8-year-old boy is brought in for several weeks of progressive unsteadiness.\n- Wide-based, unsteady gait with truncal sway when sitting unsupported\n- New slurring of speech over the same period\n- Finger-to-nose and heel-to-shin testing normal bilaterally\n- No vertigo, no nystagmus, no limb weakness\nMRI shows a midline posterior fossa mass compressing the roof of the fourth ventricle.\nThe brain structure most likely affected is the ___","choices":{"A":"intermediate zone","B":"lateral hemisphere","C":"flocculonodular lobe","D":"deep nuclei","E":"cerebellar vermis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Truncal and gait ataxia with dysarthria but normal limb coordination on finger-to-nose and heel-to-shin testing is the classic pattern of a midline cerebellar vermis lesion, since the vermis controls axial and proximal, rather than distal limb, coordination. The intermediate zone and lateral hemisphere govern ipsilateral limb coordination, which is normal here, so a lesion there would instead produce dysmetria on finger-to-nose testing. The flocculonodular lobe governs balance and eye movements and would be expected to produce nystagmus and vertigo, which are both absent. The deep nuclei relay output from the entire cerebellar cortex and a discrete lesion there would not selectively spare limb coordination while impairing gait.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Truncal or gait ataxia with dysarthria and normal limb coordination localizes to the vermis, a classic finding with midline pediatric posterior fossa tumors such as medulloblastoma. COMLEX: Distinguishing normal finger-to-nose testing from an abnormal gait is the key bedside step that separates vermian from hemispheric cerebellar disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 6 — midline vermis lesion localization using the same axial vs. appendicular coordination distinction taught for hemispheric lesions","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl18_bloom_q03","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Predict the clinical effects of lesions in the cerebellar vermis, intermediate zone, lateral hemisphere, flocculonodular lobe, and deep nuclei.","batch":3,"stem":"A 50-year-old man develops sudden-onset right-hand clumsiness after a focal cerebellar infarct.\n- Right-sided dysmetria on finger-to-nose testing, worsening as the hand approaches the target\n- Right-sided intention tremor and dysdiadochokinesia\n- Decomposition of multi-joint reaching movements\n- Gait narrow-based and stable; no truncal sway\n- No dysarthria, no vertigo, no nystagmus\nThe brain structure most likely affected is the ___","choices":{"A":"lateral cerebellar hemisphere","B":"cerebellar vermis","C":"intermediate zone","D":"flocculonodular lobe","E":"deep nuclei"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Isolated ipsilateral distal limb dysmetria and intention tremor with a preserved, stable gait and no dysarthria localizes to the lateral cerebellar hemisphere, which governs fine, distal limb coordination through its projections to the dentate nucleus and ultimately the contralateral motor cortex via the thalamus. The vermis controls axial and gait stability, which is normal here, so a vermian lesion would instead produce truncal sway and a wide-based gait. The intermediate zone (interposed nuclei) adjusts ongoing limb execution; the combination of dysdiadochokinesia, decomposition of multi-joint movement, and timing-dependent intention tremor in a planned reach points more specifically to the lateral hemisphere and dentate. The flocculonodular lobe governs balance and eye movements and would produce vertigo or nystagmus rather than isolated limb dysmetria. A discrete deep nuclear lesion would be expected to produce more severe and persistent findings than a comparably sized cortical lesion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Isolated distal limb dysmetria with a normal gait and no dysarthria is the classic hemispheric, dentate-pathway cerebellar pattern. COMLEX: Preserved gait alongside abnormal finger-to-nose testing is the bedside signature that separates a lateral hemisphere lesion from a vermian one.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 6 — lateral hemisphere and dentate nucleus circuitry contrasted directly against the midline vermis pattern","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl18_bloom_q04","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the organization of the cerebellar cortex, deep nuclei, functional zones, and peduncles.","batch":3,"stem":"A histology unknown shows a monolayer of large, flask-shaped inhibitory neurons positioned exactly at the boundary between the outer and inner cortical layers of the cerebellum.\nDamage confined to this cell layer would eliminate essentially all of which function?","choices":{"A":"cerebellar afferent input","B":"deep nuclear output","C":"cortical output","D":"climbing fiber input","E":"mossy fiber input"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The Purkinje cell layer sits at the interface of the molecular and granular layers, and its cells are the only neurons whose axons leave the cerebellar cortex, so their destruction eliminates cortical output entirely while afferent input pathways remain structurally intact upstream. Cerebellar afferent input arrives via mossy and climbing fibers rather than being generated by Purkinje cells, deep nuclear output would only be secondarily disinhibited rather than eliminated, and climbing fiber and mossy fiber input are both afferent systems that synapse onto, rather than arise from, the Purkinje cell layer.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Purkinje cells are inhibitory, GABAergic, and provide the sole output pathway from cerebellar cortex to the deep nuclei, a frequently tested cellular fact. COMLEX: Purkinje cell degeneration is the shared final histologic pathway in many hereditary and acquired cerebellar ataxias.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 Bloom → Obj 2 — identifying discrete functional cell layers and columns in the pons, applied here to the Purkinje cell layer as the sole cerebellar cortical output.","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl18_bloom_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the organization of the cerebellar cortex, deep nuclei, functional zones, and peduncles.","batch":3,"stem":"During development, the cerebellar plate is divided by transverse fissures. The first fissure to appear separates the flocculonodular lobe caudally from the corpus cerebelli rostrally. This first fissure is the ___","choices":{"A":"posterolateral fissure","B":"primary fissure","C":"horizontal fissure","D":"longitudinal fissure","E":"choroid fissure"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The cerebellum develops from the rhombic lips of the pontine alar plates, which fuse at the midline into the cerebellar plate. The first fissure to appear is the posterolateral fissure, which separates the flocculonodular lobe from the corpus cerebelli; the primary fissure, the second to appear, divides the corpus cerebelli into the anterior and posterior lobes. The horizontal fissure is a later fissure on the posterior lobe. The longitudinal fissure separates the cerebral hemispheres, and the choroid fissure is where choroid plexus invaginates into the ventricles.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Posterolateral fissure first (flocculonodular lobe off), primary fissure second (anterior from posterior lobe); the flocculonodular lobe is the vestibulocerebellum. COMLEX: Midline posterior fossa tumors of childhood often involve the vermis and flocculonodular lobe, producing truncal ataxia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — alar and basal plate organization, extended here to the rhombic lips of the pontine alar plate that form the cerebellum","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)","whyNot":{"B":"The first fissure to appear is"}},{"id":"sdl18_bloom_q06","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the organization of the cerebellar cortex, deep nuclei, functional zones, and peduncles.","batch":3,"stem":"Purkinje cells are the only efferent neurons of the cerebellar cortex. Most Purkinje cell axons end in the cerebellar nuclei, but some leave the cerebellum without synapsing there and end directly in the ___","choices":{"A":"inferior olivary nucleus","B":"basilar pontine nuclei","C":"red nucleus","D":"vestibular nuclei","E":"ventral lateral thalamus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Axons of Purkinje cells pass through the subcortical white matter to end in the cerebellar nuclei and the vestibular nuclei; the direct projection to the vestibular nuclei travels in the juxtarestiform body, the medial part of the inferior cerebellar peduncle that carries cerebellovestibular fibers. The inferior olive sends climbing fibers to the cortex rather than receiving Purkinje axons. The basilar pontine nuclei project to the cerebellum through the middle peduncle. The red nucleus and ventral lateral thalamus receive cerebellar output from the deep nuclei through the superior peduncle, not directly from Purkinje cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Purkinje cells inhibit the deep cerebellar nuclei and, directly, the vestibular nuclei; deep nuclei are the main cerebellar output. COMLEX: The direct cortex-to-vestibular connection explains why flocculonodular lesions disturb balance and eye movements so strongly.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 5 — cerebellar cortex, deep nuclei, and peduncles, applied here to the direct Purkinje projection that bypasses the deep nuclei","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)","whyNot":{"A":"The inferior olive sends climb"}},{"id":"sdl18_bloom_q07","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Predict the clinical effects of lesions in the cerebellar vermis, intermediate zone, lateral hemisphere, flocculonodular lobe, and deep nuclei.","batch":3,"stem":"A 6-year-old girl develops sudden vertigo, gaze-evoked nystagmus, and severe truncal instability with eyes closed.\n- Finger-to-nose testing normal bilaterally\n- Heel-to-shin testing normal bilaterally\n- No dysarthria, no limb weakness\nMRI shows a discrete lesion confined to a single cerebellar region.\nWhich region is most likely affected?","choices":{"A":"cerebellar vermis","B":"flocculonodular lobe","C":"intermediate zone","D":"lateral hemisphere","E":"deep cerebellar nuclei"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Vertigo, nystagmus, and truncal instability out of proportion to any limb dysmetria, with entirely normal finger-to-nose and heel-to-shin testing, localizes to the flocculonodular lobe, the phylogenetically oldest cerebellar region responsible for vestibular and ocular motor control rather than limb or gait coordination. The vermis governs axial and gait coordination and would more typically produce truncal sway with dysarthria rather than prominent vertigo and nystagmus, the intermediate zone and lateral hemisphere govern limb coordination and would be expected to produce dysmetria on finger-to-nose or heel-to-shin testing, which is absent here, and the deep nuclei relay output broadly and a discrete lesion there would not selectively spare all limb coordination.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vertigo and nystagmus with normal limb coordination is the classic flocculonodular, vestibulocerebellar, pattern, distinct from the truncal ataxia of vermian disease. COMLEX: Pediatric flocculonodular tumors such as medulloblastoma variants can present with prominent vertigo and nystagmus before limb findings ever appear.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 6 — the axial vs. appendicular coordination distinction used for vermis and hemisphere lesions, extended here to the vestibular flocculonodular lobe.","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl18_bloom_q08","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Differentiate AICA, basilar paramedian, and SCA-related patterns involving the pons and cerebellum.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient with an infarct from occlusion of paramedian basilar branches at the caudal pons has a left abducens palsy and right hemiparesis. He also cannot move either eye toward the left on attempted horizontal gaze. This additional finding indicates that the lesion has extended posteriorly into the ___","choices":{"A":"pontine nuclei of the basilar pons","B":"middle cerebellar peduncle fibers","C":"paramedian pontine reticular formation","D":"superior olivary nucleus","E":"spinal trigeminal tract and nucleus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Occlusion of paramedian basilar branches at the caudal pons causes the Foville syndrome, an ipsilateral abducens paralysis with contralateral hemiparesis and variable contralateral sensory loss. If the lesion extends posteriorly into the pontine tegmentum, the patient may also have an ipsilateral horizontal gaze paralysis, indicating damage to the medial portions of the pontine reticular formation that coordinate horizontal gaze. The pontine nuclei of the basilar pons and the middle cerebellar peduncle fibers relay cortical input to the cerebellum and cause ataxia when damaged. The superior olive is an auditory nucleus, and the spinal trigeminal tract and nucleus carry facial pain and temperature.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Abducens nerve lesion: ipsilateral lateral rectus weakness; abducens nucleus or paramedian pontine reticular formation lesion: ipsilateral horizontal gaze palsy of both eyes. COMLEX: Gaze palsy toward the lesion side plus contralateral hemiparesis localizes to the caudal pontine tegmentum and base.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 Bloom → Obj 4 — Foville and Millard-Gubler crossed motor patterns from paramedian basilar occlusion, extended here to tegmental gaze involvement","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl18_bloom_q09","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Differentiate AICA, basilar paramedian, and SCA-related patterns involving the pons and cerebellum.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has left lateral rectus weakness and left facial paralysis including the forehead, with loss of pain and temperature sensation on the right trunk and limbs. Strength is full in all four limbs, hearing is normal, and there is no ataxia. MRI shows a small lesion at the level of the facial colliculus that spares the basilar pons. The lesion is confined to the ___","choices":{"A":"caudal pontine tegmentum","B":"midpontine basilar pons","C":"rostral pontine tegmentum","D":"lateral medulla","E":"middle cerebellar peduncle"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A lesion in the caudal pontine tegmentum can damage the abducens and facial nuclei, causing ipsilateral lateral rectus and facial paralysis, together with the anterolateral system, causing contralateral loss of pain and thermal sense on the body, while sparing the corticospinal fibers in the basilar pons. A basilar pontine lesion would cause contralateral hemiparesis. Rostral pontine tegmental lesions involve the trigeminal motor nucleus and roots rather than VI and VII. A lateral medullary lesion causes ipsilateral facial pain loss, hoarseness, and dysphagia rather than abducens and facial palsy. A middle cerebellar peduncle lesion causes ataxia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pontine tegmentum lesions combine cranial nerve nuclei with sensory long tracts and spare strength; basilar lesions add hemiparesis. COMLEX: Ipsilateral VI and VII palsy plus contralateral body pain loss localizes to the caudal tegmentum of the pons.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 Bloom → Obj 4 — tegmental versus basilar pontine lesion reasoning, applied here alongside AICA and paramedian patterns in the vascular differential","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)","whyNot":{"B":"A basilar pontine lesion would"}}],"19":[{"id":"sdl19_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the major external and internal regions of the midbrain: tectum, tegmentum, basis pedunculi, cerebral aqueduct, and cerebral peduncles.","batch":1,"stem":"Which midbrain region lies posterior to the cerebral aqueduct and is marked externally by the superior and inferior colliculi?","choices":{"A":"The basis pedunculi region","B":"The midbrain tegmentum region","C":"The midbrain tectum","D":"The crus cerebri bundle","E":"The substantia nigra nucleus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The tectum is the roof of the midbrain, positioned posterior to the cerebral aqueduct, and its four surface elevations, the corpora quadrigemina, are the paired superior and inferior colliculi. The tegmentum is the central core anterior to the aqueduct, extending to the substantia nigra. The basis pedunculi is the anterolateral portion, formed by the substantia nigra and crus cerebri. The crus cerebri alone is only the descending motor fiber bundle within the basis pedunculi, not a full anterior region. The substantia nigra is one component of the basis pedunculi, not the tectal roof.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Boards test tectum-versus-tegmentum confusion constantly, remember tectum equals roof equals colliculi equals posterior to the aqueduct. COMLEX: Pineal tumors compress the tectal plate and aqueduct, producing Parinaud syndrome with noncommunicating hydrocephalus, a favorite integrated vignette.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 8 — relating the embryonic neural tube cavity to the adult ventricular system, the same cerebral aqueduct traced here as it forms the midbrain's internal ventricular channel between the third and fourth ventricles","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The basis pedunculi is the ant","E":"The substantia nigra is one co"}},{"id":"sdl19_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the major external and internal regions of the midbrain: tectum, tegmentum, basis pedunculi, cerebral aqueduct, and cerebral peduncles.","batch":1,"stem":"The central core of the midbrain, which contains the red nucleus, reticular formation, and the nuclei of cranial nerves III and IV, is best described as which region?","choices":{"A":"The tectum","B":"The tegmentum","C":"The crus cerebri","D":"The cerebral aqueduct","E":"The interpeduncular fossa"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The tegmentum extends from the base of the tectum anteriorly to the substantia nigra and houses ascending and descending tracts, relay nuclei, the red nucleus, the reticular formation, and the oculomotor and trochlear nuclei. The tectum is the posterior colliculus-bearing roof, not the central tract-and-nucleus-bearing core. The crus cerebri is a purely descending motor fiber bundle within the basis pedunculi. The cerebral aqueduct is the narrow CSF channel, not a neural region. The interpeduncular fossa is the external space between the two crura cerebri, not an internal region.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CN III and CN IV nuclei sit in the tegmentum, so a tegmental lesion can produce diplopia alongside contralateral tremor from red nucleus involvement. COMLEX: Reticular formation damage in the tegmentum contributes to altered arousal, relevant to coma localization questions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 8 — relating the embryonic neural tube cavity to the adult ventricular system, the same cerebral aqueduct traced here as it forms the midbrain's internal ventricular channel between the third and fourth ventricles","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the major external and internal regions of the midbrain: tectum, tegmentum, basis pedunculi, cerebral aqueduct, and cerebral peduncles.","batch":1,"stem":"The anterolateral portion of the midbrain, formed by the heavily pigmented dopaminergic nucleus together with the descending motor fiber bundle, is termed which structure?","choices":{"A":"The tegmentum","B":"The tectum","C":"The central gray","D":"The tectal plate","E":"The basis pedunculi"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The basis pedunculi is the anterolateral midbrain region on each side, composed of the pigmented substantia nigra and the anterior-most crus cerebri, which carries massive descending corticospinal, corticobulbar, and corticopontine fiber bundles. The tegmentum is the central core, not the anterolateral zone, and excludes the substantia nigra by definition. The tectum is the posterior roof. The central (periaqueductal) gray is the neuron sleeve surrounding the aqueduct, unrelated to the anterolateral fiber bundle. The tectal (quadrigeminal) plate is formed by the colliculi, again a posterior structure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Substantia nigra pars compacta dopaminergic loss underlies Parkinson disease, and it sits within the basis pedunculi, not the tegmentum, a frequently misplaced fact. COMLEX: The crus cerebri within the basis pedunculi is the site tested for pure contralateral hemiplegia in Weber-type localization.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 8 — relating the embryonic neural tube cavity to the adult ventricular system, the same cerebral aqueduct traced here as it forms the midbrain's internal ventricular channel between the third and fourth ventricles","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate caudal midbrain structures at the inferior colliculus level from rostral midbrain structures at the superior colliculus level.","batch":1,"stem":"A transverse section through the caudal midbrain at the inferior colliculus level shows decussating fibers occupying the central tegmentum. Which structure is decussating at this level?","choices":{"A":"The medial lemniscus","B":"The corticospinal tract","C":"The superior cerebellar peduncle","D":"The spinothalamic tract","E":"The trigeminothalamic tract"},"correct":"C","explanation":"At the inferior colliculus level, the decussation of the superior cerebellar peduncle, also called the brachium conjunctivum, occupies the central tegmentum and is a defining internal hallmark of the caudal midbrain. The medial lemniscus and spinothalamic tract continue rostrally through the tegmentum without crossing again at this level, having already decussated lower in the brainstem or spinal cord. The corticospinal tract runs uncrossed through the crus cerebri at midbrain levels. The trigeminothalamic tracts likewise pass through without a midbrain-level crossing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The superior cerebellar peduncle decussation at the inferior colliculus level is a classic imaging landmark question on axial MRI. COMLEX: This level also carries the trochlear nucleus, so a caudal midbrain lesion pairs cerebellar and CN IV findings.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 8 — the cerebral aqueduct's rostrocaudal continuity, applied here to distinguish the two clinically testable midbrain cross-sectional levels the aqueduct traverses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate caudal midbrain structures at the inferior colliculus level from rostral midbrain structures at the superior colliculus level.","batch":1,"stem":"At the level of the superior colliculus, which nucleus becomes a large, prominent, circular structure within the tegmentum?","choices":{"A":"The red nucleus","B":"The trochlear nucleus","C":"The inferior olivary nucleus","D":"The dorsal raphe nucleus","E":"The mesencephalic trigeminal nucleus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The rostral midbrain, at the superior colliculus level, is characterized by a highly prominent, large circular red nucleus within the tegmentum, alongside the oculomotor nuclear complex. The trochlear nucleus instead belongs to the caudal, inferior colliculus level. The inferior olivary nucleus is a medullary structure, not a midbrain one. The dorsal raphe nucleus is present at midbrain levels but remains a small midline structure, not the large prominent circular landmark. The mesencephalic trigeminal nucleus is a thin lateral cell column along the periaqueductal gray, not a large circular structure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The rostral, red-nucleus-bearing level pairs with CN III exit, so contralateral tremor plus ipsilateral CN III palsy localizes to this level. COMLEX: Red nucleus involvement produces a rubral or Holmes tremor pattern, a testable clinical presentation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 8 — the cerebral aqueduct's rostrocaudal continuity, applied here to distinguish the two clinically testable midbrain cross-sectional levels the aqueduct traverses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate caudal midbrain structures at the inferior colliculus level from rostral midbrain structures at the superior colliculus level.","batch":1,"stem":"Which cranial nerve nucleus is located at the caudal, inferior colliculus level of the midbrain and is closely associated with the medial longitudinal fasciculus?","choices":{"A":"The oculomotor nucleus","B":"The abducens nucleus","C":"The trigeminal motor nucleus","D":"The trochlear nucleus","E":"The facial motor nucleus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The trochlear nucleus sits at the caudal, inferior colliculus level, intimately associated with the medial longitudinal fasciculus on the midline, and its exiting fibers mark the pons-midbrain junction posteriorly. The oculomotor nucleus instead belongs to the rostral, superior colliculus level. The abducens, trigeminal motor, and facial motor nuclei are pontine structures and do not occupy either midbrain level.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CN IV is the only cranial nerve that exits the brainstem dorsally and decussates before exiting, tested alongside its caudal midbrain location. COMLEX: A caudal midbrain lesion sparing the oculomotor complex but affecting CN IV produces an isolated vertical diplopia pattern.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 8 — the cerebral aqueduct's rostrocaudal continuity, applied here to distinguish the two clinically testable midbrain cross-sectional levels the aqueduct traverses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q07","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the major external and internal regions of the midbrain: tectum, tegmentum, basis pedunculi, cerebral aqueduct, and cerebral peduncles.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man undergoes MRI for headache. A pineal region mass is noted, compressing an adjacent midbrain structure and narrowing a midline channel that connects the third and fourth ventricles. Which structure is being narrowed?","choices":{"A":"The interpeduncular fossa","B":"The quadrigeminal cistern","C":"The posterior commissure","D":"The cerebral aqueduct","E":"The medial longitudinal fasciculus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The cerebral aqueduct is the narrow ventricular channel traversing the midbrain that connects the third and fourth ventricles, and it lies just anterior to the tectal colliculi where a pineal mass exerts direct compression, producing noncommunicating hydrocephalus. The interpeduncular fossa is an anterior surface groove, not a ventricular channel. The quadrigeminal cistern is a subarachnoid space posterior to the colliculi, not a CSF conduction pathway between ventricles. The posterior commissure marks the midbrain-diencephalon boundary but is not a ventricular channel. The medial longitudinal fasciculus is a fiber tract, not a CSF space.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pineal tumors compressing the cerebral aqueduct classically cause obstructive hydrocephalus plus Parinaud syndrome from tectal compression, a high-yield combined vignette. COMLEX: Aqueductal stenosis is also a congenital cause of noncommunicating hydrocephalus in infants, tested separately from acquired pineal compression.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 8 — Ventricular system and cerebral aqueduct continuity","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q08","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the major external and internal regions of the midbrain: tectum, tegmentum, basis pedunculi, cerebral aqueduct, and cerebral peduncles.","batch":2,"stem":"A neuroanatomy student is reviewing a cross-section of the midbrain and is asked to identify the entire region below the tectum, encompassing both the tegmentum and the basis pedunculi together. Which term correctly describes this combined region?","choices":{"A":"The cerebral peduncle","B":"The crus cerebri","C":"The corpora quadrigemina","D":"The periaqueductal gray","E":"The interpeduncular fossa"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The cerebral peduncle, although sometimes used loosely as a synonym for the crus cerebri, technically refers to the entire midbrain below the tectum, comprising both the tegmentum and the basis pedunculi. The crus cerebri is only the anterior-most descending fiber bundle within the basis pedunculi, a narrower structure than the full cerebral peduncle. The corpora quadrigemina refers to the four tectal colliculi, a posterior term. The periaqueductal gray is the neuron sleeve around the aqueduct. The interpeduncular fossa is the external anterior groove between the two peduncles, not the peduncle itself.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Distinguishing crus cerebri from cerebral peduncle is a classic terminology trap on anatomy-heavy exams. COMLEX: Precise peduncle terminology matters when localizing which fiber systems a described lesion is most likely to spare or involve.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 8 — Ventricular system and cerebral aqueduct continuity","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The crus cerebri is only the a"}},{"id":"sdl19_q09","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the major external and internal regions of the midbrain: tectum, tegmentum, basis pedunculi, cerebral aqueduct, and cerebral peduncles.","batch":2,"stem":"During brainstem dissection, a fiber bundle is identified surrounding the cerebral aqueduct at all midbrain levels, composed of small neurons implicated in descending pain modulation and rich in opiate receptors. Which structure is this?","choices":{"A":"The reticular formation","B":"The dorsal raphe nucleus","C":"The periaqueductal gray","D":"The medial lemniscus","E":"The mesencephalic trigeminal tract"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The periaqueductal gray, or central gray, is a sleeve of small neurons surrounding the cerebral aqueduct at all midbrain levels, receives somatosensory input, interconnects with the hypothalamus and thalamus, and is central to descending analgesia given its high density of opiate receptors. The reticular formation is a more diffuse tegmental network, not specifically aqueduct-surrounding. The dorsal raphe nucleus lies adjacent to the periaqueductal gray on the midline but is a discrete serotonergic nucleus, not the surrounding gray matter sleeve itself. The medial lemniscus is an ascending sensory tract unrelated to pain modulation. The mesencephalic trigeminal tract lies along the lateral edge of the periaqueductal gray but carries proprioceptive fibers, not descending analgesic output.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Periaqueductal gray opioid receptor density explains why opioids and stimulation-produced analgesia both engage this structure, a mechanism question favorite. COMLEX: Descending PAG-to-raphe-to-dorsal horn analgesic pathways are relevant to chronic pain management discussions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 8 — Ventricular system and cerebral aqueduct continuity","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q10","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate caudal midbrain structures at the inferior colliculus level from rostral midbrain structures at the superior colliculus level.","batch":2,"stem":"An axial T2 MRI shows a midbrain cross-section with a goblet-shaped nucleus receiving anteriorly entering fiber bundles that carry auditory information, projecting onward via a brachium to the medial geniculate body. At which midbrain level was this image obtained?","choices":{"A":"The inferior colliculus level, caudal midbrain","B":"The superior colliculus level, rostral midbrain","C":"The midbrain-diencephalon junction","D":"The pons-midbrain junction, anterior surface","E":"The level of the posterior commissure"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The caudal midbrain at the inferior colliculus level is characterized by a large central nucleus receiving lateral lemniscus fibers anteriorly, producing a goblet-like appearance, and this auditory relay projects to the medial geniculate body via the brachium of the inferior colliculus. The rostral superior colliculus level instead handles visual-motor tracking, not audition. The midbrain-diencephalon junction is marked by the posterior commissure, unrelated to auditory relay. The pons-midbrain junction is an anterior surface landmark, not an internal auditory nucleus. The posterior commissure level again marks the rostral boundary, not the caudal auditory level.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Inferior colliculus equals auditory relay, superior colliculus equals visual-motor, a pairing tested repeatedly on imaging-based questions. First Aid mnemonic: eyes above ears, superior colliculus, visual, sits above inferior colliculus, auditory. COMLEX: Auditory brainstem response abnormalities localize partly to inferior colliculus dysfunction in central hearing pathway disorders.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 8 — Ventricular system and cerebral aqueduct continuity","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"The posterior commissure level"}},{"id":"sdl19_q11","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate caudal midbrain structures at the inferior colliculus level from rostral midbrain structures at the superior colliculus level.","batch":2,"stem":"A cross-section of the rostral midbrain demonstrates layered surface structures involved in visual-motor gaze tracking, with an underlying preganglionic parasympathetic complex embedded within an adjacent cranial nerve nucleus. Which paired structures are being described together?","choices":{"A":"The inferior colliculus and the trochlear nucleus complex","B":"The superior colliculus and the isolated trochlear nucleus","C":"The inferior colliculus and the Edinger-Westphal complex","D":"The tectal plate and the pontine abducens nucleus","E":"The superior colliculus and the Edinger-Westphal nucleus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"At the rostral, superior colliculus level, the layered superior colliculus mediates visual-motor gaze tracking, and the oculomotor complex at this same level includes the Edinger-Westphal nucleus, the preganglionic parasympathetic component controlling pupillary constriction and accommodation. The inferior colliculus and trochlear nucleus instead belong together at the caudal level. Pairing the superior colliculus with the trochlear nucleus or the inferior colliculus with Edinger-Westphal mismatches structures from opposite levels. The abducens nucleus is a pontine structure, not present at either midbrain level.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Edinger-Westphal nucleus damage produces a blown pupil, and its rostral midbrain location alongside the superior colliculus is tested in pupillary reflex questions. COMLEX: This same rostral level's tectal involvement underlies upgaze palsy in dorsal midbrain compression.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 8 — Ventricular system and cerebral aqueduct continuity","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Pairing the superior colliculu"}},{"id":"sdl19_q12","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate caudal midbrain structures at the inferior colliculus level from rostral midbrain structures at the superior colliculus level.","batch":2,"stem":"A comparative anatomy exercise asks students to contrast the internal cytoarchitecture of the two colliculi. Which statement correctly distinguishes the superior colliculus from the inferior colliculus in humans?","choices":{"A":"The inferior colliculus is layered, the superior colliculus is homogeneous","B":"The superior colliculus shows alternating cell and fiber layers, the inferior colliculus is homogeneous","C":"Both colliculi share an identical layered cytoarchitecture throughout adulthood","D":"Neither colliculus develops any layered cytoarchitecture at any point in life","E":"The superior colliculus is homogeneous, the inferior colliculus shows radial columns"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In humans, the superior colliculus consists of alternating layers of cells and fibers reflecting its role integrating visual and visual-motor information, whereas the inferior colliculus, the auditory relay, appears more histologically homogeneous. Reversing the pattern, claiming identical architecture, denying any layering, or inventing a radial-column description for the inferior colliculus all misstate the actual described organization.","boardPrep":"Step 1: This layering distinction rarely appears directly but underlies why the superior colliculus is often described as having laminar organization on histology-based questions. COMLEX: Superior colliculus lamination correlates with its multimodal sensory integration role in reflexive gaze orienting.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 8 — Ventricular system and cerebral aqueduct continuity","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Reversing the pattern, claimin","C":"Reversing the pattern, claimin","D":"Reversing the pattern, claimin"}},{"id":"sdl19_bloom_q01","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate caudal midbrain structures at the inferior colliculus level from rostral midbrain structures at the superior colliculus level.","batch":3,"stem":"A 44-year-old man presents with a 2-day history of vertical diplopia that worsens when looking down and to the right.\n- Compensatory head tilt to the right\n- Left eye hypertropic at rest, worsening on right gaze and on left head tilt\n- Pupils equal and reactive; no ptosis\n- No limb weakness, no ataxia, no facial or hearing deficit\nThis patient's condition is most likely caused by an injury to the midbrain at the ___","choices":{"A":"superior colliculus level","B":"pontomedullary junction","C":"rostral midbrain tegmentum","D":"inferior colliculus level","E":"cerebral aqueduct"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The trochlear nerve is the only cranial nerve to exit the dorsal brainstem, and its nucleus and decussating fibers are located at the caudal midbrain, inferior colliculus level. Vertical diplopia worse on downgaze in adduction and on tilting toward the affected side, relieved by tilting away, with an otherwise normal exam, is the classic trochlear nerve pattern localizing to this level. The superior colliculus level houses the oculomotor nucleus and red nucleus rather than the trochlear nucleus. The pontomedullary junction is where the abducens and facial nerves exit, not the trochlear nerve. The rostral midbrain tegmentum and cerebral aqueduct are both midline or rostral structures that do not correspond to the caudal trochlear nucleus location.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The trochlear nerve is unique among cranial nerves for exiting dorsally and decussating before exiting, and its nucleus localizes precisely to the inferior colliculus level. COMLEX: A head tilt away from the affected eye with worse diplopia on downgaze is a classic bedside sign of trochlear nerve palsy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 Bloom → Obj 4 — cranial nerve nucleus level localization used for CN VI in the pons, applied here to CN IV at the caudal midbrain","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl19_bloom_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate caudal midbrain structures at the inferior colliculus level from rostral midbrain structures at the superior colliculus level.","batch":3,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman presents with a 1-hour history of sudden severe headache followed by right eyelid droop and double vision.\n- Right pupil 7 mm and non-reactive to light\n- Right eye deviated down and out at rest\n- Left eye and left extraocular movements normal\n- Strength and sensation intact in all four limbs\nCT angiography shows an enlarging aneurysm at the junction of the internal carotid and posterior communicating arteries compressing the oculomotor nerve just after it leaves the midbrain.\nThe compressed nerve arises from a nucleus located in the midbrain at the ___","choices":{"A":"inferior colliculus level","B":"superior colliculus level","C":"pontomedullary junction","D":"cerebral aqueduct","E":"cervicomedullary junction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The oculomotor nerve nucleus is located at the rostral midbrain, superior colliculus level, and its fascicles exit ventrally through the interpeduncular fossa, precisely where an enlarging posterior communicating artery aneurysm classically compresses it, producing a fixed, dilated pupil with the eye deviated down and out. The inferior colliculus level houses the trochlear rather than oculomotor nucleus. The pontomedullary junction is where CN VI and VII exit, unrelated to this vessel or nerve. The cerebral aqueduct is a midline CSF channel rather than a nerve exit site, and the cervicomedullary junction is far too caudal to be compressed by a posterior communicating artery aneurysm.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A posterior communicating artery aneurysm compressing the oculomotor nerve at the superior colliculus level is a classic cause of a painful, pupil-involving third nerve palsy and a neurosurgical emergency. COMLEX: A blown, non-reactive pupil with severe headache should always prompt urgent evaluation for a compressive rather than ischemic third nerve palsy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 7 — cavernous sinus and CN III compressive pathology principles extended here to the interpeduncular fossa exit of the oculomotor nerve","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl19_bloom_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the major external and internal regions of the midbrain: tectum, tegmentum, basis pedunculi, cerebral aqueduct, and cerebral peduncles.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old man presents with a 3-hour history of sudden-onset numbness affecting the left face, arm, and leg.\n- Decreased pinprick and temperature sensation over the left face, arm, and leg\n- Decreased vibration and joint position sense over the same distribution\n- Strength full and symmetric bilaterally\n- No diplopia, no ptosis, no ataxia\nMRI shows a small right dorsolateral midbrain infarct sparing the ventral surface.\nThe brain structure most likely affected is the ___","choices":{"A":"basis pedunculi","B":"tectum","C":"cerebral aqueduct","D":"substantia nigra","E":"tegmentum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Combined contralateral loss of pain, temperature, vibration, and joint position sense without any motor deficit indicates that both the spinothalamic tract and the medial lemniscus, which converge in the midbrain tegmentum, are affected while the ventrally located motor fibers are spared. The basis pedunculi carries the corticospinal and corticobulbar fibers and its involvement would instead produce contralateral weakness, which is absent here. The tectum, the dorsal midbrain roof formed by the colliculi, governs eye movements and reflexes rather than somatosensory relay. The cerebral aqueduct is a CSF channel with no sensory function of its own. The substantia nigra is a component of the basal ganglia circuit governing movement, not sensation, and its damage would not produce a sensory syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A pure contralateral hemisensory syndrome with no weakness localizes to the midbrain tegmentum, sparing the ventral basis pedunculi, since both major somatosensory tracts converge there. COMLEX: The presence of both pain-temperature and vibration-position loss together on the same side is the clue that two separate tracts, not just one, are affected in the tegmentum.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 → Obj 4 — tegmentum vs. basis pedunculi localization extended here to the named midbrain vascular syndromes that build on this same anatomic division","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl19_bloom_q04","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the major external and internal regions of the midbrain: tectum, tegmentum, basis pedunculi, cerebral aqueduct, and cerebral peduncles.","batch":3,"stem":"On a sagittal MRI, a radiologist identifies the subarachnoid space immediately posterior to the superior and inferior colliculi. It contains the exiting trochlear nerves, the great cerebral vein of Galen, and distal branches of the posterior cerebral arteries. This space is the ___","choices":{"A":"interpeduncular cistern","B":"quadrigeminal cistern","C":"crural cistern","D":"cisterna magna","E":"prepontine cistern"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The subarachnoid space immediately posterior to the colliculi is the quadrigeminal cistern, which contains the exiting trochlear nerves, the great vein of Galen, and distal branches of the posterior cerebral arteries. The interpeduncular cistern occupies the interpeduncular fossa between the crura on the anterior midbrain, where the oculomotor nerves exit. The crural cistern lies between the crus cerebri and the parahippocampal gyrus and contains the anterior and medial posterior choroidal arteries and the basal vein. The cisterna magna lies below the cerebellum near the foramen of Magendie, and the prepontine cistern lies anterior to the pons around the basilar artery.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Midbrain cisterns: quadrigeminal (posterior, trochlear nerve, vein of Galen), ambient (lateral, P2-P3, SCA), crural (crus and parahippocampal gyrus), interpeduncular (anterior, oculomotor nerves). COMLEX: Pineal-region masses grow into the quadrigeminal cistern and compress the tectum.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 Bloom → Obj 1 — pontine landmark identification on imaging, applied here one level rostrally to the cistern behind the midbrain tectum","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl19_bloom_q05","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the major external and internal regions of the midbrain: tectum, tegmentum, basis pedunculi, cerebral aqueduct, and cerebral peduncles.","batch":3,"stem":"The crus cerebri is organized into thirds. Corticospinal and corticonuclear fibers occupy the middle third, and parietopontine, occipitopontine, and temporopontine fibers occupy the lateral third. The fibers in the medial third of the crus cerebri are the ___","choices":{"A":"corticospinal fibers to the leg","B":"corticonuclear fibers to the face","C":"rubrospinal fibers from the red nucleus","D":"frontopontine fibers from the frontal lobe","E":"temporopontine fibers from the temporal lobe"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Corticopontine fibers in the medial third of the crus cerebri arise from the frontal lobe as frontopontine fibers, whereas those in the lateral third arise from the parietal, occipital, and temporal lobes. The middle third carries corticospinal and corticonuclear fibers, with lower extremity fibers placed laterally, face fibers medially, and upper extremity fibers in between, so neither leg corticospinal nor face corticonuclear fibers occupy the medial third. Rubrospinal fibers arise in the tegmentum and cross in the anterior tegmental decussation; they do not travel in the crus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crus cerebri: medial third frontopontine, middle third corticospinal and corticonuclear (face medial, arm intermediate, leg lateral), lateral third parieto-, occipito-, and temporopontine. COMLEX: Corticopontine fibers relay cortical input to the pontine nuclei and then to the contralateral cerebellum.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 Bloom → Obj 3 — the corticospinal tract's course through the basilar pons, traced here one level rostrally through the thirds of the crus cerebri","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl19_bloom_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate caudal midbrain structures at the inferior colliculus level from rostral midbrain structures at the superior colliculus level.","batch":3,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman has sudden-onset right-sided coarse tremor, ataxia, and mild weakness.\n- Pupils equal and reactive; no ptosis\n- No diplopia\nMRI shows a small infarct centered on the red nucleus.\nAt which midbrain level does this lesion localize?","choices":{"A":"superior colliculus level","B":"inferior colliculus level","C":"pontomedullary junction","D":"cerebral aqueduct level","E":"cervicomedullary junction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The red nucleus is a rostral midbrain tegmental structure located at the superior colliculus level, alongside the oculomotor nucleus, so an infarct centered on it localizes precisely to this rostral level even though the pupillary and oculomotor exam happen to be normal here. The inferior colliculus level instead houses the trochlear nucleus rather than the red nucleus, the pontomedullary junction is where CN VI and VII exit and is unrelated to midbrain structures, the cerebral aqueduct is a midline CSF channel rather than a level-defining structure on its own, and the cervicomedullary junction is far too caudal to contain the red nucleus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The red nucleus localizes to the superior colliculus, rostral midbrain, level alongside the oculomotor nucleus, even when a lesion spares CN III itself, as in an isolated Claude syndrome variant. COMLEX: Normal pupils and extraocular movements do not rule out a rostral midbrain lesion if the red nucleus alone, rather than the adjacent oculomotor fascicles, is affected.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — landmark-based level localization used for the medulla, applied here to the caudal vs. rostral midbrain using the red nucleus as the localizing structure.","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"}],"20":[{"id":"sdl20_q01","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Localize lesions involving CN III, CN IV, corticospinal fibers, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic tract, red nucleus, substantia nigra, and pretectal area.","batch":1,"stem":"A lesion confined to the middle third of the crus cerebri, sparing the tegmentum entirely, produces which deficit?","choices":{"A":"Pure contralateral spastic hemiplegia without sensory loss","B":"Ipsilateral facial numbness with jaw weakness","C":"Contralateral loss of vibration and joint sense","D":"Ipsilateral limb ataxia with intention tremor","E":"Bilateral loss of pain and temperature sensation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The crus cerebri carries descending corticospinal fibers in its middle third, and an isolated lesion here damages only that tract, producing pure contralateral spastic hemiplegia without sensory or cranial nerve findings because the tegmentum is spared. Facial numbness with jaw weakness reflects trigeminal pathway damage, not present in the crus. Loss of vibration and joint sense reflects medial lemniscus injury, a tegmental structure. Ipsilateral ataxia with tremor reflects red nucleus or cerebellar peduncle damage, also tegmental. Bilateral pain and temperature loss does not localize to a unilateral crus cerebri lesion at all.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A pure motor hemiplegia with no sensory or cranial nerve findings is the classic clue that a lacunar-type lesion is confined to the crus cerebri or internal capsule. COMLEX: This pure motor pattern is a favorite distractor against Weber syndrome, which requires an added ipsilateral CN III finding to qualify.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 2 — the caudal inferior-colliculus versus rostral superior-colliculus level distinction, applied here to pinpoint which named tract or nucleus a given lesion has damaged","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q02","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Localize lesions involving CN III, CN IV, corticospinal fibers, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic tract, red nucleus, substantia nigra, and pretectal area.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient develops contralateral loss of vibration and proprioception with fully preserved pain and temperature sensation after a small paramedian midbrain infarct. Which structure was selectively damaged?","choices":{"A":"The spinothalamic tract","B":"The corticospinal tract","C":"The medial lemniscus","D":"The red nucleus","E":"The substantia nigra"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The medial lemniscus occupies a paramedian position in the midbrain tegmentum, so a small paramedian infarct can selectively damage it while sparing the more laterally placed spinothalamic tract, producing isolated contralateral loss of discriminative touch, vibration, and proprioception with intact pain and temperature sensation. The spinothalamic tract sits laterally and would instead cause pain and temperature loss if damaged. Corticospinal fibers in the crus cerebri would cause weakness, not a sensory dissociation. The red nucleus produces ataxia and tremor, not a sensory finding. The substantia nigra produces parkinsonian signs, again not a sensory finding.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Selective dorsal column sensory loss with preserved pain and temperature is a paramedian, medial-lemniscus-predominant lesion pattern, distinct from the lateral spinothalamic dissociation seen in syringomyelia. COMLEX: This pure sensory dissociation pattern is tested against Brown-Sequard and central cord syndrome distractors at the spinal cord level.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 2 — the caudal inferior-colliculus versus rostral superior-colliculus level distinction, applied here to pinpoint which named tract or nucleus a given lesion has damaged","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Localize lesions involving CN III, CN IV, corticospinal fibers, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic tract, red nucleus, substantia nigra, and pretectal area.","batch":1,"stem":"Damage to a large, prominent, circular tegmental nucleus at the rostral midbrain level produces contralateral ataxia, incoordination, and a coarse resting-and-postural tremor. Which structure was damaged?","choices":{"A":"The substantia nigra nucleus","B":"The red nucleus structure","C":"The pretectal area nuclei","D":"The oculomotor nucleus complex","E":"The medial lemniscus tract"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The red nucleus, a prominent circular structure in the rostral tegmentum, receives cerebellothalamic and rubroolivary connections, and its damage produces contralateral ataxia, incoordination, and a rubral or Holmes tremor. The substantia nigra instead produces contralateral rigidity and bradykinesia from dopaminergic loss, a different movement pattern. The pretectal area governs the pupillary light reflex and vertical gaze, not limb coordination. The oculomotor nucleus complex governs eye movements and pupil constriction, not limb tremor. The medial lemniscus governs discriminative sensation, not coordination.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Rubral tremor is a coarse, low-frequency tremor present at rest, posture, and action, distinguishing red nucleus damage from the resting-only tremor of substantia nigra dysfunction. COMLEX: Cerebellothalamic fiber involvement alongside the red nucleus explains why this ataxia is contralateral rather than ipsilateral, unlike direct cerebellar hemisphere lesions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 2 — the caudal inferior-colliculus versus rostral superior-colliculus level distinction, applied here to pinpoint which named tract or nucleus a given lesion has damaged","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the clinical findings in Weber syndrome, Benedikt syndrome, Claude syndrome, Parinaud syndrome, and compressive third nerve palsy.","batch":1,"stem":"In Weber syndrome, a medial midbrain lesion damages the exiting oculomotor root and the crus cerebri, causing an ipsilateral oculomotor palsy with contralateral limb weakness. Which additional findings result from involvement of corticonuclear fibers in the crus?","choices":{"A":"Ipsilateral weakness of the entire face, including the forehead","B":"Ipsilateral tongue deviation and ipsilateral palatal weakness","C":"Contralateral loss of facial pain and temperature sensation","D":"Bilateral weakness of jaw closure and chewing","E":"Contralateral lower face weakness and tongue deviation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Occlusion of vessels serving the medial midbrain produces the Weber syndrome: ipsilateral paralysis of all extraocular muscles except the lateral rectus and superior oblique with a dilated pupil (oculomotor root), contralateral limb paralysis (corticospinal fibers in the crus), and, from corticonuclear fibers in the crus, weakness of the lower half of the face and deviation of the protruded tongue, both on the side contralateral to the lesion. Whole-face weakness including the forehead is a lower motor neuron facial palsy, not a corticonuclear sign. Ipsilateral tongue and palate weakness would require a lower motor neuron lesion of XII or the nucleus ambiguus. Facial pain and temperature travel in trigeminothalamic fibers, not the crus, and the trigeminal motor nucleus receives bilateral corticonuclear input, so jaw strength is preserved.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crus cerebri lesions add contralateral central facial weakness (forehead spared) and contralateral tongue deviation to the hemiparesis. COMLEX: Uncal herniation can produce the same Weber-like picture by compressing the third nerve and crus.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — UMN facial and hypoglossal patterns from corticobulbar lesions, applied here to the corticonuclear fibers of the crus cerebri","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Ipsilateral tongue and palate "}},{"id":"sdl20_q05","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the clinical findings in Weber syndrome, Benedikt syndrome, Claude syndrome, Parinaud syndrome, and compressive third nerve palsy.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient presents with an ipsilateral dilated pupil and eye held down and out, plus contralateral tremor and ataxia, but no limb weakness on examination. Which named midbrain syndrome best fits this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Claude syndrome pattern","B":"Weber syndrome pattern","C":"Benedikt syndrome pattern","D":"Compressive CN III palsy","E":"Central herniation pattern"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Claude syndrome arises from a central tegmental lesion damaging the oculomotor fascicles together with the red nucleus and cerebellothalamic fibers, producing ipsilateral CN III palsy plus contralateral ataxia and tremor, while the crus cerebri and its corticospinal fibers are spared, so no hemiplegia occurs. Weber syndrome instead requires crus cerebri involvement and produces hemiplegia without tremor. Benedikt syndrome combines both the Weber and Claude territories, so it would also include hemiplegia. Compressive CN III palsy alone would not cause a contralateral limb movement disorder. Central herniation produces bilateral, progressively worsening findings rather than this isolated unilateral pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Claude syndrome is the classic example of a CN III palsy paired with contralateral cerebellar-type signs and no weakness, distinguishing it from Weber and Benedikt. COMLEX: Remembering Claude spares the crus cerebri, so its motor exam remains strength-normal, is a frequently tested discriminator.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 Obj 3 — cranial nerve exam findings used for lesion localization, applied here to the specific CN III palsy patterns that define each named midbrain syndrome","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q06","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the clinical findings in Weber syndrome, Benedikt syndrome, Claude syndrome, Parinaud syndrome, and compressive third nerve palsy.","batch":1,"stem":"A large midbrain lesion spans both the crus cerebri and the central tegmentum, producing ipsilateral CN III palsy, contralateral hemiplegia, and contralateral tremor with ataxia. Which named syndrome is this?","choices":{"A":"Weber syndrome","B":"Claude syndrome","C":"Parinaud syndrome","D":"Benedikt syndrome","E":"Uncal herniation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Benedikt syndrome results from a large lesion spanning both the crus cerebri and the central tegmentum, combining the territories of Weber and Claude syndromes, so patients show ipsilateral CN III palsy together with both contralateral hemiplegia from crus involvement and contralateral tremor and ataxia from red nucleus involvement. Weber syndrome alone lacks the tremor and ataxia because the tegmentum is spared. Claude syndrome alone lacks the hemiplegia because the crus is spared. Parinaud syndrome instead reflects dorsal tectal compression with an upward gaze deficit, not this ventral-plus-tegmental combination. Uncal herniation can mimic parts of this picture but progresses with declining consciousness rather than presenting as a fixed focal syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Benedikt syndrome is essentially Weber plus Claude combined, a useful memory shortcut for boards. COMLEX: In all three syndromes, the pupil is dilated because parasympathetic Edinger-Westphal fibers are damaged while sympathetic dilator input remains intact.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 Obj 3 — cranial nerve exam findings used for lesion localization, applied here to the specific CN III palsy patterns that define each named midbrain syndrome","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Localize lesions involving CN III, CN IV, corticospinal fibers, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic tract, red nucleus, substantia nigra, and pretectal area.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman is found to have a small lesion at the midbrain-diencephalon junction, lateral to the posterior commissure. She reports normal visual acuity but has an impaired pupillary light reflex bilaterally, with pupils that still constrict on accommodation. Which structure is most likely damaged?","choices":{"A":"The medial geniculate nucleus","B":"The lateral geniculate nucleus","C":"The inferior colliculus","D":"The pretectal area nuclei","E":"The mesencephalic trigeminal nucleus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The pretectal nuclei sit at the midbrain-diencephalon junction lateral to the posterior commissure and form the relay center for the pupillary light reflex, receiving retinal input and projecting bilaterally to the Edinger-Westphal nuclei, so a lesion here produces light-near dissociation with preserved constriction on accommodation but impaired light response. The medial geniculate nucleus relays auditory, not pupillary, information. The lateral geniculate nucleus relays visual information for conscious sight, which remains intact here. The inferior colliculus is an auditory relay, unrelated to the pupillary reflex. The mesencephalic trigeminal nucleus carries jaw proprioception, not pupillary function.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Light-near dissociation localizes to the pretectal area and is classically tested with neurosyphilis Argyll Robertson pupils and with dorsal midbrain compression from pineal masses. COMLEX: Preserved accommodation with impaired light reflex is a high-yield exam finding that separates pretectal lesions from a full CN III palsy, which would impair both reflexes equally.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 6 — Meningeal anatomy applied to herniation syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Localize lesions involving CN III, CN IV, corticospinal fibers, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic tract, red nucleus, substantia nigra, and pretectal area.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old man develops vertical diplopia that worsens when he looks down and to the right, along with a head tilt to the right. Imaging reveals a small lesion at the dorsal midbrain-pons junction affecting the decussated fibers of a cranial nerve just after they cross the midline. Which nerve is affected, and on which side is the deficit?","choices":{"A":"The oculomotor nerve, ipsilateral to the lesion","B":"The trochlear nerve, ipsilateral to the lesion","C":"The abducens nerve, bilaterally","D":"The oculomotor nerve, contralateral to the lesion","E":"The trochlear nerve, contralateral to the lesion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The trochlear nerve is unique among cranial nerves in that its fibers decussate within the superior medullary velum before exiting the brainstem dorsally, so a lesion of the already-crossed fibers damages the axons supplying the superior oblique on the same side, producing an ipsilateral palsy; only a lesion of the nucleus or of fibers before they cross produces a contralateral palsy. The oculomotor nerve fascicles do not decussate, so a fascicular lesion gives an ipsilateral CN III palsy with ptosis, a dilated pupil, and a down-and-out eye rather than isolated vertical diplopia on downgaze. The abducens nerve is a pontine nerve that does not decussate, is not at this dorsal midbrain–pons level, and causes horizontal rather than vertical diplopia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CN IV is the only cranial nerve that exits the brainstem dorsally and crosses the midline before exiting, so nuclear lesions are contralateral and lesions of the crossed fibers or nerve are ipsilateral, a classic laterality trap. COMLEX: Superior oblique palsy classically produces diplopia worse on downgaze in adduction, with head tilt away from the affected eye as a compensatory maneuver.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 6 — Meningeal anatomy applied to herniation syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Localize lesions involving CN III, CN IV, corticospinal fibers, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic tract, red nucleus, substantia nigra, and pretectal area.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with long-standing hypertension develops new-onset right-sided rigidity and bradykinesia over several weeks, without tremor at rest. MRI reveals a small chronic infarct affecting a pigmented nucleus in the left basis pedunculi. Which structure explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"The substantia nigra","B":"The red nucleus","C":"The pretectal area","D":"The medial lemniscus","E":"The corticospinal tract"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The substantia nigra pars compacta is the heavily pigmented dopaminergic nucleus forming part of the basis pedunculi, and its focal ischemic damage can produce contralateral parkinsonian signs such as rigidity and bradykinesia, sometimes without the classic resting tremor. The red nucleus, when damaged, produces ataxia and a coarse tremor rather than rigidity and bradykinesia. The pretectal area governs pupillary and vertical gaze reflexes, not limb movement quality. The medial lemniscus governs discriminative sensation, and its damage would not produce rigidity. The corticospinal tract, if damaged, produces spastic weakness with upper motor neuron signs, a different clinical picture than parkinsonism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vascular parkinsonism from a focal nigral or basal ganglia infarct is a recognized, testable mimic of idiopathic Parkinson disease, often lacking the classic resting tremor. COMLEX: Localizing the substantia nigra to the basis pedunculi, distinct from the tegmental red nucleus, is a frequently confused anatomic pairing on written exams.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 6 — Meningeal anatomy applied to herniation syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the clinical findings in Weber syndrome, Benedikt syndrome, Claude syndrome, Parinaud syndrome, and compressive third nerve palsy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 14-year-old boy presents with headache and difficulty looking upward. Examination shows bilateral pupils that react poorly to light but constrict normally with near effort, along with convergence-retraction nystagmus on attempted upgaze. MRI reveals a mass in the pineal region compressing the adjacent midbrain roof. Which structure is most directly compressed to produce this presentation?","choices":{"A":"The crus cerebri bilaterally","B":"The tectum and pretectal area","C":"The bilateral red nuclei","D":"The oculomotor nerve fascicles","E":"The bilateral substantia nigra"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Parinaud syndrome results from dorsal midbrain compression of the tectum and adjacent pretectal area, typically by a pineal region mass, producing upward gaze palsy, light-near dissociation of the pupils, and convergence-retraction nystagmus, often with noncommunicating hydrocephalus from concurrent cerebral aqueduct obstruction. Bilateral crus cerebri compression would instead produce bilateral spastic weakness, not an isolated gaze and pupil syndrome. Bilateral red nucleus compression would produce bilateral ataxia and tremor, not upgaze palsy. Direct oculomotor fascicle compression would produce a full ipsilateral CN III palsy rather than the bilateral supranuclear vertical gaze deficit described here. Bilateral substantia nigra compression would produce parkinsonian features, not this presentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Parinaud syndrome from a pineal region tumor, classically a germinoma or pinealoma in this age group, is a textbook pairing of upward gaze palsy, light-near dissociation, and obstructive hydrocephalus. COMLEX: Pineal germ cell tumors can secrete β-hCG, producing precocious puberty in boys, an integrative clue sometimes layered onto this vignette type.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — Posterior cerebral artery and basilar territory deficits","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the clinical findings in Weber syndrome, Benedikt syndrome, Claude syndrome, Parinaud syndrome, and compressive third nerve palsy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman develops a sudden severe headache followed by a fixed, dilated right pupil and ptosis, with the right eye deviated down and out. Extraocular movements on the left are normal, and there is no limb weakness. Angiography reveals an enlarging aneurysm at the junction of the internal carotid and posterior communicating arteries. Which feature of this presentation is explained by the aneurysm's compressive rather than ischemic mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Early pupil dilation from compressed superficial fibers","B":"Late pupil involvement from central fiber ischemia","C":"Contralateral hemiplegia from crus cerebri infarction","D":"Bilateral ptosis from sympathetic chain disruption","E":"Spared pupil with full ophthalmoplegia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The parasympathetic fibers destined for the Edinger-Westphal nucleus travel along the superficial perimeter of the oculomotor nerve, so an external compressive lesion such as a posterior communicating artery aneurysm affects these superficial fibers first, producing early pupillary dilation alongside the developing ophthalmoplegia. In contrast, ischemic microvascular CN III palsy, as seen in diabetes, damages the centrally located motor fascicles preferentially while sparing the superficial pupillary fibers, classically producing a pupil-sparing palsy. Contralateral hemiplegia would require crus cerebri involvement, not described in this isolated CN III presentation. Bilateral ptosis from sympathetic disruption describes Horner syndrome, an unrelated pattern. Preserved pupillary constriction with full ophthalmoplegia is the opposite of what a compressive lesion produces.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pupil-involving CN III palsy from a compressive aneurysm versus pupil-sparing CN III palsy from diabetic ischemia is one of the highest-yield distinctions tested in cranial nerve anatomy. COMLEX: Any new pupil-involving CN III palsy warrants urgent vascular imaging to rule out a ruptured or expanding aneurysm before assuming a benign microvascular cause.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — Posterior cerebral artery and basilar territory deficits","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"In contrast, ischemic microvas"}},{"id":"sdl20_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the clinical findings in Weber syndrome, Benedikt syndrome, Claude syndrome, Parinaud syndrome, and compressive third nerve palsy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 33-year-old man is struck in the head during an assault and develops a rapidly expanding epidural hematoma over the right temporal lobe. Over the next hour he becomes progressively less responsive. On examination his right pupil is dilated and unreactive, and his left arm and leg show new weakness. Which process best explains this evolving presentation?","choices":{"A":"Central herniation with bilateral pupil involvement","B":"Upward cerebellar herniation obstructing the aqueduct","C":"Uncal herniation compressing the third nerve and crus cerebri","D":"An ischemic stroke in the posterior cerebral territory","E":"A primary lesion of the contralateral pretectal area"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Uncal herniation occurs when an expanding supratentorial mass, often a temporal lobe hematoma, forces the uncus over the tentorial edge against the midbrain, first compressing the ipsilateral oculomotor nerve to produce a dilated pupil, then compressing the crus cerebri to produce contralateral hemiparesis as herniation progresses, closely mimicking features of Weber syndrome. Central transtentorial herniation instead follows diffuse downward displacement of the diencephalon and typically produces bilateral, not unilateral, early pupillary change. Upward cerebellar herniation arises from a posterior fossa mass pushing upward through the tentorial notch, not from a temporal lobe lesion. An isolated posterior cerebral artery stroke would not explain the rapidly progressive, mass-effect-driven decline described here. A primary contralateral pretectal lesion would not produce an ipsilateral dilated pupil or contralateral hemiparesis in this pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ipsilateral blown pupil followed by contralateral hemiparesis after a rapidly expanding supratentorial mass is the classic uncal herniation sequence, a can't-miss neurosurgical emergency. COMLEX: In severe cases the Kernohan phenomenon can push the opposite crus cerebri against the tentorial edge, producing hemiparesis on the same side as the dilated pupil and falsely suggesting the wrong side of injury.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — Posterior cerebral artery and basilar territory deficits","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_bloom_q01","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the clinical findings in Weber syndrome, Benedikt syndrome, Claude syndrome, Parinaud syndrome, and compressive third nerve palsy.","batch":3,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with hypertension presents with a 1-hour history of sudden-onset left eyelid droop and double vision plus right-sided weakness.\n- Left pupil dilated and poorly reactive to light\n- Left eye deviated down and out at rest\n- Right lower facial droop, sparing the forehead\n- Right arm and leg strength 2/5\n- No tremor, no ataxia; sensation intact bilaterally\nWhich named syndrome best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Benedikt syndrome","B":"Claude syndrome","C":"Weber syndrome","D":"Parinaud syndrome","E":"Millard-Gubler syndrome"},"correct":"C","explanation":"An ipsilateral third nerve palsy, ptosis with a down-and-out, dilated pupil, combined with a purely contralateral hemiparesis of the face, arm, and leg and no tremor or ataxia, localizes to a lesion involving the exiting CN III fascicles together with the crus cerebri, sparing the tegmentum entirely. This is Weber syndrome. Benedikt syndrome adds red nucleus involvement, producing contralateral tremor and ataxia, which are absent here. Claude syndrome is a purely tegmental lesion, CN III plus red nucleus, and produces contralateral tremor and ataxia without any weakness, which does not fit this purely motor picture. Parinaud syndrome produces upgaze palsy and light-near dissociation from dorsal tectal compression, not a crossed motor syndrome. Millard-Gubler syndrome is a pontine, not midbrain, syndrome combining ipsilateral CN VI and VII palsy with contralateral hemiparesis, which does not match this patient's isolated CN III findings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Weber syndrome, ipsilateral CN III palsy plus contralateral pure motor hemiparesis with no tremor or ataxia, is the classic crus cerebri infarct and a frequently tested localization. COMLEX: The absence of tremor or ataxia is the key discriminator that rules out both Benedikt and Claude syndromes in a crossed CN III presentation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 Bloom → Obj 1 — basis pedunculi, crus cerebri, involvement producing pure motor findings, contrasted here against the tegmental-only syndromes","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl20_bloom_q02","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the clinical findings in Weber syndrome, Benedikt syndrome, Claude syndrome, Parinaud syndrome, and compressive third nerve palsy.","batch":3,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman presents with a 1-hour history of sudden-onset left eyelid droop and double vision plus new right-sided tremor and incoordination.\n- Left pupil dilated and poorly reactive to light\n- Left eye deviated down and out at rest\n- Right-sided coarse tremor, worse with movement, and right dysmetria on finger-to-nose testing\n- Mild right arm and leg drift, but antigravity strength preserved at 4+/5\nWhich named syndrome best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Benedikt syndrome","B":"Weber syndrome","C":"Claude syndrome","D":"Parinaud syndrome","E":"Millard-Gubler syndrome"},"correct":"A","explanation":"An ipsilateral CN III palsy combined with contralateral tremor and dysmetria, from red nucleus involvement, together with only mild contralateral weakness from partial crus cerebri extension, is the classic pattern of Benedikt syndrome, a tegmental lesion that also encroaches slightly on the adjacent crus cerebri. Weber syndrome is a purely motor, crus cerebri only, syndrome with no tremor or ataxia, which does not fit the prominent movement disorder seen here. Claude syndrome is confined entirely to the tegmentum, red nucleus and superior cerebellar peduncle decussation, and produces tremor and ataxia with no weakness at all, which does not account for this patient's mild drift and reduced antigravity strength. Parinaud syndrome produces an upgaze and pupillary light-near dissociation pattern rather than a crossed motor and cerebellar syndrome. Millard-Gubler syndrome is a pontine syndrome involving CN VI and VII, not CN III, and does not fit this patient's findings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Benedikt syndrome sits between Weber, pure motor, and Claude, pure tegmental, and is recognized by CN III palsy plus contralateral tremor and ataxia with only mild weakness. COMLEX: The presence of even mild contralateral weakness alongside a movement disorder is the clue that distinguishes Benedikt from the purely tegmental Claude syndrome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 Bloom → Obj 1 — the red nucleus and tegmentum described anatomically, applied here to the tremor and ataxia component of Benedikt syndrome","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl20_bloom_q03","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the clinical findings in Weber syndrome, Benedikt syndrome, Claude syndrome, Parinaud syndrome, and compressive third nerve palsy.","batch":3,"stem":"A 63-year-old man presents with a 2-hour history of sudden-onset right eyelid droop and double vision along with new left-sided incoordination.\n- Right pupil dilated and non-reactive to light\n- Right eye deviated down and out at rest\n- Left dysmetria and intention tremor on finger-to-nose testing, left heel-to-shin ataxia\n- Strength full and symmetric, 5/5, in all four limbs\nWhich named syndrome best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Weber syndrome","B":"Benedikt syndrome","C":"Parinaud syndrome","D":"Claude syndrome","E":"Millard-Gubler syndrome"},"correct":"D","explanation":"An ipsilateral CN III palsy with contralateral ataxia and intention tremor from red nucleus and superior cerebellar peduncle decussation involvement, but with completely normal strength, indicates a lesion confined to the tegmentum and sparing the crus cerebri entirely. This is Claude syndrome. Weber syndrome instead requires crus cerebri involvement and produces contralateral weakness rather than ataxia, which is absent here. Benedikt syndrome shares the tremor and ataxia of Claude syndrome but also includes at least mild contralateral weakness from partial crus cerebri extension, which this patient does not have. Parinaud syndrome produces an upgaze palsy and light-near pupillary dissociation from dorsal tectal compression rather than a crossed CN III and cerebellar syndrome. Millard-Gubler syndrome is a pontine syndrome involving CN VI and VII rather than CN III and does not match this presentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Claude syndrome, CN III palsy with contralateral ataxia and tremor but entirely normal strength, is the purely tegmental midbrain syndrome and is distinguished from Benedikt by the complete absence of weakness. COMLEX: Fully preserved strength in the presence of contralateral ataxia is the single best clue separating Claude from Benedikt syndrome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 Bloom → Obj 1 — the tegmentum-only sensory syndrome described for a nonvascular vignette, applied here to the tegmentum-only motor-sparing Claude syndrome","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)","whyNot":{"B":"Benedikt syndrome shares the t"}},{"id":"sdl20_bloom_q04","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Localize lesions involving CN III, CN IV, corticospinal fibers, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic tract, red nucleus, substantia nigra, and pretectal area.","batch":3,"stem":"A 71-year-old man has an infarct involving the nuclei of the superior and inferior colliculi, much of the periaqueductal gray, and the brachium of the inferior colliculus, with the anterior midbrain spared. The posterior midbrain territory affected is supplied primarily by the ___","choices":{"A":"anterior choroidal artery","B":"posterior communicating artery","C":"superior cerebellar artery","D":"medial posterior choroidal artery","E":"quadrigeminal artery"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The posterior midbrain is served primarily by the quadrigeminal, or collicular, artery, which typically arises from the P1 segment of the posterior cerebral artery; its branches supply much of the periaqueductal gray, the nuclei of the superior and inferior colliculi, the anterolateral system, and the brachium of the inferior colliculus. The anterior choroidal and medial posterior choroidal arteries help supply ventrolateral regions, including the lateral crus, lateral substantia nigra, and medial lemniscus. The posterior communicating artery contributes paramedian branches to medial structures such as the oculomotor nuclei and red nucleus. Medial branches of the superior cerebellar artery add supply around the trochlear exit and inferior colliculus but are not the primary posterior supply.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Midbrain supply: paramedian (posteromedial) branches of P1 and PComm medially, quadrigeminal and choroidal arteries ventrolaterally, quadrigeminal artery posteriorly. COMLEX: Rostral basilar and P1 disease can therefore produce combined tectal, tegmental, and thalamic signs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 Bloom → Obj 1 — the midbrain tectum as a structural region, applied here to the artery that supplies it","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl20_bloom_q05","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Localize lesions involving CN III, CN IV, corticospinal fibers, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic tract, red nucleus, substantia nigra, and pretectal area.","batch":3,"stem":"A medial and dorsal extension of the dopaminergic substantia nigra pars compacta sends a ventral stream of mesostriatal, mesocortical, and mesolimbic fibers to the striatum, frontal cortex, limbic cortex, hypothalamus, hippocampus, and amygdala, and plays a major role in reward. This cell group is the ___","choices":{"A":"pars reticulata","B":"ventral tegmental area","C":"interpeduncular nucleus","D":"periaqueductal gray","E":"posterior raphe nucleus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A medial and dorsal extension of the dopaminergic neurons of the substantia nigra pars compacta is called the ventral tegmental area; its dopamine neurons contribute heavily to mesostriatal, mesocortical, and mesolimbic fibers to the striatum, frontal and limbic cortex, hypothalamus, hippocampus, and amygdala, and play a major role in positive reinforcement and reward. The pars reticulata projects to the superior colliculus, thalamus, and midbrain reticular formation rather than releasing dopamine broadly. The interpeduncular nucleus lies between the crura. The periaqueductal gray is involved in pain modulation, and the posterior raphe nucleus is serotonergic.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Dopamine pathways: nigrostriatal (pars compacta, movement), mesolimbic and mesocortical (ventral tegmental area, reward and cognition), tuberoinfundibular (prolactin). COMLEX: Antipsychotic benefit comes from mesolimbic D2 blockade, while nigrostriatal blockade causes parkinsonism.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 Bloom → Obj 1 — midbrain tegmentum and basis pedunculi organization, applied here to the dopaminergic cell group medial to the substantia nigra","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl20_bloom_q06","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Localize lesions involving CN III, CN IV, corticospinal fibers, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic tract, red nucleus, substantia nigra, and pretectal area.","batch":3,"stem":"The red nucleus has a caudal large-celled region and a rostral small-celled region. One efferent projection travels mainly to the contralateral cervical spinal cord; the other descends in the central tegmental tract to the ipsilateral inferior olive. The rubrospinal fibers arise from the ___","choices":{"A":"rostral parvocellular region","B":"pars compacta of the nigra","C":"interstitial nucleus of Cajal","D":"nucleus of Darkschewitsch","E":"caudal magnocellular region"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The red nucleus is composed of caudal magnocellular and rostral parvocellular regions. Rubrospinal fibers arise from the magnocellular portion, cross in the anterior tegmental decussation, and travel primarily to the contralateral cervical spinal cord; rubroolivary fibers arise from the parvocellular portion and descend in the central tegmental tract to the ipsilateral inferior olive. Afferents to the red nucleus come mainly from the contralateral cerebellar nuclei and the ipsilateral cerebral cortex. The pars compacta is the dopaminergic part of the substantia nigra. The interstitial nucleus of Cajal and nucleus of Darkschewitsch lie near the oculomotor complex and do not give rise to the rubrospinal tract.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Red nucleus outputs: magnocellular to rubrospinal (crossed, cervical cord), parvocellular to rubro-olivary (ipsilateral, central tegmental tract); inputs from the contralateral cerebellar nuclei and ipsilateral cortex. COMLEX: Red nucleus lesions cause contralateral tremor and ataxia because they interrupt crossed cerebellar output.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 Bloom → Obj 1 — the red nucleus described anatomically at the superior colliculus level, applied here to its two efferent projections","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"}],"21":[{"id":"sdl21_q01","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply midbrain anatomy to board-style questions involving eye movement abnormalities, pupillary findings, tremor/ataxia, altered consciousness, and stroke localization.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient has an ipsilateral fixed, dilated pupil with ptosis and impaired adduction, elevation, and depression of one eye, together with new contralateral limb weakness and no other cranial nerve findings. This alternating pattern localizes the lesion to which brainstem level?","choices":{"A":"The pons, via the abducens fascicles and pyramidal fibers","B":"The medulla, via the hypoglossal fascicles and pyramid","C":"The internal capsule, via the posterior limb alone","D":"The cavernous sinus, via a purely compressive mechanism","E":"The midbrain, via the oculomotor fascicles and crus cerebri"},"correct":"E","explanation":"An ipsilateral CN III palsy paired with contralateral hemiparesis is a classic alternating, or crossed, sign that localizes to the midbrain, since only there do the exiting oculomotor fascicles and the still-uncrossed corticospinal fibers of the crus cerebri run close enough together to be damaged by a single lesion. A pontine lesion would instead pair a CN VI or VII finding with the crossed weakness, not CN III. A medullary lesion would pair a CN XII finding with crossed weakness. A purely compressive cavernous sinus lesion would not explain contralateral limb weakness. An internal capsule lesion produces pure contralateral weakness without any ipsilateral cranial nerve deficit.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Alternating brainstem signs are a core localization tool; the specific cranial nerve involved pinpoints the level, with CN III always indicating the midbrain. COMLEX: This exact pattern is the anatomic basis of Weber syndrome, one of the highest-yield named brainstem syndromes tested.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 4 — the five named midbrain syndromes, applied here as integrated board-style questions spanning eye movements, pupils, tremor, consciousness, and stroke localization","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A pontine lesion would instead","B":"A medullary lesion would pair "}},{"id":"sdl21_q02","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply midbrain anatomy to board-style questions involving eye movement abnormalities, pupillary findings, tremor/ataxia, altered consciousness, and stroke localization.","batch":1,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with poorly controlled diabetes develops a painless CN III palsy with severely limited eye movement but a pupil that constricts normally to light. Which mechanism best explains why the pupil is spared in this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Compressive injury striking the superficial fibers first","B":"Ischemic injury centered on the central fascicular fibers","C":"Simultaneous injury to parasympathetic and sympathetic fibers","D":"Selective sparing of the Edinger-Westphal nucleus alone","E":"A coexisting lesion of the ciliary ganglion outflow"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Diabetic microvascular ischemia damages the vasa nervorum supplying the central, deeper motor fascicles of CN III while the superficial parasympathetic fibers governing pupillary constriction, which have their own separate blood supply along the nerve's surface, are relatively spared, producing a pupil-sparing palsy. Compressive lesions instead strike the superficial fibers first and typically involve the pupil early. Simultaneous parasympathetic and sympathetic injury would not selectively spare the pupil. Isolated Edinger-Westphal nucleus sparing does not explain a peripheral nerve fascicular pattern. A ciliary ganglion lesion would be a separate, unrelated finding.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pupil-sparing CN III palsy from diabetic ischemia versus pupil-involving CN III palsy from a compressive aneurysm is one of the single highest-yield distinctions in cranial nerve testing. COMLEX: Any new pupil-involving CN III palsy still requires urgent vascular imaging even when diabetes is present, since compression and ischemia can coexist.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 4 — the five named midbrain syndromes, applied here as integrated board-style questions spanning eye movements, pupils, tremor, consciousness, and stroke localization","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q03","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply midbrain anatomy to board-style questions involving eye movement abnormalities, pupillary findings, tremor/ataxia, altered consciousness, and stroke localization.","batch":1,"stem":"A focal lesion centered on the midbrain reticular formation produces progressive hypersomnolence with slow respirations and a large-amplitude, slow-wave EEG pattern resembling natural sleep. Which functional system best explains this clinical picture?","choices":{"A":"The ascending reticular activating system","B":"The medial longitudinal fasciculus","C":"The spinothalamic tract sensory pathway","D":"The descending corticospinal pathway","E":"The pretectal light reflex pathway"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The rostral and caudal midbrain reticular formation, including the cuneiform and subcuneiform nuclei, projects to the thalamus and hypothalamus and forms a critical relay of the ascending reticular activating system, so its disruption impairs cortical arousal and produces hypersomnolence with a sleep-like EEG pattern. The medial longitudinal fasciculus coordinates conjugate eye movements, not arousal. The spinothalamic tract carries pain and temperature sensation, unrelated to wakefulness. The corticospinal tract governs voluntary motor output, not consciousness level. The pretectal pupillary pathway governs the light reflex, not arousal state.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The ascending reticular activating system passing through the midbrain tegmentum is the anatomic basis for why midbrain lesions, unlike isolated cortical lesions, so readily impair consciousness. COMLEX: Progressive descent from alertness through stupor to coma during transtentorial herniation reflects exactly this reticular activating system compression.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 4 — the five named midbrain syndromes, applied here as integrated board-style questions spanning eye movements, pupils, tremor, consciousness, and stroke localization","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"The corticospinal tract govern"}},{"id":"sdl21_q04","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply midbrain anatomy to board-style questions involving eye movement abnormalities, pupillary findings, tremor/ataxia, altered consciousness, and stroke localization.","batch":2,"stem":"Three months after a small left rostral midbrain infarct, a 55-year-old man develops a slow, coarse tremor of the right arm that is present at rest, worsens with sustained posture, and worsens further as he reaches for a target. He also has mild right-sided limb incoordination on finger-to-nose testing. Which structure was most likely damaged to eventually produce this delayed tremor?","choices":{"A":"The substantia nigra pars compacta dopaminergic neurons","B":"The medial lemniscus and its thalamic sensory projections","C":"The red nucleus and its cerebellothalamic connections","D":"The corticospinal fibers traveling within the crus cerebri","E":"The oculomotor nucleus and its exiting fascicular fibers"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A rubral, or Holmes, tremor combines rest, postural, and action components with limb ataxia, and arises from damage to the red nucleus and the cerebellothalamic fibers passing through it, often emerging weeks to months after the initial infarct as aberrant connections reorganize; the tremor and ataxia are contralateral to a lesion in the left tegmentum, consistent with right-sided findings here. Substantia nigra damage instead produces a resting-predominant parkinsonian tremor with rigidity and bradykinesia, not this combined pattern. Medial lemniscus damage produces sensory loss, not tremor. Crus cerebri corticospinal damage produces spastic weakness, not tremor. Oculomotor fascicle damage produces a CN III palsy, not a limb movement disorder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Rubral or Holmes tremor is distinguished from parkinsonian tremor by its combined rest-postural-action character and its delayed onset after a structural midbrain lesion. COMLEX: The cerebellothalamic fibers passing through the red nucleus explain why this tremor and ataxia appear contralateral to the causative lesion, mirroring cerebellar outflow crossing.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 3 — localizing red nucleus and tremor-ataxia lesions within midbrain cross-sections","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply midbrain anatomy to board-style questions involving eye movement abnormalities, pupillary findings, tremor/ataxia, altered consciousness, and stroke localization.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman with atrial fibrillation develops sudden left arm and leg weakness along with a right dilated, unreactive pupil and an inability to adduct, elevate, or depress the right eye. Which vessel occlusion best explains this combination of findings?","choices":{"A":"A paramedian branch of the posterior cerebral artery","B":"The right middle cerebral artery main trunk territory","C":"The anterior spinal artery at the medullary level","D":"The right posterior inferior cerebellar artery territory","E":"The basilar artery at the mid-pontine level"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This alternating pattern of ipsilateral CN III palsy with contralateral hemiparesis is the classic Weber syndrome presentation, which most commonly follows occlusion of paramedian branches arising from the proximal posterior cerebral artery or the basilar bifurcation supplying the medial midbrain, a plausible embolic source given her atrial fibrillation. A right MCA trunk occlusion would produce cortical signs such as aphasia or neglect without a CN III palsy. Anterior spinal artery occlusion at the medullary level would produce tongue weakness, not CN III involvement. PICA occlusion produces lateral medullary syndrome with vertigo and dysphagia, not this pattern. Mid-pontine basilar occlusion would pair crossed findings with CN VI or VII deficits, not CN III.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Recognizing Weber syndrome from its alternating sign pattern and linking it to paramedian posterior cerebral or basilar branch occlusion is a core stroke-localization skill. First Aid specifically names Weber syndrome as the midbrain stroke from occlusion of paramedian branches of the PCA, producing ipsilateral CN III palsy and contralateral hemiplegia. COMLEX: Atrial fibrillation as an embolic source should always prompt consideration of posterior circulation strokes, not just the more commonly tested anterior circulation territories.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — PCA and basilar-territory deficits within the broader stroke-localization algorithm","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"PICA occlusion produces latera"}},{"id":"sdl21_q06","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply midbrain anatomy to board-style questions involving eye movement abnormalities, pupillary findings, tremor/ataxia, altered consciousness, and stroke localization.","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old man with a large right cerebellar hemorrhage becomes increasingly drowsy. Repeat imaging shows medial cerebellar tissue displaced upward through the tentorial notch and compressing the midbrain, with new enlargement of the lateral and third ventricles and a new infarct on the superior surface of the cerebellum. Which process best explains these findings?","choices":{"A":"Uncal herniation of the medial temporal lobe over the tentorial edge","B":"Upward cerebellar herniation through the tentorial notch","C":"Central transtentorial herniation forcing the diencephalon downward","D":"Tonsillar herniation through the foramen magnum compressing the medulla","E":"Subfalcine herniation of the cingulate gyrus under the falx"},"correct":"B","explanation":"An expanding posterior fossa mass can push medial portions of the cerebellum upward through the tentorial notch, compressing the midbrain. This upward cerebellar herniation can infarct the cerebellum by compressing the superior cerebellar arteries and cause hydrocephalus by obstructing the cerebral aqueduct, matching the enlarged lateral and third ventricles; treatment may require posterior fossa decompression. Uncal and central herniation are driven by supratentorial masses moving downward. Tonsillar herniation moves cerebellar tonsils downward through the foramen magnum. Subfalcine herniation shifts the cingulate gyrus beneath the falx and compresses the anterior cerebral artery.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Midbrain-related herniations: central (downward), uncal (downward and medial), and upward cerebellar (from a posterior fossa mass); know which vessel and which CSF pathway each compresses. COMLEX: A deteriorating patient with a cerebellar hemorrhage needs urgent neurosurgical decompression.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — rostral and caudal midbrain structures, including the aqueduct and tectum compressed by upward herniation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Uncal and central herniation a"}},{"id":"sdl21_bloom_q01","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply midbrain anatomy to board-style questions involving eye movement abnormalities, pupillary findings, tremor/ataxia, altered consciousness, and stroke localization.","batch":3,"stem":"A 61-year-old man has a 2-hour history of sudden-onset diplopia and left-sided weakness. Exam findings:\n- Right eye deviated down and out, with ptosis and a dilated, poorly reactive pupil\n- Left hemiparesis involving the face, arm, and leg\n- No facial droop on the right, no hearing loss, no tongue deviation\nThis patient's condition is most likely caused by ischemia in a region supplied by the ___.","choices":{"A":"basilar","B":"posterior cerebral","C":"superior cerebellar","D":"posterior communicating","E":"anterior choroidal"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The ipsilateral CN III findings, down-and-out eye, ptosis, and a dilated pupil, paired with contralateral hemiparesis, is a crossed pattern that only occurs where exiting CN III fascicles and the still-uncrossed crus cerebri sit together, the midbrain. The paramedian penetrating branches supplying this territory arise from the posterior cerebral artery. Some paramedian midbrain perforators arise at the basilar bifurcation, but the vessel classically named for Weber syndrome is the PCA via its paramedian branches; the basilar trunk's own paramedian branches supply the pons. The superior cerebellar artery supplies the superior cerebellum and rostral cerebellar peduncle, not the crus cerebri. The posterior communicating artery is the classic site of aneurysmal CN III compression, but that produces an isolated painful palsy without contralateral weakness. The anterior choroidal artery supplies the posterior limb of the internal capsule and optic tract, not the crus cerebri.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crossed CN III plus contralateral hemiparesis localizes to the midbrain and implicates PCA paramedian branches, one of the highest-yield vessel-to-syndrome links tested. COMLEX: Distinguishing PCA paramedian ischemia from PCoA aneurysmal compression, both cause CN III palsy, hinges entirely on whether contralateral weakness is present.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 4 — the five named midbrain syndromes, restyled here as board-qbank vignettes that never name the syndrome and instead require the student to derive vessel, structure, or finding from raw crossed findings","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl21_bloom_q02","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply midbrain anatomy to board-style questions involving eye movement abnormalities, pupillary findings, tremor/ataxia, altered consciousness, and stroke localization.","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman has acute-onset right eye ptosis with a dilated pupil and impaired adduction, elevation, and depression of that eye. Over the following days she develops a coarse, irregular tremor and ataxia of the left arm that worsens with intentional movement. This patient's condition is most likely caused by an injury to the ___.","choices":{"A":"dentate nucleus","B":"inferior olive","C":"vestibular nuclei","D":"red nucleus","E":"medial lemniscus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ipsilateral CN III palsy combined with a delayed contralateral coarse tremor and ataxia reflects involvement of the red nucleus, which lies adjacent to the exiting oculomotor fascicles and relays cerebellar output before it crosses again, so red nucleus damage produces a contralateral rubral tremor. The dentate nucleus sends the same dentatorubrothalamic fibers toward the red nucleus, a genuine anatomic neighbor, but isolated dentate injury would not explain the ipsilateral CN III finding, since it sits within the cerebellum, not the midbrain. The inferior olive relays to the cerebellum and, when damaged with the central tegmental tract, can produce palatal tremor, but not this CN III-paired limb tremor. The vestibular nuclei govern balance and eye reflexes, not limb tremor, and sit in the medulla and pons, not the midbrain. The medial lemniscus carries proprioceptive and vibratory sensation, not motor coordination, and its damage would not produce tremor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CN III palsy plus contralateral cerebellar-type tremor localizes to the red nucleus and is a classic named-syndrome trap question when the syndrome name is withheld. COMLEX: The delayed onset of the tremor relative to the acute CN III findings reflects the time needed for cerebellar deafferentation effects to manifest and is a frequently tested detail.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 4 — the five named midbrain syndromes, restyled here as board-qbank vignettes that never name the syndrome and instead require the student to derive vessel, structure, or finding from raw crossed findings","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)","whyNot":{"A":"The dentate nucleus sends the "}},{"id":"sdl21_bloom_q03","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply midbrain anatomy to board-style questions involving eye movement abnormalities, pupillary findings, tremor/ataxia, altered consciousness, and stroke localization.","batch":3,"stem":"In the Weber, Claude, and Benedikt syndromes, the pupil on the side of the lesion is often dilated as well as the eye being turned down and out. This pupillary finding occurs because damage to the Edinger-Westphal preganglionic fibers leaves the iris dilator driven by intact ___","choices":{"A":"sympathetic input to the dilator muscle","B":"accommodation input to the ciliary muscle","C":"abducens input to the lateral rectus","D":"trochlear input to the superior oblique","E":"pretectal input to the ciliary ganglion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In each of these medial and central midbrain syndromes, the visceral efferent preganglionic parasympathetic fibers arising in the Edinger-Westphal nucleus may be damaged; the pupil on the side of the lesion is then dilated because the sympathetic input to the dilator muscle of the iris remains intact and unopposed. Accommodation input to the ciliary muscle travels with the same lost parasympathetic fibers, so it cannot drive the pupil. The abducens and trochlear nerves act on the lateral rectus and superior oblique, which explains why the eye rests down and out, not why the pupil dilates. Pretectal input reaches the Edinger-Westphal nucleus, not the ciliary ganglion directly.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A blown pupil with a down-and-out eye means oculomotor parasympathetic loss with unopposed sympathetic tone; a small pupil with ptosis means Horner syndrome. COMLEX: A new unilateral dilated pupil in a drowsy patient suggests third nerve compression from herniation until proven otherwise.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 4 — the named midbrain syndromes, extended here to the pupillary mechanism they share","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"}],"22":[{"id":"sdl22_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — List the brainstem levels associated with cranial nerves III-XII and identify the CN XI spinal accessory exception.","batch":1,"stem":"The nuclei of which two cranial nerves are located at midbrain levels?","choices":{"A":"Cranial nerves V and VI, trigeminal and abducens","B":"Cranial nerves III and IV, oculomotor and trochlear","C":"Cranial nerves IX and X, glossopharyngeal and vagus","D":"Cranial nerves VII and VIII, facial and vestibulocochlear","E":"Cranial nerves XI and XII, accessory and hypoglossal"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The oculomotor nucleus sits at the rostral, superior colliculus level and the trochlear nucleus at the caudal, inferior colliculus level, making CN III and CN IV the only two nerves whose nuclei reside in the midbrain. CN V is the pons, CN VI/VII/VIII sit at the pons-medulla junction, CN IX/X are medullary, and CN XI arises from cervical spinal cord, not the midbrain.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Only two cranial nerve nuclei live in the midbrain, a fact tested constantly when localizing alternating brainstem syndromes. COMLEX: A dilated pupil with down-and-out eye immediately narrows localization to the midbrain level.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 2 — pontine cranial nerve nuclei V, VI, VII, and VIII, now placed within the complete rostrocaudal brainstem-level survey of all twelve cranial nerves plus the CN XI exception","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"CN V is the pons, CN VI/VII/VI","E":"CN V is the pons, CN VI/VII/VI"}},{"id":"sdl22_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — List the brainstem levels associated with cranial nerves III-XII and identify the CN XI spinal accessory exception.","batch":1,"stem":"The trigeminal motor and principal sensory nuclei are centered at which brainstem level?","choices":{"A":"The pons, home to CN V nuclei","B":"The midbrain, home to CN III/IV","C":"The medulla, home to CN IX/X","D":"The pons-medulla junction, VI/VII/VIII","E":"The cervical spinal cord, home to CN XI"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The trigeminal motor nucleus and the principal sensory nucleus both sit at midpontine levels, making CN V the defining nerve of the pons proper, even though its sensory column extends both rostrally into the midbrain and caudally into the medulla. CN VI, VII, and VIII instead exit at the caudal pons-medulla junction rather than mid-pons. CN III/IV belong to the midbrain, CN IX/X to the medulla, and CN XI to the cervical cord.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CN V is the classic exception nerve, its sensory nucleus spans nearly the entire brainstem, but its motor and principal sensory nuclei still mark the pons as their home level. COMLEX: Ipsilateral facial numbness with jaw deviation toward the lesion localizes to a pontine trigeminal lesion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 2 — pontine cranial nerve nuclei V, VI, VII, and VIII, now placed within the complete rostrocaudal brainstem-level survey of all twelve cranial nerves plus the CN XI exception","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — List the brainstem levels associated with cranial nerves III-XII and identify the CN XI spinal accessory exception.","batch":1,"stem":"A physician explains that cranial nerve XI is unusual because, unlike every other cranial nerve, its motor neurons are not located in the brainstem at all. Where do these motor neurons actually reside?","choices":{"A":"The nucleus ambiguus of the caudal medulla oblongata","B":"The dorsal motor nucleus within the medullary vagal trigone","C":"The lateral cell column of the lower thoracic spinal cord","D":"The anterior horn of the cervical cord, C1-C6","E":"The intermediate gray matter of the lumbosacral spinal cord"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The accessory nerve is the true exception among cranial nerves: its somatic efferent motor neurons originate exclusively from anterior horn cells of the cervical spinal cord, roughly C1-C6, with no contribution from medullary nucleus ambiguus despite the old teaching of a separate cranial root. These fibers ascend through the foramen magnum, briefly join the vagus, and exit via the jugular foramen. Nucleus ambiguus and the dorsal motor vagal nucleus belong to the vagus nerve, and thoracic or lumbosacral cord levels are unrelated to CN XI.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The 'cranial root of CN XI' is now considered a misnomer, boards increasingly test that all accessory motor neurons are spinal, C1-C6, not medullary. COMLEX: This spinal origin explains why isolated CN XI palsy causes trapezius/sternocleidomastoid weakness without any other cranial nerve findings.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 2 — pontine cranial nerve nuclei V, VI, VII, and VIII, now placed within the complete rostrocaudal brainstem-level survey of all twelve cranial nerves plus the CN XI exception","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Nucleus ambiguus and the dorsa"}},{"id":"sdl22_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate somatic motor, branchial motor, parasympathetic, general sensory, special sensory, and visceral sensory components.","batch":1,"stem":"Motor neurons of the trigeminal motor nucleus innervate the muscles of mastication, which develop from the first pharyngeal arch. Which functional component classification applies to these motor neurons?","choices":{"A":"Somatic motor, also termed general somatic efferent","B":"Parasympathetic, also termed general visceral efferent","C":"General sensory, also termed general somatic afferent","D":"Visceral sensory, also termed general visceral afferent","E":"Branchial motor, also termed special visceral efferent"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Muscles derived from pharyngeal arch mesoderm, such as the muscles of mastication innervated by CN V, the muscles of facial expression innervated by CN VII, and the pharyngeal and laryngeal muscles innervated by CN IX and X, are classified as branchial motor, or special visceral efferent, because that mesoderm migrated through the pharyngeal arches. Somatic motor instead innervates extraocular and tongue muscles from paraxial mesoderm. Parasympathetic is a visceral efferent component to smooth muscle and glands, not skeletal muscle. General sensory and visceral sensory are afferent categories, not motor ones.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Branchial motor versus somatic motor hinges entirely on embryologic origin, arch mesoderm versus paraxial mesoderm, a distinction tested repeatedly. COMLEX: Recognizing branchial motor nerves helps predict which muscles atrophy together after a specific pharyngeal arch nerve injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 7 — somatic, visceral, general, and special functional categories, now applied specifically to classifying each cranial nerve's individual functional components","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate somatic motor, branchial motor, parasympathetic, general sensory, special sensory, and visceral sensory components.","batch":1,"stem":"The Edinger-Westphal nucleus gives rise to preganglionic fibers that synapse in the ciliary ganglion to constrict the pupil. Which functional component best describes these fibers?","choices":{"A":"Somatic motor, also termed general somatic efferent","B":"Branchial motor, also termed special visceral efferent","C":"Parasympathetic, also termed general visceral efferent","D":"Special sensory, also termed special somatic afferent","E":"Visceral sensory, also termed general visceral afferent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The Edinger-Westphal nucleus is the cranial parasympathetic, or general visceral efferent, component of CN III, along with the superior and inferior salivatory nuclei of CN VII and CN IX and the dorsal motor vagal nucleus of CN X, all giving rise to preganglionic fibers that terminate in peripheral ganglia innervating smooth muscle or glands. Somatic motor and branchial motor are efferent categories reserved for skeletal muscle. Special sensory and visceral sensory are afferent, not efferent, categories.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Edinger-Westphal equals parasympathetic outflow to the pupil, a favorite pairing when boards ask about compressive versus ischemic CN III palsy. COMLEX: These superficially located parasympathetic fibers are compressed first by an expanding aneurysm, sparing extraocular strength initially.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 7 — somatic, visceral, general, and special functional categories, now applied specifically to classifying each cranial nerve's individual functional components","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate somatic motor, branchial motor, parasympathetic, general sensory, special sensory, and visceral sensory components.","batch":1,"stem":"The glossopharyngeal nerve supplies a single skeletal muscle, the stylopharyngeus, which assists swallowing and takes part in the efferent limb of the gag reflex. From which nucleus, and with which functional component, do these motor fibers arise?","choices":{"A":"The hypoglossal nucleus, as somatic motor fibers","B":"The inferior salivatory nucleus, as parasympathetic fibers","C":"The dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus, as parasympathetic fibers","D":"The nucleus ambiguus, as branchial motor fibers","E":"The facial motor nucleus, as branchial motor fibers"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Motor fibers on the glossopharyngeal nerve come from two nuclei: the inferior salivatory nucleus, whose preganglionic parasympathetic fibers reach the otic ganglion for the parotid gland, and the nucleus ambiguus, whose branchial motor fibers innervate the stylopharyngeus, the third pharyngeal arch muscle that assists swallowing and the gag reflex. The hypoglossal nucleus supplies tongue muscles through cranial nerve XII. The inferior salivatory nucleus drives parotid secretion, not a skeletal muscle. The dorsal motor vagal nucleus supplies thoracic and abdominal viscera, and the facial motor nucleus supplies second-arch muscles through cranial nerve VII.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cranial nerve IX motor: stylopharyngeus (nucleus ambiguus, branchial motor) and parotid secretion (inferior salivatory nucleus via the otic ganglion). COMLEX: Isolated IX lesions mainly reduce taste on the posterior third of the tongue and weaken the gag reflex on that side.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — the nucleus ambiguus and its branchial motor axons in cranial nerves IX and X","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Use cranial nerve findings to localize lesions to the midbrain, pons, medulla, cavernous sinus, internal acoustic meatus, jugular foramen, or hypoglossal canal.","batch":1,"stem":"A mass at the superior orbital fissure causes ophthalmoplegia with ptosis and a dilated pupil, plus numbness of the forehead. Sensation over the cheek and upper lip is normal. Why is cheek sensation preserved with a lesion at this site?","choices":{"A":"Cheek sensation is carried by the nasociliary branch of the ophthalmic division","B":"The maxillary division exits the skull through the foramen rotundum instead","C":"The cheek is supplied by the facial nerve, which is not in the orbit","D":"The maxillary division passes through the fissure but carries motor fibers exclusively","E":"The cheek is supplied by the mandibular division through the foramen ovale"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cranial nerves III, IV, and VI and the ophthalmic division of V pass through the superior orbital fissure into the orbit, so a lesion there causes ophthalmoplegia, ptosis, a dilated pupil, and forehead numbness. The maxillary division leaves the cranial cavity separately through the foramen rotundum and reaches the face through the pterygopalatine fossa, inferior orbital fissure, and infraorbital foramen, so cheek sensation is spared; a cavernous sinus lesion, in contrast, can involve the maxillary division as well. The nasociliary branch of the ophthalmic division supplies the eye and nose, not the cheek. The facial nerve is motor to facial muscles and does not supply cheek skin. The maxillary division is sensory, and the mandibular division supplies the lower face and jaw through the foramen ovale.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Exits of V: V1 superior orbital fissure, V2 foramen rotundum, V3 foramen ovale (with the motor root). COMLEX: Involvement of V2 distinguishes a cavernous sinus lesion from a superior orbital fissure lesion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — cavernous sinus relationships, now contrasted with the superior orbital fissure as a distinct localization site","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Use cranial nerve findings to localize lesions to the midbrain, pons, medulla, cavernous sinus, internal acoustic meatus, jugular foramen, or hypoglossal canal.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient develops progressive ipsilateral hearing loss, tinnitus, and unsteadiness, later joined by facial weakness on the same side. Imaging shows a mass at the site through which both the affected cranial nerves pass. Which site is this?","choices":{"A":"The cavernous sinus wall","B":"The jugular foramen","C":"The internal acoustic meatus","D":"The hypoglossal canal","E":"The foramen magnum"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The internal acoustic meatus transmits CN VII and CN VIII together through the petrous temporal bone, so a mass here, classically a vestibular schwannoma, produces early hearing loss, tinnitus, and vertigo from CN VIII compression followed by facial weakness as CN VII becomes involved. The cavernous sinus carries CN III, IV, VI, V1, and V2, not VII or VIII. The jugular foramen carries CN IX, X, and XI, and the hypoglossal canal carries only CN XII, neither matching this deficit pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vestibular schwannoma at the cerebellopontine angle and internal acoustic meatus is the textbook cause of this exact deficit sequence, VIII before VII. COMLEX: Bilateral vestibular schwannomas at the internal acoustic meatus point toward neurofibromatosis type 2.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — cavernous sinus relationships, now extended into a full set of cranial nerve lesion-localization landmarks including the internal acoustic meatus, jugular foramen, and hypoglossal canal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Use cranial nerve findings to localize lesions to the midbrain, pons, medulla, cavernous sinus, internal acoustic meatus, jugular foramen, or hypoglossal canal.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient has hoarseness, dysphagia, an absent gag reflex, and weakness turning the head to the opposite side plus ipsilateral shoulder droop, but the tongue protrudes normally at the midline. Which structure is most likely the site of the lesion?","choices":{"A":"The jugular foramen","B":"The hypoglossal canal","C":"The cavernous sinus","D":"The internal acoustic meatus","E":"The superior orbital fissure"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The jugular foramen transmits CN IX, X, and XI together, so a lesion here produces the classic Vernet syndrome, hoarseness and dysphagia from IX/X, an absent gag reflex, and sternocleidomastoid/trapezius weakness from XI, while sparing the hypoglossal nerve, which exits separately through the adjacent hypoglossal canal, explaining the normal midline tongue protrusion. The hypoglossal canal alone would cause only tongue deviation. The cavernous sinus and internal acoustic meatus carry entirely different nerve groups that do not match this pattern. The superior orbital fissure carries CN III, IV, V1 and VI into the orbit, so a lesion there would cause ophthalmoplegia, not this lower cranial nerve pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vernet syndrome, the jugular foramen triad of IX, X, and XI with a spared hypoglossal nerve, is a classic localizing vignette. COMLEX: Collet-Sicard syndrome adds ipsilateral tongue weakness when the lesion extends externally to recruit the adjacent hypoglossal canal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — cavernous sinus relationships, now extended into a full set of cranial nerve lesion-localization landmarks including the internal acoustic meatus, jugular foramen, and hypoglossal canal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — List the brainstem levels associated with cranial nerves III-XII and identify the CN XI spinal accessory exception.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man is brought to the emergency department after sudden onset of double vision. Examination shows a right eye that rests down and out with ptosis and a dilated, poorly reactive pupil, combined with left-sided arm and leg weakness. There is no facial weakness, hearing loss, or tongue deviation. Which brainstem level best explains this cranial nerve finding?","choices":{"A":"The pons, where CN VI fascicles pass near the corticospinal fibers","B":"The midbrain, where CN III fascicles pass near the corticospinal fibers","C":"The medulla, where CN XII fascicles pass near the corticospinal fibers","D":"The pons-medulla junction, near the exiting CN VII fascicles","E":"The cervical spinal cord, near the exiting CN XI fascicles"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The oculomotor nucleus and exiting CN III fascicles are located in the midbrain, passing directly through the crus cerebri where corticospinal fibers descend, so a single midbrain lesion produces the classic alternating pattern of ipsilateral CN III palsy, down-and-out eye with a blown pupil, plus contralateral hemiparesis, this is Weber syndrome. A pontine lesion would instead pair CN VI or VII findings with contralateral weakness, a medullary lesion would pair CN XII findings, and cervical cord involvement would not produce isolated ophthalmoplegia at all.","boardPrep":"Step 1: An alternating CN III palsy with contralateral hemiparesis is the single most tested midbrain localizing pattern, Weber syndrome. COMLEX: Always check pupil involvement first, since compressive lesions affect the pupil early while ischemic microvascular CN III palsy classically spares it.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 3 — Midbrain CN III/IV lesion localization alongside corticospinal fibers","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A pontine lesion would instead"}},{"id":"sdl22_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — List the brainstem levels associated with cranial nerves III-XII and identify the CN XI spinal accessory exception.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman develops isolated right shoulder droop and difficulty turning her head to the left against resistance after a penetrating neck injury at the level of C3. She has no facial asymmetry, no tongue deviation, no dysphagia, and no hearing changes. Which explanation best accounts for the isolated nature of this deficit?","choices":{"A":"CN XI motor neurons originate in the cervical cord, not the brainstem","B":"CN XI motor neurons share the nucleus ambiguus with CN IX and X","C":"CN XI motor neurons originate in the pons with CN VII","D":"CN XI motor neurons decussate fully before exiting the medulla","E":"CN XI motor neurons travel only within the vagus nerve"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because the accessory nerve's somatic efferent motor neurons arise entirely from the anterior horn of the cervical spinal cord, roughly C1-C6, rather than from any brainstem nucleus, a lesion confined to the cervical cord or the nerve's cervical course can damage only the trapezius and sternocleidomastoid muscles without producing any brainstem cranial nerve findings such as facial weakness, tongue deviation, dysphagia, or hearing loss. The nucleus ambiguus supplies CN IX and X, not XI. CN XI does not originate in the pons, does not fully decussate, and only temporarily and briefly associates with the vagus before separating to exit independently.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The isolated, purely spinal origin of CN XI motor neurons is the key fact explaining why peripheral CN XI injury never comes with other cranial nerve findings. COMLEX: Penetrating posterior triangle neck trauma is a classic mechanism for isolated spinal accessory nerve injury.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 3 — Cranial nerve findings applied to lesion localization across cortex, brainstem, cavernous sinus, orbit, skull base, or peripheral nerve","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"CN XI does not originate in th"}},{"id":"sdl22_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — List the brainstem levels associated with cranial nerves III-XII and identify the CN XI spinal accessory exception.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman presents with hoarseness, difficulty swallowing, an absent gag reflex, and tongue deviation toward the right side on protrusion. Vertical eye movements, facial strength, and hearing are all normal. Which brainstem level houses the nuclei responsible for this constellation of findings?","choices":{"A":"The midbrain, home to the oculomotor and trochlear nuclei","B":"The pons, home to the trigeminal motor and principal sensory nuclei","C":"The pons-medulla junction, home to the abducens and facial nuclei","D":"The medulla, home to the nucleus ambiguus and the hypoglossal nucleus","E":"The cervical spinal cord, home to the accessory nucleus alone"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The nucleus ambiguus, giving rise to CN IX and X motor fibers to the pharynx and larynx, and the hypoglossal nucleus, giving rise to CN XII fibers to the tongue, are both medullary structures, so hoarseness, dysphagia, absent gag, and ipsilateral tongue deviation together localize precisely to the medulla, sparing the midbrain-level vertical gaze, pontine facial strength, and hearing pathways. None of the other listed levels contain both the nucleus ambiguus and the hypoglossal nucleus together.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Combined IX/X/XII findings with spared eye movements and facial strength is a classic medullary localization pattern. COMLEX: This same combination appears in medial and lateral medullary stroke vignettes, distinguished by which additional tracts are involved.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — Medullary cranial nerve nuclei: hypoglossal nucleus, dorsal motor vagal nucleus, nucleus ambiguus, solitary nucleus, vestibular nuclei, spinal trigeminal nucleus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate somatic motor, branchial motor, parasympathetic, general sensory, special sensory, and visceral sensory components.","batch":2,"stem":"A first-year student classifies the motor neurons of the trigeminal motor nucleus as special visceral efferent, following the traditional scheme. The course text also presents a contemporary scheme that emphasizes only somatic versus visceral and drops the general and special labels. In that contemporary scheme, how are the motor neurons to pharyngeal arch muscles of cranial nerves V, VII, IX, and X classified, and why?","choices":{"A":"Somatic efferent, because these striated arch muscles arise from paraxial mesoderm","B":"Visceral efferent, because these muscles form around the embryonic gut tube","C":"Visceral efferent, because these neurons lie in the basal plate next to the sulcus limitans","D":"Somatic afferent, because these muscles contain abundant proprioceptive spindles","E":"General visceral efferent, because they act through ganglia near their target muscles"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Muscle-forming mesoderm arises from the paraxial mesoderm and migrates into pharyngeal arches I to IV, where it forms the striated muscles innervated by cranial nerves V, VII, IX, and X. Because these muscles share their embryologic source with other skeletal muscle, the contemporary scheme applies the functional component somatic efferent to all of these motor neurons and their axons; the traditional special visceral efferent, or branchial motor, label remains acceptable, and the course color-codes both. The muscles form from paraxial mesoderm rather than from gut-associated tissue. Basal plate position is shared by all motor nuclei and does not decide somatic versus visceral. Proprioceptive spindles are an afferent feature, and these neurons synapse directly on muscle without peripheral ganglia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know both labels: traditional branchial motor (special visceral efferent) and contemporary somatic efferent for pharyngeal arch motor neurons. COMLEX: Whatever label is used, these are lower motor neurons, so their lesions cause ipsilateral flaccid weakness of the arch muscles.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 2 — Pontine trigeminal motor and principal sensory nuclei","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The muscles form from paraxial","E":"Proprioceptive spindles are an"}},{"id":"sdl22_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate somatic motor, branchial motor, parasympathetic, general sensory, special sensory, and visceral sensory components.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with a rapidly expanding posterior communicating artery aneurysm develops a dilated, unreactive right pupil before any measurable weakness of the extraocular muscles. Which functional component explains why this deficit appears first?","choices":{"A":"Somatic motor fibers riding superficially along CN III","B":"Branchial motor fibers riding superficially along CN III","C":"Parasympathetic fibers riding superficially along CN III","D":"General sensory fibers riding superficially along CN III","E":"Visceral sensory fibers riding superficially along CN III"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The parasympathetic, or general visceral efferent, fibers originating from the Edinger-Westphal nucleus travel along the outer surface of the oculomotor nerve, making them the first fibers compressed by an expanding extrinsic mass such as a posterior communicating artery aneurysm, producing early pupillary dilation before the more centrally located somatic motor fibers to the extraocular muscles become symptomatic. Somatic motor fibers to the extraocular muscles are centrally located within the nerve, becoming affected later. Branchial motor and general sensory are not components carried by CN III at all, and visceral sensory afferents are not carried by the oculomotor nerve either.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pupil-involving CN III palsy from compression versus pupil-sparing CN III palsy from ischemic microvascular disease is one of the highest-yield distinctions on the exam. COMLEX: Any new pupil-involving third nerve palsy warrants urgent vascular imaging to exclude an aneurysm.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 3 — Midbrain CN III localization and compressive versus ischemic palsy patterns","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Somatic motor fibers to the ex"}},{"id":"sdl22_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate somatic motor, branchial motor, parasympathetic, general sensory, special sensory, and visceral sensory components.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old woman's audiologist explains that, although the cochlear division of cranial nerve VIII is classically described as purely sensory, it also carries a small bundle of cholinergic axons from cells beside the superior olivary nuclei that end on the inner and outer hair cells. What is the function of these efferent fibers?","choices":{"A":"They release glutamate that excites the spiral ganglion neurons","B":"They inhibit, or dampen, the response of the hair cells to stimuli","C":"They contract the stapedius muscle to stiffen the ossicular chain","D":"They carry taste fibers from the tongue that join the cochlear nerve","E":"They supply parasympathetic secretomotor input to the inner ear glands"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cholinergic cells adjacent to the principal and accessory olivary nuclei give rise to axons that leave the brainstem on the cochlear nerve as the olivocochlear tract, also called the efferent cochlear bundle or bundle of Rasmussen; they synapse in relation to the inner and outer hair cells and inhibit, or dampen, the ability of the hair cells to respond to stimuli. They are cholinergic and inhibitory rather than glutamatergic and excitatory. The stapedius is supplied by a branch of the facial nerve. Taste travels in cranial nerves VII, IX, and X to the solitary nucleus, and the cochlear nerve carries no parasympathetic secretomotor fibers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cranial nerve VIII is mainly special sensory (hearing and balance) but carries olivocochlear efferents that dampen hair cell responses. COMLEX: Do not label every cranial nerve fiber by its main function; several nerves carry small minority components.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 1 — auditory receptor and pathway organization, here the efferent control of cochlear hair cells","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"They are cholinergic and inhib","C":"The stapedius is supplied by a"}},{"id":"sdl22_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Use cranial nerve findings to localize lesions to the midbrain, pons, medulla, cavernous sinus, internal acoustic meatus, jugular foramen, or hypoglossal canal.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with a skull base tumor has numbness of the right lower lip, chin, lower teeth, and the skin over the right mandible, along with weakness of right jaw closure. Forehead and cheek sensation, eye movements, and facial expression are normal. At which opening is the lesion most likely located?","choices":{"A":"The superior orbital fissure","B":"The foramen rotundum","C":"The internal acoustic meatus","D":"The foramen ovale","E":"The jugular foramen"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve exits the cranial cavity through the foramen ovale together with the motor root of V, which supplies the masticatory muscles, the tensor tympani, the tensor veli palatini, the mylohyoid, and the anterior belly of the digastric. A lesion at the foramen ovale therefore causes numbness of the lower face and teeth with weakness of jaw closure on that side, sparing the forehead and cheek. The superior orbital fissure carries the ophthalmic division and the eye movement nerves. The foramen rotundum carries the maxillary division to the cheek. The internal acoustic meatus carries cranial nerves VII and VIII, and the jugular foramen carries IX, X, and XI.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sensory loss in a single trigeminal division localizes the lesion peripheral to the ganglion; adding jaw weakness points to V3 and the foramen ovale. COMLEX: On opening, the jaw deviates toward the weak side because the opposite pterygoids are unopposed.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — skull base relationships of the dural venous sinuses, contrasted here with the foramina that carry trigeminal divisions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Use cranial nerve findings to localize lesions to the midbrain, pons, medulla, cavernous sinus, internal acoustic meatus, jugular foramen, or hypoglossal canal.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman has a right cerebellopontine angle mass. Unlike the usual course of a vestibular schwannoma, her first symptom was right facial weakness, followed months later by right hearing loss and then facial pain. CT shows only slight erosion of the internal acoustic meatus without significant widening. Which lesion is most likely?","choices":{"A":"A vestibular schwannoma arising from the vestibular nerve within the meatus","B":"An epidermoid tumor arising from rests of epithelial cells at the angle","C":"A choroid plexus papilloma extending from the foramen of Luschka","D":"A pilocytic astrocytoma arising from the cerebellar hemisphere","E":"A meningioma arising from the margin of the internal acoustic meatus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Lesions at the cerebellopontine angle are mostly vestibular schwannomas, about 85%, followed by meningiomas, 5% to 10%, and epidermoid tumors, about 5%. Meningiomas may arise from the margins of the internal acoustic meatus and cause facial weakness early, followed later by hearing loss and trigeminal pain; they may erode or slightly enlarge the meatus, whereas significant widening is more typical of a vestibular schwannoma. A vestibular schwannoma usually begins with hearing loss and tinnitus. An epidermoid tumor involves V, VII, and VIII but without this meatal-margin sequence. Choroid plexus papillomas are mainly intraventricular tumors of childhood, and a pilocytic astrocytoma is an intra-axial cerebellar tumor of children.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cerebellopontine angle masses: vestibular schwannoma most common (hearing loss first, widened meatus), meningioma next (facial weakness may come first, dural tail). COMLEX: Bilateral vestibular schwannomas suggest neurofibromatosis type 2.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 6 — Applying cranial nerve anatomy to board-style tumor cases, the same skull base tumor framework used to reach this diagnosis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A vestibular schwannoma usuall","B":"An epidermoid tumor involves V"}},{"id":"sdl22_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Use cranial nerve findings to localize lesions to the midbrain, pons, medulla, cavernous sinus, internal acoustic meatus, jugular foramen, or hypoglossal canal.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with a skull base tumor develops hoarseness, dysphagia, an absent gag reflex, weakness of the ipsilateral trapezius and sternocleidomastoid, ipsilateral tongue weakness with deviation toward the lesion on protrusion, and an ipsilateral Horner syndrome with ptosis and miosis. Which term best describes this most extensive combined syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Vernet syndrome, involving CN IX, X, and XI only","B":"Collet-Sicard syndrome, involving CN IX, X, XI, and XII only","C":"Villaret syndrome, involving CN IX-XII and cervical sympathetics","D":"Isolated hypoglossal canal syndrome, involving CN XII alone","E":"Isolated cavernous sinus syndrome, involving CN III, IV, and VI"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Villaret, or retropharyngeal, syndrome is the most inclusive jugular foramen-region syndrome, combining CN IX, X, and XI deficits with adjacent hypoglossal canal involvement, CN XII, plus disruption of the nearby superior cervical sympathetic chain producing an ipsilateral Horner syndrome. Vernet syndrome is limited to CN IX, X, and XI without hypoglossal or sympathetic involvement. Collet-Sicard syndrome adds CN XII but lacks the Horner component that Villaret syndrome includes. Isolated hypoglossal canal or cavernous sinus syndromes involve entirely different, narrower nerve groups that do not match this full presentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vernet, Collet-Sicard, and Villaret syndromes form a testable escalating spectrum at the jugular foramen and hypoglossal canal, distinguished by exactly which structures are recruited. COMLEX: The presence of a Horner syndrome is the key discriminating feature that upgrades a Collet-Sicard picture to Villaret syndrome.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 8 — Medullary lesion patterns and nucleus ambiguus/hypoglossal nucleus involvement","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_bloom_q01","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Use cranial nerve findings to localize lesions to the midbrain, pons, medulla, cavernous sinus, internal acoustic meatus, jugular foramen, or hypoglossal canal.","batch":3,"stem":"A 49-year-old man with a mass just outside the skull base below the jugular foramen has loss of taste on the posterior third of the tongue, hoarseness and dysphagia, weakness of the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius, and tongue deviation toward the lesion. There is no Horner syndrome. This combination is best named the ___","choices":{"A":"Vernet syndrome","B":"Villaret syndrome","C":"Collet-Sicard syndrome","D":"Wallenberg syndrome","E":"Gradenigo syndrome"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Lesions immediately external to the jugular foramen, as in the Collet-Sicard syndrome, damage cranial nerves IX, X, and XI plus the hypoglossal nerve, because the hypoglossal canal is adjacent to the jugular foramen and the root of XII is recruited into the lesion. The Vernet syndrome, the syndrome of the jugular foramen itself, involves only IX, X, and XI. The Villaret, or retropharyngeal, syndrome adds sympathetic fibers of the superior cervical ganglion and an ipsilateral Horner syndrome, absent here. The Wallenberg syndrome is a lateral medullary infarct with crossed sensory loss. The Gradenigo syndrome involves the petrous apex, affecting the abducens and trigeminal nerves.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Jugular foramen syndromes: Vernet (IX-XI), Collet-Sicard (IX-XII), Villaret (IX-XII plus Horner). COMLEX: Glomus tumors, metastases, and skull base infections are typical causes of these syndromes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 -> Obj 5 — the same crossed-finding localization logic used for midbrain stroke syndromes, applied here to the full set of extra-axial cranial nerve exit sites","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl22_bloom_q02","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Use cranial nerve findings to localize lesions to the midbrain, pons, medulla, cavernous sinus, internal acoustic meatus, jugular foramen, or hypoglossal canal.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has sudden onset of right facial weakness, decreased hearing on the right, and unsteady gait, along with decreased pain and temperature sensation over the left trunk and limbs. The level most likely responsible for this combined pattern is the ___.","choices":{"A":"medullary tegmentum","B":"midbrain tegmentum","C":"cerebellopontine angle","D":"internal acoustic meatus","E":"pontine tegmentum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The ipsilateral CN VII and VIII deficits, facial weakness and hearing loss, paired with contralateral body pain and temperature loss, is a crossed pattern that only an intra-axial lesion can produce, since the spinothalamic tract is already crossed by the time it ascends through the brainstem; the CN VII and VIII nuclei and fascicles sit at the pontine level, making the pontine tegmentum the answer. A medullary tegmentum lesion would instead pair CN IX, X, or XII findings with the crossed sensory loss, not CN VII/VIII. A midbrain tegmentum lesion would produce a CN III or IV finding instead. A cerebellopontine angle mass and an internal acoustic meatus lesion are both extra-axial and compress CN VII and VIII locally, but neither can produce a contralateral body finding, since they do not interrupt the already-crossed spinothalamic tract; this is the key feature separating an intra-axial pontine stroke from an extra-axial CPA or IAM mass.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A contralateral body finding accompanying ipsilateral CN VII/VIII deficits is the single feature that rules out a CPA or IAM mass and rules in an intra-axial pontine lesion, a high-yield localization trap. COMLEX: AICA territory infarction is the vascular correlate of this exact crossed pontine pattern.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 -> Obj 5 — the same crossed-finding localization logic used for midbrain stroke syndromes, applied here to the full set of extra-axial and intra-axial cranial nerve exit sites","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl22_bloom_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Use cranial nerve findings to localize lesions to the midbrain, pons, medulla, cavernous sinus, internal acoustic meatus, jugular foramen, or hypoglossal canal.","batch":3,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman with a skull base metastasis has weakness and atrophy of the right half of the tongue with fasciculations, and the tongue deviates to the right on protrusion. Swallowing, voice, gag reflex, shoulder shrug, facial sensation, and limb strength are normal. The site most likely responsible for this isolated pattern is the ___","choices":{"A":"jugular foramen","B":"hypoglossal canal","C":"foramen magnum","D":"medial medulla","E":"internal capsule"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The hypoglossal nerve leaves the skull through the hypoglossal canal, which may also contain an emissary vein and a small meningeal branch of the ascending pharyngeal artery. A lesion there produces an isolated lower motor neuron tongue palsy: ipsilateral weakness, atrophy, and fasciculations with deviation toward the lesion. A jugular foramen lesion would add deficits of IX, X, and XI. A foramen magnum mass would typically involve the cervicomedullary junction and long tracts. A medial medullary lesion would add contralateral hemiparesis and lemniscal loss. An internal capsule lesion causes an upper motor neuron tongue weakness without atrophy, with deviation away from the lesion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: An isolated LMN tongue palsy with atrophy localizes to the hypoglossal nucleus, fascicles, or canal; adding IX to XI places the lesion just outside the jugular foramen. COMLEX: Isolated hypoglossal palsy calls for skull base imaging to look for metastasis or tumor.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 -> Obj 5 — the same localization logic used for midbrain syndromes, applied here to an isolated hypoglossal canal lesion","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl22_bloom_q04","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — List the brainstem levels associated with cranial nerves III-XII and identify the CN XI spinal accessory exception.","batch":3,"stem":"A 60-year-old man has sudden vertigo, hoarseness, and right-sided limb ataxia. Exam findings:\n- Decreased pain and temperature sensation over the right face\n- Decreased pain and temperature sensation over the left trunk and limbs\n- Absent gag reflex on the right\nGiven that the trigeminal nerve itself enters the brainstem at the pontine level, the ipsilateral facial pain and temperature loss in this patient localizes to a CN V nuclear column that extends caudally into the ___.","choices":{"A":"medullary tegmentum","B":"pontine tegmentum","C":"cervicomedullary junction","D":"midbrain tegmentum","E":"diencephalic tegmentum"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The spinal trigeminal nucleus, which carries pain and temperature sensation from the face, is unique among the CN V nuclei in that it extends far caudally from the pons down through the entire medulla, so a lateral medullary lesion can produce ipsilateral facial pain and temperature loss even though CN V itself exits the brainstem at the pons. The pontine tegmentum houses the principal sensory and motor nuclei of CN V, not the caudal extension responsible for this finding. A cervicomedullary junction lesion would extend the spinal trigeminal involvement further but would not by itself explain the combined vertigo, gag, and crossed body findings localizing squarely to the medulla. The midbrain tegmentum houses CN III and IV nuclei, unrelated to this presentation. The diencephalic tegmentum is not a brainstem level and plays no role in cranial nerve nuclear organization.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The spinal trigeminal nucleus spanning from the pons through the entire medulla is the key fact explaining ipsilateral facial sensory loss in lateral medullary syndrome despite CN V exiting at the pons. COMLEX: This same principle explains why facial numbness can occur with medullary, not just pontine, lesions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 8 — the same lateral medullary vessel territory tested here from the nuclear-level organization angle instead of the vascular angle","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)","whyNot":{"B":"The pontine tegmentum houses t"}},{"id":"sdl22_bloom_q05","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — List the brainstem levels associated with cranial nerves III-XII and identify the CN XI spinal accessory exception.","batch":3,"stem":"A newborn with a Chiari II malformation has cerebellar tonsillar herniation through the foramen magnum. Exam shows right shoulder droop and weakness turning the head to the left, with normal palate elevation, gag reflex, tongue protrusion, and swallowing. This isolated pattern indicates compression of a nerve that, unlike every other cranial nerve, has its motor neurons originating in the ___.","choices":{"A":"medullary tegmentum","B":"pontine tegmentum","C":"midbrain tegmentum","D":"cervical spinal cord","E":"cervicomedullary junction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"CN XI, the spinal accessory nerve, is the only cranial nerve whose motor neurons do not arise in the brainstem at all; instead they originate from the anterior horn of the cervical spinal cord, roughly C1 through C6, and the resulting rootlets ascend through the foramen magnum before exiting the skull via the jugular foramen. This is why isolated CN XI compression at the foramen magnum, as in Chiari malformation, spares CN IX, X, and XII, all of which have their motor cell bodies within the medulla itself. A medullary tegmentum lesion would be expected to also involve the nucleus ambiguus or hypoglossal nucleus, producing gag or tongue findings absent here. A pontine or midbrain tegmentum lesion would not explain a lower cranial nerve deficit at all. A cervicomedullary junction lesion could compress CN XI rootlets similarly, but would still typically implicate the brainstem-based nuclei of the other lower cranial nerves as well, unlike this purely spinal-origin nerve.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CN XI is the single spinal-cord-origin exception among all twelve cranial nerves, a fact tested repeatedly through Chiari malformation and foramen magnum mass vignettes. COMLEX: Sparing of CN IX, X, and XII with isolated trapezius and sternocleidomastoid weakness is the clinical signature of this anatomic exception.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 3 — the same jugular foramen exit site, here approached from the embryologic/spinal origin of CN XI rather than its combined syndrome with CN IX and X","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl22_bloom_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — List the brainstem levels associated with cranial nerves III-XII and identify the CN XI spinal accessory exception.","batch":3,"stem":"A 50-year-old man has acute-onset inability to abduct his left eye and left facial weakness involving the forehead and eye closure, together with right-sided arm and leg weakness. This crossed pattern localizing CN VI and CN VII nuclei and fascicles places the lesion at the ___.","choices":{"A":"midbrain tegmentum","B":"pontine tegmentum","C":"medullary tegmentum","D":"cervicomedullary junction","E":"diencephalic tegmentum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"CN VI and CN VII nuclei and fascicles both lie within the pontine tegmentum, and a lesion there interrupting the nearby corticospinal fibers before their medullary decussation produces this classic crossed pattern, ipsilateral CN VI and complete (LMN) CN VII palsy with contralateral hemiparesis. A midbrain tegmentum lesion would instead produce a CN III or IV finding, not CN VI or VII. A medullary tegmentum lesion would involve CN IX through XII rather than CN VI and VII. A cervicomedullary junction lesion would spare all of the pontine cranial nerves entirely. A diencephalic tegmentum lesion is not a brainstem level and would not produce any cranial nerve finding.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ipsilateral CN VI plus CN VII palsy with contralateral hemiparesis is the classic crossed pontine pattern, historically named Millard-Gubler syndrome, and is frequently tested without the eponym. COMLEX: Forehead involvement confirms a nuclear or fascicular (LMN) facial lesion, unlike the forehead-sparing pattern of a supranuclear corticobulbar lesion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 5 — the same crossed brainstem localization logic used for midbrain syndromes, applied here one level caudal at the pons","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl22_bloom_q07","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate somatic motor, branchial motor, parasympathetic, general sensory, special sensory, and visceral sensory components.","batch":3,"stem":"A 34-year-old man develops right-sided herpes zoster oticus with ear canal vesicles and severe otalgia, followed by facial paralysis and vertigo. The ear canal pain in this presentation is carried by fibers of CN VII classified as which functional component, distinguishing them from the special sensory taste fibers also carried by the same nerve?","choices":{"A":"special sensory","B":"branchial motor","C":"parasympathetic","D":"visceral sensory","E":"general sensory"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A small population of CN VII fibers carries general somatic sensation from the skin of the external auditory canal and concha, a general sensory (general somatic afferent) component distinct from the special sensory (special visceral afferent) taste fibers the nerve also carries from the anterior tongue. This is why herpes zoster oticus produces ear canal pain, Hitselberger's sign territory, in addition to any taste disturbance. Special sensory fibers carry taste, a separate functional category from cutaneous pain. Branchial motor fibers innervate the muscles of facial expression, a motor rather than sensory component. Parasympathetic fibers control lacrimal and salivary secretion, unrelated to cutaneous sensation. Visceral sensory fibers carry sensation from mucosal or glandular surfaces, not skin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CN VII carrying four distinct functional components, branchial motor, parasympathetic, special sensory taste, and general sensory skin sensation, in a single nerve is a frequently tested fact, especially the general sensory ear canal branch. COMLEX: Ear canal vesicles preceding facial paralysis is the classic Ramsay Hunt syndrome pattern tied to this general sensory territory.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 6 — the same varicella-zoster reactivation mechanism, approached here through the fiber-component classification rather than the geniculate ganglion localization","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)","whyNot":{"A":"Special sensory fibers carry t"}},{"id":"sdl22_bloom_q08","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate somatic motor, branchial motor, parasympathetic, general sensory, special sensory, and visceral sensory components.","batch":3,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with carotid sinus hypersensitivity develops recurrent syncope triggered by neck pressure. The afferent signal from the carotid sinus baroreceptors that triggers this reflex bradycardia travels centrally via CN IX fibers classified as which functional component?","choices":{"A":"special sensory","B":"general sensory","C":"visceral sensory","D":"parasympathetic efferent","E":"branchial motor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Carotid sinus baroreceptor afferents are visceral sensory (general visceral afferent) fibers of CN IX, carrying information from a vascular/visceral structure to the solitary nucleus, which then relays to the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus to trigger reflex bradycardia. Special sensory fibers carry taste, a distinct modality unrelated to baroreceptor signaling. General sensory fibers carry cutaneous sensation, such as from the posterior tongue and pharyngeal wall, not vascular baroreceptor input. Parasympathetic efferent fibers are the motor limb of this reflex arc, traveling in CN X to slow the heart, the opposite direction of the afferent signal being asked about here. Branchial motor fibers innervate stylopharyngeus, a motor rather than sensory component of CN IX.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Carotid sinus and carotid body afferents traveling as CN IX visceral sensory fibers, distinct from the CN X visceral sensory fibers relaying aortic arch information, is a frequently tested baroreceptor/chemoreceptor pairing. COMLEX: Carotid sinus massage exploits this exact visceral afferent pathway and can precipitate syncope or bradyarrhythmia in susceptible patients.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 7 — the same autonomic reflex-arc reasoning used there for descending sympathetic fibers, applied here to an ascending visceral sensory pathway instead","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl22_bloom_q09","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate somatic motor, branchial motor, parasympathetic, general sensory, special sensory, and visceral sensory components.","batch":3,"stem":"A 40-year-old man has an acute pontine lesion causing isolated inability to abduct the right eye, with normal facial movement. The motor fibers responsible for this deficit arise from a nucleus whose neurons are classified as which functional component, distinguishing them from the branchial motor fibers of the adjacent facial nucleus?","choices":{"A":"somatic motor","B":"branchial motor","C":"parasympathetic","D":"general sensory","E":"visceral sensory"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The abducens nucleus contains somatic motor (general somatic efferent) neurons that innervate the lateral rectus, a muscle derived from somites rather than pharyngeal arch mesoderm, distinguishing it functionally from the adjacent facial nucleus, whose branchial motor (special visceral efferent) neurons innervate arch-derived muscles of facial expression. Branchial motor fibers, as just noted, supply pharyngeal arch-derived muscles, not the lateral rectus. Parasympathetic fibers control smooth muscle and glands, not skeletal extraocular muscle. General sensory fibers carry cutaneous sensation, not motor output. Visceral sensory fibers carry sensation from mucosal or glandular surfaces, unrelated to extraocular motor function.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CN VI is one of only three cranial nerves, alongside CN III and IV, carrying true somatic motor fibers to extraocular muscles, a classification distinction tested against the branchial motor cranial nerves. COMLEX: Isolated abducens palsy without facial involvement confirms a lesion sparing the adjacent facial nucleus despite their close anatomic proximity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 1 — the same pontine level housing both nuclei, here distinguished by their embryologic and functional fiber classification rather than by localization alone","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)","whyNot":{"B":"Branchial motor fibers, as jus"}}],"23":[{"id":"sdl23_q01","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain pupillary light, corneal, jaw jerk, gag, and vestibulo-ocular reflex pathways.","batch":1,"stem":"Which pair of cranial nerves forms the afferent and efferent limbs of the pupillary light reflex?","choices":{"A":"CN V1 afferent limb, CN VII efferent limb via facial nucleus","B":"CN II afferent limb, CN III efferent limb via Edinger-Westphal","C":"CN VIII afferent limb, CN VI efferent limb via abducens nucleus","D":"CN IX afferent limb, CN X efferent limb via nucleus ambiguus","E":"CN V afferent limb, CN V efferent limb via mesencephalic nucleus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Light striking the retina travels via the optic nerve to the pretectal area, which projects bilaterally to the Edinger-Westphal nucleus; parasympathetic fibers then travel with CN III to the ciliary ganglion and constrict the pupil, producing both a direct and consensual response. CN V1/CN VII form the corneal reflex, CN VIII/CN VI relate to vestibulo-ocular circuitry, CN IX/CN X form the gag reflex, and CN V/CN V describes the jaw jerk, none of which govern pupillary constriction to light.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The bilateral pretectal projection is why shining light in one eye constricts both pupils, tested constantly as the direct-versus-consensual response. COMLEX: An afferent pupillary defect from an optic nerve lesion produces a paradoxical pupillary dilation on the swinging-flashlight test.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 2 — afferent sensory versus efferent motor functional components, now paired together as the two limbs of five discrete cranial nerve reflex arcs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"CN V1/CN VII form the corneal "}},{"id":"sdl23_q02","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain pupillary light, corneal, jaw jerk, gag, and vestibulo-ocular reflex pathways.","batch":1,"stem":"Touching the cornea normally produces a bilateral blink. Which cranial nerves carry the afferent and efferent limbs of this corneal reflex?","choices":{"A":"CN II afferent limb, CN III efferent limb via Edinger-Westphal","B":"CN IX afferent limb, CN X efferent limb via nucleus ambiguus","C":"CN V1 afferent limb, CN VII efferent limb via facial nucleus","D":"CN VIII afferent limb, CN VI efferent limb via abducens nucleus","E":"CN V3 afferent limb, CN XII efferent limb via hypoglossal nucleus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pain and touch receptors in the cornea send fibers via the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve to the trigeminal ganglion and spinal trigeminal nucleus, which sends collaterals bilaterally to the facial motor nucleus, whose CN VII fibers innervate the orbicularis oculi to produce a bilateral blink. CN II/CN III governs pupillary light, CN IX/CN X governs gag, CN VIII/CN VI relates to vestibulo-ocular movement, and CN V3/CN XII is not a recognized reflex pairing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A V1 (afferent) lesion abolishes both the direct and consensual blink when the affected cornea is touched, while touching the opposite cornea still produces a bilateral blink. COMLEX: A facial (efferent) lesion abolishes only the ipsilateral blink, whichever cornea is touched.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 2 — afferent sensory versus efferent motor functional components, now paired together as the two limbs of five discrete cranial nerve reflex arcs","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q03","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain pupillary light, corneal, jaw jerk, gag, and vestibulo-ocular reflex pathways.","batch":1,"stem":"The jaw jerk reflex is unusual among cranial nerve reflexes because both limbs travel on the same nerve. Which nerve and nucleus pair carries the afferent limb?","choices":{"A":"CN V afferent limb, cell bodies in the mesencephalic nucleus","B":"CN VII afferent limb, cell bodies in the geniculate ganglion","C":"CN IX afferent limb, cell bodies in the inferior ganglion","D":"CN X afferent limb, cell bodies in the nodose ganglion","E":"CN VIII afferent limb, cell bodies in Scarpa ganglion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Stretch receptors in the muscles of mastication send afferent fibers on the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve whose unusual primary sensory cell bodies sit within the mesencephalic nucleus rather than a peripheral ganglion; collaterals project bilaterally to the trigeminal motor nucleus, which supplies the efferent limb that closes the jaw. CN VII, CN IX, CN X, and CN VIII are not involved in this stretch-reflex arc, and their listed ganglia carry unrelated sensory modalities.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The trigeminal nerve is the only cranial nerve whose primary afferent cell bodies reside centrally, in the mesencephalic nucleus, rather than in a peripheral ganglion. COMLEX: A brisk jaw jerk suggests a bilateral upper motor neuron lesion above the pons, such as pseudobulbar palsy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 2 — afferent sensory versus efferent motor functional components, now paired together as the two limbs of five discrete cranial nerve reflex arcs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"CN VII, CN IX, CN X, and CN VI"}},{"id":"sdl23_q04","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Distinguish LMN facial palsy from UMN facial weakness and LMN hypoglossal lesions from UMN corticobulbar lesions.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient has weakness of the lower two-thirds of the left face but can still wrinkle the left forehead and close the left eye fully. Which pattern does this represent?","choices":{"A":"Lower motor neuron facial palsy involving the entire face","B":"Neuromuscular junction weakness fluctuating with fatigue","C":"Myopathic weakness affecting proximal limb-girdle muscles","D":"Upper motor neuron facial weakness sparing the forehead","E":"Bilateral facial diplegia sparing no facial region at all"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Corticonuclear fibers to the facial motor neurons supplying the upper face are bilateral, while those to the lower face are predominantly crossed, so a unilateral corticonuclear, upper motor neuron, lesion paralyzes only the contralateral lower face and spares forehead wrinkling and eye closure, exactly as described here. A lower motor neuron lesion of the facial nucleus or nerve instead paralyzes the entire ipsilateral face including the forehead. Neuromuscular junction and myopathic weakness patterns do not selectively spare the forehead, and bilateral diplegia would affect both sides.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Forehead sparing is the single highest-yield sign separating a central, upper motor neuron facial lesion from a peripheral, Bell palsy pattern. COMLEX: This sign is tested at bedside within seconds by asking the patient to raise both eyebrows.","crossRef":"📎 Upper motor neuron, lower motor neuron, neuromuscular junction, and myopathic weakness patterns, now applied specifically to the facial and hypoglossal nerves","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Distinguish LMN facial palsy from UMN facial weakness and LMN hypoglossal lesions from UMN corticobulbar lesions.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient cannot wrinkle the forehead, close the eye, or move the corner of the mouth on the entire right side of the face. Which localization best explains this full ipsilateral pattern?","choices":{"A":"Upper motor neuron lesion of the contralateral motor cortex","B":"Upper motor neuron lesion within the posterior limb of the internal capsule","C":"Bilateral upper motor neuron lesion producing pseudobulbar palsy","D":"Isolated corticonuclear fiber lesion sparing the facial nucleus","E":"Lower motor neuron lesion of the facial nucleus or nerve"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Because the facial nucleus itself, or the peripheral facial nerve, supplies every muscle of facial expression on one side without any contralateral cortical backup for the upper face, a lesion at or distal to the nucleus, a lower motor neuron lesion such as Bell palsy, paralyzes the entire ipsilateral face, forehead included. Upper motor neuron lesions of the cortex or internal capsule spare the forehead because of bilateral upper-face innervation, bilateral upper motor neuron pseudobulbar palsy produces bilateral rather than unilateral weakness, and an isolated corticonuclear lesion sparing the nucleus would not paralyze the whole face.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Complete unilateral facial paralysis including the forehead localizes to the facial nucleus or nerve itself, never to the cortex or internal capsule. COMLEX: Bell palsy is the classic idiopathic lower motor neuron facial palsy, often preceded by a viral prodrome and improving with corticosteroids started early.","crossRef":"📎 Upper motor neuron, lower motor neuron, neuromuscular junction, and myopathic weakness patterns, now applied specifically to the facial and hypoglossal nerves","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q06","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Distinguish LMN facial palsy from UMN facial weakness and LMN hypoglossal lesions from UMN corticobulbar lesions.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with a lesion confined to the right hypoglossal nerve root protrudes the tongue and it deviates to the right. A separate patient with a right internal capsule stroke protrudes the tongue and it deviates to the left. Which principle explains this difference?","choices":{"A":"LMN lesions deviate the tongue toward the lesion, UMN away","B":"LMN lesions deviate the tongue away, UMN lesions toward it","C":"Both LMN and UMN lesions deviate the tongue toward the lesion","D":"Both LMN and UMN lesions deviate the tongue away from the lesion","E":"Tongue deviation direction depends only on which muscle is weak"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A lower motor neuron hypoglossal nerve lesion weakens the ipsilateral genioglossus, and the intact contralateral genioglossus pushes the tongue toward the weak, lesioned side on protrusion, whereas an upper motor neuron corticonuclear lesion in the internal capsule interrupts predominantly crossed corticonuclear fibers to the contralateral hypoglossal nucleus, weakening the contralateral genioglossus and deviating the tongue away from the cortical lesion, toward the side opposite the stroke. The other listed options invert or eliminate this predictable directional rule.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tongue deviates toward a peripheral, LMN lesion but away from a central, UMN corticobulbar lesion, mirroring the logic of the Babinski response direction rule. First Aid mnemonic for the LMN pattern: the tongue deviates toward the side of the lesion as if it were licking its own wounds. COMLEX: A lesion of only the hypoglossal nerve root produces isolated tongue deviation without any other cranial nerve or long-tract findings.","crossRef":"📎 Upper motor neuron, lower motor neuron, neuromuscular junction, and myopathic weakness patterns, now applied specifically to the facial and hypoglossal nerves","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q07","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Apply cranial nerve anatomy to board-style cases involving stroke, aneurysm, tumor, demyelination, infection, and trauma.","batch":1,"stem":"An expanding posterior communicating artery aneurysm compresses the oculomotor nerve. Which reflex finding is expected to appear first, before any extraocular weakness develops?","choices":{"A":"Loss of the corneal reflex blink in the affected eye","B":"Loss of the gag reflex on the same side as the lesion","C":"Loss of the jaw jerk on the same side as the lesion","D":"Loss of the pupillary light reflex in the affected eye","E":"Loss of the vestibulo-ocular reflex on the same side"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The parasympathetic pupilloconstrictor fibers destined for the ciliary ganglion travel superficially along the outer surface of the oculomotor nerve, so an expanding extrinsic compressive lesion such as an aneurysm affects them first, abolishing the pupillary light reflex in that eye before the more centrally located somatic motor fibers to the extraocular muscles become symptomatic. The corneal, gag, jaw jerk, and vestibulo-ocular reflexes depend on entirely different cranial nerves not compressed by this lesion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pupil involvement preceding ophthalmoplegia is the classic sign of compressive CN III palsy, distinguishing it from pupil-sparing ischemic microvascular CN III palsy. COMLEX: Any acute pupil-involving CN III palsy warrants emergent vascular imaging to exclude a ruptured or expanding aneurysm.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 3 — cranial nerve lesion-localization landmarks, now applied to specific board-style etiologies: stroke, aneurysm, tumor, demyelination, infection, and trauma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q08","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Apply cranial nerve anatomy to board-style cases involving stroke, aneurysm, tumor, demyelination, infection, and trauma.","batch":1,"stem":"A young woman with multiple sclerosis attempts to look left. Her left eye abducts normally, but her right eye fails to adduct, and the abducting left eye shows nystagmus. Which structure has been demyelinated?","choices":{"A":"The right oculomotor nerve fascicles in the midbrain","B":"The right abducens nerve fascicles in the pons","C":"The right medial longitudinal fasciculus","D":"The right trigeminal motor nucleus in the pons","E":"The right facial nucleus at the pons-medulla junction"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Internuclear ophthalmoplegia results from a lesion of the medial longitudinal fasciculus, the tract that carries the signal from the contralateral abducens nucleus to the ipsilateral oculomotor subnucleus controlling the medial rectus; a right medial longitudinal fasciculus lesion prevents the right eye from adducting on attempted left gaze while the left eye still abducts, often with dissociated nystagmus. A right oculomotor nerve or nucleus lesion would impair adduction constantly, not only on conjugate gaze, and the abducens, trigeminal motor, and facial structures do not control this coordinated conjugate eye movement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Internuclear ophthalmoplegia from medial longitudinal fasciculus demyelination is a classic multiple sclerosis presentation, especially when bilateral in a young adult. COMLEX: Unilateral internuclear ophthalmoplegia in an older patient more often reflects a small vascular brainstem infarct rather than demyelination.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 3 — cranial nerve lesion-localization landmarks, now applied to specific board-style etiologies: stroke, aneurysm, tumor, demyelination, infection, and trauma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q09","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Apply cranial nerve anatomy to board-style cases involving stroke, aneurysm, tumor, demyelination, infection, and trauma.","batch":1,"stem":"A large supratentorial hematoma forces the uncus of the temporal lobe through the tentorial notch. Which finding is expected first as the herniating uncus compresses the oculomotor nerve?","choices":{"A":"Contralateral pupillary dilation from parasympathetic compression","B":"Ipsilateral pupillary dilation from parasympathetic compression","C":"Ipsilateral facial droop from facial nucleus compression","D":"Bilateral hearing loss from vestibulocochlear nerve compression","E":"Ipsilateral tongue deviation from hypoglossal nerve compression"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Uncal herniation pushes the medial temporal lobe against the ipsilateral oculomotor nerve, and because its superficial parasympathetic fibers are compressed before the deeper somatic motor fibers, the earliest sign is an ipsilateral dilated, poorly reactive pupil, followed later by a down-and-out eye and, if herniation progresses, contralateral hemiplegia from crus cerebri compression. Contralateral pupillary dilation, facial nucleus, vestibulocochlear, and hypoglossal nerve compression are not the structures directly compressed by early uncal herniation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A blown pupil ipsilateral to an expanding supratentorial mass is a neurosurgical emergency sign of uncal herniation, tested repeatedly. COMLEX: Rarely the contralateral crus cerebri is pushed against the tentorial edge, producing a falsely localizing ipsilateral hemiplegia called the Kernohan phenomenon.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 3 — cranial nerve lesion-localization landmarks, now applied to specific board-style etiologies: stroke, aneurysm, tumor, demyelination, infection, and trauma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Contralateral pupillary dilati","D":"Contralateral pupillary dilati","E":"Contralateral pupillary dilati"}},{"id":"sdl23_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain pupillary light, corneal, jaw jerk, gag, and vestibulo-ocular reflex pathways.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man recovering from a brainstem stroke has difficulty swallowing. On examination, touching the posterior pharyngeal wall on the right elicits no gag or elevation of the palate, while touching the left side produces a normal bilateral gag response. Which nerve pair explains the isolated right-sided afferent loss?","choices":{"A":"CN X carries the afferent limb, CN IX the efferent limb","B":"CN IX carries the afferent limb, CN X the efferent limb","C":"CN VII carries the afferent limb, CN X the efferent limb","D":"CN V carries the afferent limb, CN XII the efferent limb","E":"CN XI carries the afferent limb, CN X the efferent limb"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The glossopharyngeal nerve senses touch on the pharyngeal wall and carries the afferent limb of the gag reflex to the solitary nucleus and nucleus ambiguus, which then sends the efferent limb via the vagus nerve to the pharyngeal and palatal muscles bilaterally; a right glossopharyngeal lesion abolishes gag when the right side is touched, since the afferent signal never reaches the brainstem, even though touching the intact left side still produces a normal bilateral response through the left CN IX and both vagus nerves. CN X does not carry the sensory limb, and CN VII, CN V, and CN XI are not part of this reflex arc.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Testing each side of the pharynx separately localizes an isolated CN IX afferent lesion, since the efferent vagal limb and contralateral afferent pathway remain intact. COMLEX: Combined IX and X lesions at the jugular foramen abolish gag when either side is touched, unlike an isolated CN IX lesion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — Medullary cranial nerve nuclei, including nucleus ambiguus as the shared motor origin for CN IX and CN X","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"CN X does not carry the sensor","D":"CN X does not carry the sensor"}},{"id":"sdl23_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain pupillary light, corneal, jaw jerk, gag, and vestibulo-ocular reflex pathways.","batch":2,"stem":"A comatose 24-year-old man is examined with the oculocephalic maneuver. When his head is turned to the right, both eyes move conjugately to the left, maintaining fixation, indicating an intact brainstem reflex arc. Which structures form this vestibulo-ocular pathway?","choices":{"A":"CN VIII input relayed through the spinothalamic tract to CN III","B":"CN II input relayed through the pretectal area to CN III alone","C":"CN V input relayed through the trigeminal nucleus to CN VII","D":"CN VIII input relayed through the MLF to CN III and VI","E":"CN IX input relayed through the solitary nucleus to CN X"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Head rotation stimulates the semicircular canals, whose signals travel on the vestibular portion of CN VIII to the vestibular nuclei, which project via the medial longitudinal fasciculus to the abducens nucleus on one side and the oculomotor subnucleus on the other, driving conjugate contralateral eye movement that maintains visual fixation during head turning, an intact finding in this comatose patient. The spinothalamic tract, pretectal-to-CN III pupillary pathway, trigeminal-to-facial corneal pathway, and glossopharyngeal-to-vagal gag pathway are unrelated circuits that do not mediate this reflex.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Preserved oculocephalic, doll's eyes, reflexes indicate an intact brainstem from medulla through midbrain in a comatose patient, a key coma examination finding. COMLEX: Absent oculocephalic reflexes suggest severe brainstem dysfunction and prompt urgent neuroimaging and airway assessment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 1 — Brainstem levels of CN VIII at the pons-medulla junction and CN III/VI at the midbrain and pons","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"The spinothalamic tract, prete"}},{"id":"sdl23_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain pupillary light, corneal, jaw jerk, gag, and vestibulo-ocular reflex pathways.","batch":2,"stem":"A 33-year-old woman with optic neuritis undergoes the swinging-flashlight test. Shining light in her healthy left eye constricts both pupils normally, but swinging the light to the affected right eye causes both pupils to paradoxically dilate. Which limb of the pupillary light reflex has been damaged?","choices":{"A":"The efferent limb, the right oculomotor nerve carrying CN III output","B":"The efferent limb, the left oculomotor nerve carrying CN III output","C":"The afferent limb, the right trigeminal nerve carrying CN V1 input","D":"Both limbs equally, from bilateral Edinger-Westphal nucleus damage","E":"The afferent limb, the right optic nerve carrying CN II input"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This relative afferent pupillary defect, or Marcus Gunn pupil, occurs when a diseased optic nerve transmits a weaker light signal to the pretectal area than the healthy contralateral optic nerve does, so swinging the light into the damaged eye produces less pupilloconstrictor drive than the consensual input it was just receiving from the healthy eye, causing an apparent paradoxical dilation even though the efferent CN III pathway and pupillary muscles remain fully intact bilaterally. An efferent CN III lesion would instead prevent constriction whenever light enters either eye, and CN V1 and bilateral Edinger-Westphal damage do not explain this specific asymmetric afferent pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A relative afferent pupillary defect localizes disease to the optic nerve or severe retinal pathology, classically tested with optic neuritis in multiple sclerosis. COMLEX: The swinging-flashlight test isolates the afferent limb because the efferent pathway and pupillary muscles are being compared against themselves, not each other.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 3 — Interpreting cranial nerve examination findings, including pupillary testing, to localize lesions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Distinguish LMN facial palsy from UMN facial weakness and LMN hypoglossal lesions from UMN corticobulbar lesions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman from Connecticut develops weakness of both sides of her face over a week, with inability to close either eye fully or wrinkle either forehead. Three weeks earlier she had a hiking trip and an expanding red rash on her thigh. Limb strength and reflexes are normal. Which cause is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Bilateral strokes in the corona radiata sparing the forehead","B":"Guillain-Barre syndrome with ascending limb weakness","C":"Lyme disease from Borrelia burgdorferi infection","D":"Möbius syndrome from congenital nuclear agenesis","E":"Botulism with descending paralysis and dilated pupils"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Bilateral lower motor neuron facial weakness involving the forehead and eye closure on both sides is facial diplegia, which the course notes is characteristic of Lyme disease and is also seen in Guillain-Barre syndrome, botulism, diphtheria, and congenital conditions such as Möbius syndrome. After an expanding erythema migrans rash from a tick-endemic area, Lyme disease is most likely; First Aid lists facial nerve palsy, typically bilateral, among its features. Corona radiata strokes cause upper motor neuron weakness that spares the forehead. Guillain-Barre syndrome usually brings ascending limb weakness and lost reflexes, which are normal here. Möbius syndrome is congenital, and botulism causes descending paralysis with ptosis, dilated pupils, and bulbar weakness.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bilateral facial nerve palsy: think Lyme disease, Guillain-Barre syndrome, sarcoidosis, botulism; treat Lyme with doxycycline per First Aid. COMLEX: Ask about tick exposure and rash in any patient with facial palsy, especially when it is bilateral.","crossRef":"📖 Differentiating upper motor neuron, lower motor neuron, neuromuscular junction, and myopathic weakness patterns","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Guillain-Barre syndrome usuall","D":"Möbius syndrome is congenital,"}},{"id":"sdl23_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Distinguish LMN facial palsy from UMN facial weakness and LMN hypoglossal lesions from UMN corticobulbar lesions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman develops sudden right-sided facial droop involving the forehead, eye, and mouth, along with decreased tearing, loss of taste on the anterior right tongue, and hyperacusis. There is no limb weakness. Which site of injury best explains this full constellation of deficits?","choices":{"A":"The facial nerve proximal to the geniculate ganglion","B":"The facial motor cortex in the precentral gyrus","C":"The internal capsule posterior limb corticonuclear fibers","D":"The facial nerve distal to the stylomastoid foramen","E":"The facial nerve between the geniculate ganglion and chorda tympani"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A lesion proximal to the geniculate ganglion affects the entire facial nerve trunk before the greater petrosal, stapedial, and chorda tympani branches diverge, producing complete ipsilateral facial paralysis, decreased tearing from greater petrosal nerve involvement, decreased anterior tongue taste from chorda tympani involvement, and hyperacusis from stapedial nerve involvement, exactly the pattern described, consistent with a lower motor neuron Bell palsy at its most proximal site. Cortical and internal capsule lesions spare the forehead and do not affect taste or tearing. A lesion distal to the stylomastoid foramen would spare taste and tearing since those branches have already exited, and a lesion between the ganglion and chorda tympani would spare tearing but not taste.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The specific combination of hyperacusis, taste loss, and decreased tearing localizes a facial nerve lesion to a precise point along its intratemporal course, a favorite localization vignette. COMLEX: Idiopathic Bell palsy is thought to involve viral reactivation and nerve swelling within the narrow facial canal.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 2 — Pontine facial nerve nucleus and the intratemporal course of CN VII","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Cortical and internal capsule "}},{"id":"sdl23_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Distinguish LMN facial palsy from UMN facial weakness and LMN hypoglossal lesions from UMN corticobulbar lesions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 51-year-old man has months of irregular, involuntary, sometimes painful twitching of the muscles around his left eye and mouth, often triggered by voluntary facial movement. There is no weakness or sensory loss. MRI shows a vascular loop in contact with the root of the left facial nerve at the brainstem. Which vessel is the most likely culprit?","choices":{"A":"An aberrant branch of the superior cerebellar artery","B":"An aberrant branch of the anterior inferior cerebellar artery","C":"An aberrant branch of the posterior cerebral artery","D":"An aberrant branch of the middle meningeal artery","E":"An aberrant branch of the anterior choroidal artery"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hemifacial spasms are irregular and sometimes painful contractions of the facial muscles that may be triggered by voluntary movement; they may follow Bell palsy or result from compression of the facial nerve root by a lesion or vessel, and aberrant branches of the anterior inferior cerebellar artery are the likely candidates. The superior cerebellar artery is the classic vessel compressing the trigeminal root in trigeminal neuralgia. The posterior cerebral artery lies near the oculomotor nerve at the midbrain. The middle meningeal artery is extradural, and the anterior choroidal artery runs with the optic tract, far from the facial root.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Neurovascular compression: AICA loop on the facial root causes hemifacial spasm; SCA loop on the trigeminal root causes trigeminal neuralgia. COMLEX: Hemifacial spasm is an irritative facial nerve disorder without weakness, unlike Bell palsy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 7 — AICA territory and its course near the facial and vestibulocochlear nerves at the cerebellopontine angle","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Apply cranial nerve anatomy to board-style cases involving stroke, aneurysm, tumor, demyelination, infection, and trauma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman has brief, electric-shock attacks of severe pain over her right cheek and upper lip, triggered by chewing, talking, putting on makeup, or a breeze on her face. Sensory examination and facial strength are normal between attacks. MRI shows a vessel compressing the right trigeminal nerve root. Which vessel is most often responsible for this compression?","choices":{"A":"The anterior inferior cerebellar artery","B":"The posterior inferior cerebellar artery","C":"The basilar artery's paramedian branches","D":"The superior cerebellar artery","E":"The posterior communicating artery"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This is trigeminal neuralgia, or tic douloureux: severe, unexpected, lancinating pain restricted to one or more divisions of the trigeminal nerve, provoked from trigger zones by shaving, makeup, talking, chewing, eating, or a breeze. It is seen more often after age 35, most often involves the maxillary division, and may occur with multiple sclerosis, degeneration of the trigeminal ganglion, or vascular malformations; compression of the trigeminal root by aberrant vessels, usually branches of the superior cerebellar artery, is one known cause. AICA loops are associated with hemifacial spasm at the facial root. PICA branches lie near the lower cranial nerves at the medulla. Paramedian basilar branches penetrate the pons rather than looping on the trigeminal root, and the posterior communicating artery relates to the oculomotor nerve.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Trigeminal neuralgia: V2 most often, then V3, rarely V1; consider multiple sclerosis in a young patient or bilateral pain. COMLEX: Carbamazepine is first-line therapy per First Aid; microvascular decompression is an option for vascular compression.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 2 — the pontine trigeminal nuclei and the trigeminal root at its midpontine exit","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"AICA loops are associated with","B":"PICA branches lie near the low"}},{"id":"sdl23_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Apply cranial nerve anatomy to board-style cases involving stroke, aneurysm, tumor, demyelination, infection, and trauma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with diabetes develops severe ear pain followed by vesicles on the external ear and ear canal, complete ipsilateral facial paralysis including the forehead, and vertigo with hearing loss. Which mechanism best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Idiopathic viral-associated swelling within the facial canal","B":"Demyelination of the medial longitudinal fasciculus","C":"Varicella-zoster virus reactivation in the geniculate ganglion","D":"Vestibular schwannoma at the cerebellopontine angle","E":"Ischemic microvascular infarction of the facial nerve trunk"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ramsay Hunt syndrome results from reactivation of latent varicella-zoster virus within the geniculate ganglion, producing painful vesicles on the external ear or canal along with a complete lower motor neuron facial palsy and, because the inflammation spreads to the adjacent vestibulocochlear nerve within the internal acoustic meatus, vertigo and hearing loss, a combination not seen in idiopathic Bell palsy. Idiopathic Bell palsy lacks the characteristic vesicular rash. Medial longitudinal fasciculus demyelination causes internuclear ophthalmoplegia, not facial or vestibulocochlear findings. A vestibular schwannoma produces a slowly progressive rather than acute painful presentation, and ischemic microvascular facial nerve infarction is not a recognized entity that produces vesicles.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ramsay Hunt syndrome is the classic infectious mimicker of Bell palsy, distinguished by ear vesicles and vestibulocochlear involvement. COMLEX: Antiviral therapy plus corticosteroids started early improves recovery odds in Ramsay Hunt syndrome more than in idiopathic Bell palsy alone.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 3 — Interpreting cranial nerve findings to localize lesions across the orbit, skull base, or peripheral nerve","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Idiopathic Bell palsy lacks th"}},{"id":"sdl23_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Apply cranial nerve anatomy to board-style cases involving stroke, aneurysm, tumor, demyelination, infection, and trauma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old woman has six months of progressive right hearing loss and tinnitus. She now also reports absent corneal blink on the right when touched and mild right facial weakness. MRI shows an extra-axial mass at the cerebellopontine angle. Which sequence of reflex and cranial nerve involvement best explains her progression?","choices":{"A":"CN VIII first, then CN V afferent, then CN VII efferent","B":"CN VII compressed first, then CN VIII, then CN V afferent limb","C":"CN V afferent limb compressed first, then CN VIII, then CN VII","D":"CN III compressed first, then CN VIII, then CN VII efferent limb","E":"CN IX compressed first, then CN X, then CN VII efferent limb"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A vestibular schwannoma at the cerebellopontine angle and internal acoustic meatus first compresses CN VIII, causing hearing loss and tinnitus, then as it enlarges compresses the adjacent trigeminal root, abolishing the corneal reflex in both eyes when the right cornea is touched (afferent loss), while touching the left cornea still produces a blink, and finally compresses CN VII, producing facial weakness and loss of the efferent limb as well. CN VII being compressed first, CN V being compressed before CN VIII, CN III involvement, and CN IX/CN X involvement do not match the expected anatomic progression at this specific location.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The stepwise CN VIII-then-V-then-VII progression of a vestibular schwannoma, including loss of the corneal reflex afferent limb, is a classic cerebellopontine angle tumor vignette. COMLEX: Bilateral vestibular schwannomas at this same location strongly suggest neurofibromatosis type 2.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 3 — Localizing lesions to the internal acoustic meatus, transmitting CN VII and CN VIII","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"CN VII being compressed first,","C":"CN VII being compressed first,"}},{"id":"sdl23_bloom_q01","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Apply cranial nerve anatomy to board-style cases involving stroke, aneurysm, tumor, demyelination, infection, and trauma.","batch":3,"stem":"A 39-year-old woman develops complete right facial paralysis involving the forehead, eye, and mouth, along with hyperacusis on the right and loss of taste over the anterior two-thirds of the tongue. Tearing is normal bilaterally, and facial sensation and hearing are intact. This patient's facial nerve lesion is most likely located ___.","choices":{"A":"proximal to the geniculate ganglion","B":"distal to the chorda tympani","C":"within the internal acoustic meatus","D":"between the geniculate ganglion and the stapedius nerve","E":"distal to the stylomastoid foramen"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Normal tearing means the greater petrosal branch, which exits at the geniculate ganglion, is intact, so the lesion must be distal to the ganglion. Hyperacusis and taste loss mean the stapedius branch and chorda tympani are both affected, so the lesion must be proximal to the nerve to stapedius, placing it between the geniculate ganglion and that branch. A lesion proximal to the geniculate ganglion would also impair tearing, which is preserved here. A lesion distal to the chorda tympani would spare taste, which is lost here. A lesion within the internal acoustic meatus lies proximal to the geniculate ganglion and would impair tearing as well as cause CN VIII findings, neither present. A lesion distal to the stylomastoid foramen would spare hyperacusis and taste entirely, since both branches exit before that point.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sequential loss of greater petrosal, stapedius, and chorda tympani function as a facial nerve lesion moves distally is a classic branch-point localization ladder tested on exams. COMLEX: This exact branch-by-branch reasoning is used clinically to stage the severity and site of Bell palsy and traumatic facial nerve injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 -> Obj 5 — board-style integrated cranial nerve vignettes built on the same crossed and branch-point localization skills used for midbrain syndromes","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)","whyNot":{"A":"A lesion proximal to the genic","B":"A lesion distal to the chorda "}},{"id":"sdl23_bloom_q02","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Apply cranial nerve anatomy to board-style cases involving stroke, aneurysm, tumor, demyelination, infection, and trauma.","batch":3,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman with multiple sclerosis is examined. On attempted right gaze, neither eye moves past midline. On attempted left gaze, the right eye fails to adduct past midline while the left eye abducts fully but shows nystagmus. Convergence is intact. This pattern most likely results from a lesion involving the ___.","choices":{"A":"right pontine tegmentum","B":"right midbrain tegmentum","C":"bilateral MLF and PPRF","D":"right abducens nerve","E":"right cerebellar peduncle"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Absent movement of both eyes toward the right reflects loss of the right PPRF and abducens nucleus, the horizontal gaze center, while the failure of the right eye to adduct on left gaze reflects a right MLF lesion, an internuclear ophthalmoplegia; together these two adjacent structures sit in the right dorsal pontine tegmentum, and their combined loss with only contralateral abduction and nystagmus preserved is a single localized lesion there. The midbrain tegmentum houses CN III and IV circuitry, not the horizontal gaze apparatus. A bilateral MLF and PPRF lesion would cause bilateral adduction and gaze failure on both sides, not the unilateral pattern seen here. An isolated right abducens nerve lesion would cause failure of right eye abduction, not the pattern described. The cerebellar peduncle governs coordination, not conjugate gaze, and its lesion would not produce this eye movement pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Combined ipsilateral gaze palsy plus INO, leaving only one functioning eye movement, contralateral abduction with nystagmus, is a demyelinating disease hallmark and a favorite MS vignette. COMLEX: This distinguishes isolated INO, MLF alone, from the more extensive combined PPRF-plus-MLF lesion, a frequently tested distinction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 -> Obj 5 — board-style integrated cranial nerve vignettes built on the same crossed and branch-point localization skills used for midbrain syndromes","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl23_bloom_q03","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Distinguish LMN facial palsy from UMN facial weakness and LMN hypoglossal lesions from UMN corticobulbar lesions.","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old man has progressive dysarthria and dysphagia. Exam shows a brisk jaw jerk reflex, a spastic tongue with slow deliberate movements but no atrophy or fasciculations, and bilateral facial weakness sparing the forehead. This pattern most likely results from a lesion of the ___.","choices":{"A":"trigeminal motor nucleus","B":"facial nucleus","C":"corticobulbar tracts","D":"nucleus ambiguus","E":"hypoglossal nucleus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A brisk jaw jerk indicates loss of descending inhibition of the trigeminal motor reflex arc, and spastic tongue and facial weakness without atrophy or fasciculations indicate upper motor neuron, not nuclear, dysfunction; because both lower facial regions are affected bilaterally with forehead sparing, and bulbar muscles are bilaterally represented in the cortex, the lesion must involve the corticobulbar tracts bilaterally, producing pseudobulbar palsy. A trigeminal motor nucleus lesion would cause a flaccid, hyporeflexic jaw with deviation on opening, not hyperreflexia. A facial nucleus lesion would cause LMN facial weakness including the forehead, not sparing it. Nucleus ambiguus damage causes flaccid palatal and vocal cord weakness without hyperreflexia elsewhere. A hypoglossal nucleus lesion causes a flaccid, atrophic, fasciculating tongue that deviates toward the lesion, the opposite of the spastic tongue described here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pseudobulbar palsy, bilateral corticobulbar tract loss with hyperreflexic bulbar signs, versus bulbar palsy, nuclear or LMN loss with atrophy and fasciculations, is one of the most consistently tested UMN versus LMN cranial nerve distinctions. COMLEX: A brisk jaw jerk is the single reflex finding most specific for a corticobulbar, not nuclear, process.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 3 — the same brainstem-vs-extra-axial localization logic, applied here to distinguish upper from lower motor neuron cranial nerve lesions","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl23_bloom_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain pupillary light, corneal, jaw jerk, gag, and vestibulo-ocular reflex pathways.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has a fixed, dilated left pupil after rupture of a posterior communicating artery aneurysm. Shining light in the right eye produces normal constriction of the right pupil but no constriction of the left. Shining light in the left eye produces normal constriction of the right pupil but none of the left. This pattern indicates a defect in the ___ limb of the pupillary light reflex.","choices":{"A":"afferent","B":"efferent","C":"consensual","D":"accommodative","E":"sympathetic"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because the right eye's direct and consensual responses are both normal, the afferent limb via the right optic nerve and the efferent limb via the right oculomotor nerve are intact; the isolated failure of only the left pupil to constrict under any lighting condition means the defect must be efferent, in the left oculomotor nerve's parasympathetic fibers, since an afferent (left optic nerve) defect would still allow the left pupil to constrict consensually when light is shone in the right eye. Afferent defects, by contrast, are ruled out here because the left pupil fails to respond even when the right eye's optic nerve is transmitting proper input. Consensual is not a limb of the reflex but rather the response being tested, not the defect. Accommodative and sympathetic pathways govern near-response and pupillary dilation, respectively, neither tested by this light-reflex maneuver.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Comparing the consensual response with the direct response, not just the direct response alone, is the key maneuver that separates an afferent from an efferent pupillary defect. COMLEX: A dilated, unreactive pupil from PCoA aneurysm compression is efferent, CN III parasympathetic, not afferent, distinguishing it from optic neuropathy causing a relative afferent pupillary defect.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 5 — the same CN III/pupillary anatomy tested there via crossed hemiparesis, applied here to reflex-arc limb localization instead","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl23_bloom_q05","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain pupillary light, corneal, jaw jerk, gag, and vestibulo-ocular reflex pathways.","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has a complete lesion of the left trigeminal root. Touching his left cornea produces no blink in either eye. When the examiner touches his right cornea, the expected response is ___","choices":{"A":"no blink in either eye","B":"a blink of the right eye but not the left","C":"a blink of the left eye but not the right","D":"a delayed blink after several seconds","E":"a blink of both eyes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The afferent limb of the corneal reflex travels in the ophthalmic division of V to the spinal trigeminal nucleus, which connects bilaterally with the facial motor nuclei; the efferent limb is the facial nerve. With a left trigeminal root lesion, touching the left cornea produces no blink on either side because the afferent limb is lost. Touching the right cornea uses the intact right afferent limb and both intact facial nerves, so both eyes blink, including the eye on the side of the root lesion. A blink of one eye without the other would indicate a facial nerve lesion on the side that fails to blink. There is no basis for a delayed response.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Corneal reflex: afferent V1, efferent VII, bilateral response; a unilateral afferent lesion abolishes both blinks only when the affected cornea is touched. COMLEX: Comparing direct and consensual blinks separates trigeminal from facial nerve lesions at the bedside.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — the same CN V/VII reflex-pathway anatomy, extended here to the bilateral corneal blink after a trigeminal root lesion","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)","whyNot":{"A":"Touching the right cornea uses","B":"A blink of one eye without the","C":"A blink of one eye without the"}},{"id":"sdl23_bloom_q06","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain pupillary light, corneal, jaw jerk, gag, and vestibulo-ocular reflex pathways.","batch":3,"stem":"A comatose 55-year-old man undergoes oculocephalic (doll's eyes) testing. When his head is turned to the right, the right eye adducts appropriately, but the left eye fails to abduct past the midline. Corneal reflexes are intact bilaterally. This abnormal vestibulo-ocular response indicates that the efferent limb defect lies in the left ___.","choices":{"A":"oculomotor nucleus","B":"trochlear nerve","C":"abducens nerve or fascicle","D":"vestibular nucleus","E":"abducens nucleus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Turning the head to the right drives both eyes to the left: left abducens motor neurons abduct the left eye, while left abducens internuclear neurons project through the right MLF to the right oculomotor nucleus to adduct the right eye. Because the right eye adducts normally, the left abducens nucleus, including its internuclear neurons, must be intact, so isolated failure of left abduction localizes to the left abducens nerve or its fascicle. A lesion of the abducens nucleus itself would also abolish right-eye adduction, producing a left horizontal gaze palsy. The oculomotor nucleus is working, since the right eye adducts; the trochlear nerve supplies the superior oblique and has no role in horizontal gaze; and a vestibular nucleus lesion would disturb the movement of both eyes rather than one lateral rectus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Selective loss of abduction with preserved contralateral adduction on oculocephalic testing localizes to the abducens nerve, whereas an abducens nucleus lesion produces an ipsilateral horizontal gaze palsy. COMLEX: This same VOR circuit is exploited clinically by both the doll's eyes maneuver and cold caloric testing to assess brainstem integrity in coma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 6 — the same CN VIII-to-MLF-to-CN III/VI reflex circuitry, here isolated to its efferent abducens component rather than the general pathway","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)","whyNot":{"A":"The oculomotor nucleus is work","E":"A lesion of the abducens nucle"}},{"id":"sdl23_bloom_q07","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Distinguish LMN facial palsy from UMN facial weakness and LMN hypoglossal lesions from UMN corticobulbar lesions.","batch":3,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman has sudden onset of right lower facial weakness with forehead sparing, right-sided tongue deviation on protrusion without atrophy or fasciculations, and right arm and leg weakness. Palatal elevation and gag reflex are normal. This combined pattern of upper motor neuron facial and hypoglossal involvement together with hemiparesis most likely results from a lesion of the ___.","choices":{"A":"right corona radiata","B":"left facial nucleus","C":"left hypoglossal nucleus","D":"left corticobulbar tract","E":"left nucleus ambiguus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Because bulbar and facial motor neurons receive predominantly crossed corticobulbar input, a single lesion of the left corticobulbar tract produces contralateral (right) lower facial weakness with forehead sparing and contralateral tongue deviation on protrusion without atrophy or fasciculations, together with the accompanying right hemiparesis from adjacent corticospinal fiber involvement; the intact gag and palate confirm the lower cranial nerve nuclei themselves are undamaged. A right corona radiata lesion would produce this UMN pattern on the left side, not the right. A left facial nerve nucleus lesion would cause LMN facial weakness involving the forehead, not sparing it. A left hypoglossal nucleus lesion would cause LMN tongue weakness with atrophy and fasciculations, and the tongue would deviate toward the left, the side of the lesion, rather than toward the right. A left nucleus ambiguus lesion would impair palatal and gag function, both preserved here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Combined UMN facial and hypoglossal findings alongside hemiparesis, all sparing the forehead and sparing atrophy or fasciculations, localizes to the corticobulbar tract and is a frequently tested triad. COMLEX: The preserved gag and palate function is the detail that rules out any lower cranial nerve nuclear involvement in this vignette.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 1 — the same corticobulbar fiber pathway traced here through its combined facial and hypoglossal targets rather than its brainstem level of origin","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl23_bloom_q08","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Distinguish LMN facial palsy from UMN facial weakness and LMN hypoglossal lesions from UMN corticobulbar lesions.","batch":3,"stem":"A 61-year-old man has progressive dysarthria and dysphagia. Exam shows tongue atrophy and fasciculations with deviation toward the right on protrusion, together with a brisk jaw jerk and brisk gag reflex. This combination indicates simultaneous involvement of the ___.","choices":{"A":"LMN and UMN pathways","B":"LMN pathways only","C":"UMN pathways only","D":"corticospinal tracts only","E":"cerebellar pathways only"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Tongue atrophy, fasciculations, and deviation toward the lesion are hallmark lower motor neuron (hypoglossal nucleus/nerve) findings, while a brisk jaw jerk and brisk gag reflex reflect loss of descending corticobulbar inhibition, an upper motor neuron sign; their coexistence in the same patient is the classic mixed bulbar picture of amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, in which both the corticobulbar tracts and the brainstem motor nuclei degenerate. LMN pathways alone would produce atrophy and fasciculations but a normal or reduced, not brisk, jaw jerk and gag reflex. UMN pathways alone would produce hyperreflexia without the tongue atrophy and fasciculations described here. Corticospinal tracts alone govern limb strength, not the bulbar/hypoglossal findings central to this vignette. Cerebellar pathways govern coordination, not reflex briskness or muscle atrophy, and would not explain either finding.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Simultaneous UMN and LMN bulbar signs in the same patient is the single most distinctive diagnostic clue for ALS and a frequently tested exam finding. COMLEX: Fasciculations plus a pathologically brisk jaw jerk together should immediately raise concern for a combined upper and lower motor neuron degenerative process.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 6 — the same UMN corticobulbar pathway tested in the pseudobulbar palsy vignette, here combined with concurrent LMN degeneration to build the ALS mixed pattern","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)","whyNot":{"B":"LMN pathways alone would produ","C":"UMN pathways alone would produ","D":"Corticospinal tracts alone gov"}},{"id":"sdl23_bloom_q09","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Apply cranial nerve anatomy to board-style cases involving stroke, aneurysm, tumor, demyelination, infection, and trauma.","batch":3,"stem":"A 24-year-old man involved in a motorcycle collision has immediate complete right facial paralysis, sensorineural hearing loss, and vertigo, with blood visible behind an intact tympanic membrane. CT shows the fracture line running perpendicular to the long axis of the petrous ridge. This fracture pattern is best classified as a ___.","choices":{"A":"longitudinal fracture","B":"mixed fracture","C":"basilar fracture","D":"oblique fracture","E":"transverse fracture"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Transverse temporal bone fractures run perpendicular to the petrous ridge and characteristically cross the labyrinth and fallopian canal, producing immediate, complete facial paralysis and sensorineural hearing loss with vertigo, exactly as seen here; the tympanic membrane often remains intact, with blood accumulating behind it (hemotympanum). Longitudinal fractures run parallel to the petrous ridge, more commonly sparing the labyrinth and facial nerve initially, and typically produce conductive hearing loss with a torn tympanic membrane rather than this pattern. A mixed fracture would combine features of both patterns rather than presenting this pure transverse picture. A basilar fracture is a broader category describing any skull base fracture and is not specific to this perpendicular petrous orientation. An oblique fracture is not a standard temporal bone fracture classification and does not correspond to either recognized pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Transverse temporal bone fractures causing immediate complete facial paralysis and sensorineural hearing loss, versus longitudinal fractures causing delayed partial facial weakness and conductive hearing loss, is a classic trauma vignette pairing. COMLEX: Intact tympanic membrane with hemotympanum, rather than a visibly torn drum, supports the transverse pattern over the longitudinal one.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 3 — the same internal acoustic meatus CN VII/VIII anatomy, applied here to a traumatic rather than neoplastic mechanism of injury","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"}],"24":[{"id":"sdl24_q01","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Predict deficits from lesions affecting the corticospinal tract, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic tract, sympathetic fibers, cerebellar peduncles, and cranial nerve nuclei.","batch":1,"stem":"A lesion of the corticospinal tract within the brainstem, located rostral to the pyramidal decussation, produces which pattern of weakness?","choices":{"A":"Ipsilateral spastic hemiparesis of the arm and leg","B":"Contralateral spastic hemiparesis of the arm and leg","C":"Contralateral flaccid paralysis with absent deep reflexes","D":"Bilateral spastic weakness that spares the ipsilateral arm","E":"Ipsilateral flaccid weakness with fasciculations and atrophy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Corticospinal fibers descend ipsilaterally through the brainstem basis before crossing at the caudal medullary pyramidal decussation, so any brainstem lesion rostral to that crossing point produces an upper motor neuron pattern, spastic weakness with increased tone and hyperreflexia, on the side of the body contralateral to the lesion. Ipsilateral weakness would instead require a lesion caudal to the decussation, flaccid paralysis with absent reflexes describes an acute spinal shock or lower motor neuron pattern rather than a brainstem corticospinal lesion, bilateral sparing of one limb does not match the expected diffuse contralateral distribution, and fasciculations with atrophy are lower motor neuron findings not produced by a corticospinal tract lesion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Corticospinal tract lesions above the pyramidal decussation always produce contralateral, not ipsilateral, upper motor neuron weakness, a rule tested constantly across brainstem vignettes. COMLEX: Hyperreflexia, spasticity, and an upgoing Babinski response together confirm an upper motor neuron pattern at the bedside.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — localizing the corticospinal tract, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic tract, and sympathetic fibers within medullary cross-sections, now extended to predict the clinical deficit produced by a lesion of each individual structure","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Ipsilateral weakness would ins"}},{"id":"sdl24_q02","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Predict deficits from lesions affecting the corticospinal tract, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic tract, sympathetic fibers, cerebellar peduncles, and cranial nerve nuclei.","batch":1,"stem":"A discrete brainstem lesion damages only the medial lemniscus. Which sensory pattern is expected on examination?","choices":{"A":"Contralateral loss of vibration, proprioception, and fine touch","B":"Contralateral loss of pain, temperature, and crude touch sensation","C":"Ipsilateral loss of vibration and proprioception in that limb","D":"Bilateral loss of vibration sense sparing pain and temperature","E":"Contralateral loss of both discriminative touch and thermal sense"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The medial lemniscus carries second-order fibers of the dorsal column-medial lemniscal pathway, which cross in the caudal medulla as internal arcuate fibers, so a lesion of the medial lemniscus above that crossing produces contralateral loss of vibration, conscious proprioception, and fine discriminative touch, with pain and temperature sensation fully preserved. Loss of pain, temperature, and crude touch instead describes a spinothalamic, not medial lemniscal, lesion, an ipsilateral pattern would require damage caudal to the sensory decussation rather than within the medial lemniscus itself, a bilateral pattern does not match a discrete unilateral lesion, and combining discriminative touch loss with thermal loss would require damage to both pathways rather than the medial lemniscus alone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A dissociated sensory pattern, lost vibration and proprioception with preserved pain and temperature, localizes precisely to the medial lemniscus and is a favorite brainstem localization pearl. COMLEX: Romberg testing is markedly positive when the medial lemniscal pathway is damaged, since proprioceptive input is lost while visual compensation is still available.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — localizing the corticospinal tract, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic tract, and sympathetic fibers within medullary cross-sections, now extended to predict the clinical deficit produced by a lesion of each individual structure","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q03","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Predict deficits from lesions affecting the corticospinal tract, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic tract, sympathetic fibers, cerebellar peduncles, and cranial nerve nuclei.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with multiple sclerosis has a plaque in the right medial longitudinal fasciculus that has extended into the adjacent right trochlear nucleus. On attempted gaze to the left, the right eye fails to adduct. Which additional eye movement deficit is expected from the trochlear nucleus involvement?","choices":{"A":"Weakness of the right superior oblique muscle","B":"Weakness of the right lateral rectus muscle","C":"Weakness of the left lateral rectus muscle","D":"Weakness of the left superior oblique muscle","E":"Weakness of both inferior rectus muscles"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The trochlear nerve is the only motor cranial nerve formed entirely by axons that cross the midline before exiting, so each trochlear nucleus innervates the contralateral superior oblique muscle. A right MLF lesion that extends into the right trochlear nucleus therefore causes a right internuclear ophthalmoplegia, with failure of right eye adduction on left gaze, plus paralysis of the left superior oblique, so the left eye cannot rotate downward and outward. A right superior oblique palsy would follow a lesion of the right trochlear nerve root after it exits, not of the right nucleus. Lateral rectus weakness would require abducens involvement, and bilateral inferior rectus weakness would require oculomotor involvement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Trochlear nucleus lesion causes contralateral superior oblique palsy; trochlear nerve lesion after the crossing causes ipsilateral palsy. COMLEX: Superior oblique palsy produces vertical diplopia, worst when looking down, as in reading or descending stairs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — localizing tracts within brainstem cross-sections, now extended to predict the combined MLF and trochlear nucleus deficit","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A right superior oblique palsy"}},{"id":"sdl24_q04","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize vascular syndromes involving paramedian, circumferential, and cerebellar/brainstem arteries.","batch":1,"stem":"Paramedian perforating branches of the vertebrobasilar system are best characterized as supplying which brainstem territory?","choices":{"A":"Lateral tegmental structures and the cranial nerve sensory nuclei","B":"Only the cerebellar cortex and its three peduncles","C":"The dorsal columns of the spinal cord above the foramen magnum","D":"Purely the meninges and dural venous sinuses of the posterior fossa","E":"Medial structures near the midline, including motor pathways"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Paramedian arteries are short penetrating branches that arise close to the midline and dive directly into the adjacent medial brainstem, supplying the corticospinal tract, medial lemniscus, and medially situated cranial nerve nuclei, which is why their occlusion produces medial syndromes dominated by contralateral long-tract motor and dorsal column findings. Lateral tegmental and sensory nuclei are instead supplied by circumferential rather than paramedian branches, cerebellar cortex and peduncles are supplied by the dedicated cerebellar arteries rather than paramedian perforators, the spinal cord dorsal columns lie outside the brainstem entirely, and the meninges and dural sinuses are not the target of these small penetrating parenchymal vessels.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Paramedian versus circumferential artery territory is the single organizing principle behind every medial-versus-lateral brainstem syndrome pair tested on boards. COMLEX: Recognizing a medial, motor-predominant deficit pattern should immediately suggest paramedian rather than circumferential vessel involvement.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 6 — distinguishing medial from lateral medullary syndrome, the foundational paramedian-versus-circumferential vascular contrast now extended across the full medulla, pons, and midbrain","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Lateral tegmental and sensory "}},{"id":"sdl24_q05","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize vascular syndromes involving paramedian, circumferential, and cerebellar/brainstem arteries.","batch":1,"stem":"Occlusion of the posterior inferior cerebellar artery classically damages which brainstem territory?","choices":{"A":"The medial medulla, sparing the lateral tegmentum entirely","B":"The lateral pons, sparing the medullary structures","C":"The lateral medulla, sparing the medial pyramid and lemniscus","D":"The rostral midbrain, sparing the cerebral peduncle","E":"The medial pons, sparing the lateral cerebellar peduncle"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The posterior inferior cerebellar artery is a long circumferential vessel that supplies the lateral medulla, including the spinal trigeminal nucleus, nucleus ambiguus, anterolateral system, descending sympathetic fibers, and inferior cerebellar peduncle, while sparing the paramedian pyramid and medial lemniscus supplied instead by the anterior spinal artery. The medial medulla is spared by PICA, not damaged by it, the lateral pons is a separate, more rostral territory supplied by AICA rather than PICA, the rostral midbrain tegmentum is supplied by branches of the posterior cerebral and superior cerebellar arteries far from PICA territory, and the medial pons is again outside the medullary territory PICA actually supplies.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PICA territory is the anatomic basis of lateral medullary, Wallenberg, syndrome and is one of the most frequently tested vascular territories on Step 1. First Aid mnemonic: don't pick a PICA lame horse that can't eat, tying PICA to the hoarseness and dysphagia of lateral medullary syndrome. COMLEX: PICA occlusion classically spares limb strength, since the corticospinal fibers in the pyramid lie outside its supplied territory.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 6 — distinguishing medial from lateral medullary syndrome, the foundational paramedian-versus-circumferential vascular contrast now extended across the full medulla, pons, and midbrain","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q06","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize vascular syndromes involving paramedian, circumferential, and cerebellar/brainstem arteries.","batch":1,"stem":"The cerebellar arteries, PICA, AICA, and SCA, are unified by which shared anatomic feature that distinguishes them from paramedian perforators?","choices":{"A":"Each supplies both cerebellar tissue and adjacent tegmentum","B":"Each supplies only cerebellar cortex without any brainstem tissue","C":"Each arises directly from the internal carotid artery bilaterally","D":"Each supplies exclusively the medial brainstem and pyramidal tract","E":"Each bypasses the brainstem entirely to reach the cerebellum alone"},"correct":"A","explanation":"PICA, AICA, and SCA are circumferential branches of the vertebrobasilar system that wrap around the brainstem to reach the cerebellum, and along their course each also sends penetrating branches into the adjacent lateral brainstem tegmentum, which is why occlusion of any one of them produces a combined picture of cerebellar signs plus lateral tegmental cranial nerve and long-tract findings at that specific level. Supplying only cerebellar cortex without brainstem tissue ignores their tegmental branches, none of the three arises from the internal carotid system, supplying exclusively the medial brainstem and pyramidal tract describes paramedian rather than cerebellar arteries, and bypassing the brainstem entirely is incorrect since their course along the brainstem surface is exactly what produces combined syndromes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Recognizing that PICA, AICA, and SCA each supply both cerebellum and adjacent lateral tegmentum explains why their occlusion always produces combined cerebellar-plus-cranial-nerve deficits rather than pure ataxia. COMLEX: This shared anatomic logic lets a clinician predict the level of a cerebellar stroke from the accompanying cranial nerve findings alone.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 6 — distinguishing medial from lateral medullary syndrome, the foundational paramedian-versus-circumferential vascular contrast now extended across the full medulla, pons, and midbrain","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q07","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Predict deficits from lesions affecting the corticospinal tract, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic tract, sympathetic fibers, cerebellar peduncles, and cranial nerve nuclei.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man has sudden onset of decreased pain and temperature sensation over the left trunk and limbs, with vibration and joint position sense fully preserved on that same side. Sensory examination of the right face shows decreased pain and temperature as well. Which structure and side best explain the trunk and limb finding?","choices":{"A":"The right medial lemniscus, above the level of decussation","B":"The left spinothalamic tract, below the level of decussation","C":"The right spinothalamic tract, above the level of decussation","D":"The right dorsal columns, before they ascend to the medulla","E":"The left corticospinal tract, above its decussation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Spinothalamic fibers cross near their spinal level of entry and then ascend contralaterally, so a right brainstem spinothalamic lesion produces contralateral, left-sided, loss of pain and temperature over the trunk and limbs while sparing vibration and proprioception, which travel in the separate medial lemniscus; the accompanying right facial pain and temperature loss localizes the level to the brainstem, where the ipsilateral spinal trigeminal tract runs alongside the spinothalamic fibers. The medial lemniscus carries vibration and proprioception rather than pain and temperature, so its damage would not spare those modalities as described here. A lesion below the spinal level of decussation would not produce this pattern together with facial involvement. The dorsal columns carry vibration and proprioception, not pain and temperature. The corticospinal tract produces weakness rather than a sensory dissociation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A dissociated pattern of contralateral body pain-temperature loss combined with ipsilateral facial pain-temperature loss is a classic sign that localizes precisely to the lateral brainstem tegmentum. COMLEX: Testing pinprick sensation on both the face and the trunk in the same encounter helps rapidly distinguish a brainstem from a spinal cord lesion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 7 — relating medullary vascular territories to the anterior spinal artery, vertebral artery, and posterior inferior cerebellar artery, the vessel-to-structure logic underlying this spinothalamic and spinal trigeminal localization","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The medial lemniscus carries v"}},{"id":"sdl24_q08","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Predict deficits from lesions affecting the corticospinal tract, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic tract, sympathetic fibers, cerebellar peduncles, and cranial nerve nuclei.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman develops sudden left-sided limb clumsiness and unsteady gait, with a tendency to fall toward the left. Strength, reflexes, and sensation in the limbs are entirely normal, and there is no facial weakness or hearing loss. Which structure best explains this isolated finding?","choices":{"A":"The right inferior cerebellar peduncle, from a crossed pathway","B":"The left medial lemniscus, carrying limb proprioceptive fibers","C":"The left inferior cerebellar peduncle, or restiform body","D":"The left corticospinal tract, producing an upper motor pattern","E":"The left vestibular nuclear complex, producing pure vertigo"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The inferior cerebellar peduncle, or restiform body, carries uncrossed spinocerebellar afferents and crossed olivocerebellar afferents into the ipsilateral cerebellar hemisphere, so its isolated damage produces ipsilateral limb ataxia and gait instability with a tendency to fall toward that same side, while sparing strength, reflexes, and sensation, exactly as described here. The medial lemniscus carries conscious proprioception and vibration, so its damage would produce measurable sensory loss, which is absent here, a contralateral peduncle would not explain left-sided findings, corticospinal tract damage produces spastic weakness rather than isolated ataxia with preserved strength, and isolated vestibular nuclear damage would produce prominent vertigo and nystagmus rather than this limb and gait ataxia pattern alone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Isolated ipsilateral limb and gait ataxia with a tendency to fall toward the lesioned side, and normal strength, localizes specifically to the inferior cerebellar peduncle rather than to any long motor tract. COMLEX: Falling toward the side of a brainstem or cerebellar lesion, rather than away from it, is a reliable bedside lateralizing sign.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — localizing the inferior cerebellar peduncle posterolaterally within the medulla, the anatomic basis for why its damage produces isolated ipsilateral ataxia","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The medial lemniscus carries c"}},{"id":"sdl24_q09","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Predict deficits from lesions affecting the corticospinal tract, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic tract, sympathetic fibers, cerebellar peduncles, and cranial nerve nuclei.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man has hoarseness and difficulty swallowing after a brainstem infarct. Laryngoscopy shows the left vocal cord fails to move, and the soft palate droops on the left with uvula deviation to the right on phonation. Limb strength and sensation are normal. Which nucleus has most likely been damaged?","choices":{"A":"The left dorsal vagal nucleus, a parasympathetic nucleus","B":"The left hypoglossal nucleus, supplying intrinsic tongue muscles","C":"The right nucleus ambiguus, from a crossed corticobulbar pathway","D":"The left solitary nucleus, receiving taste and visceral afferents","E":"The left nucleus ambiguus, supplying pharyngeal and laryngeal muscles"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The nucleus ambiguus supplies the branchial motor fibers of CN IX and CN X to the muscles of the pharynx, larynx, and soft palate, so its ipsilateral damage produces vocal cord paralysis, dysphagia, and palatal droop with contralateral uvula deviation on phonation as the intact side pulls the palate away from the lesion, matching this presentation exactly. The dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus is a parasympathetic, visceral efferent nucleus that does not innervate skeletal pharyngeal or laryngeal muscle, the hypoglossal nucleus governs tongue movement rather than palatal or laryngeal function, a right-sided nucleus would not explain left-sided findings since nucleus ambiguus function is not meaningfully bilateral at this level, and the solitary nucleus is a sensory relay for taste and visceral afferents rather than a motor nucleus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Nucleus ambiguus damage produces the classic ipsilateral vocal cord paralysis and palatal droop with contralateral uvula deviation, a frequently tested cranial nerve nucleus localization. COMLEX: Asking a patient to say 'ah' and watching for uvula deviation away from the lesioned side is a quick bedside test of nucleus ambiguus or CN IX/X function.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 → Obj 8 — predicting clinical findings from lesions involving the nucleus ambiguus, now applied within this isolated cranial nerve nucleus vignette","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The dorsal motor nucleus of th","B":"The dorsal motor nucleus of th","C":"The dorsal motor nucleus of th"}},{"id":"sdl24_q10","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize vascular syndromes involving paramedian, circumferential, and cerebellar/brainstem arteries.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man has abrupt, profound hearing loss and severe vertigo with nausea in the right ear. Facial strength, facial sensation, swallowing, limb strength, limb sensation, and coordination are normal, and the brainstem appears normal on MRI. The vessel that travels with the facial and vestibulocochlear nerves through the internal acoustic meatus to the inner ear is suspected. From which artery does this vessel arise?","choices":{"A":"The posterior inferior cerebellar artery","B":"The anterior inferior cerebellar artery","C":"The superior cerebellar artery","D":"The middle meningeal artery","E":"The posterior cerebral artery"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The labyrinthine artery travels with the facial and vestibulocochlear nerves through the internal acoustic meatus and serves the inner ear; it arises from the anterior inferior cerebellar artery. Its occlusion can cause sudden ipsilateral hearing loss and vertigo without brainstem signs, whereas occlusion of the AICA itself adds lateral pontine signs such as facial weakness and limb ataxia. PICA supplies the lateral medulla and inferior cerebellum. The superior cerebellar artery supplies the rostral pons and superior cerebellum, the middle meningeal artery supplies the dura, and the posterior cerebral artery supplies the midbrain and occipital lobe.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Labyrinthine artery from AICA: inner ear ischemia causes sudden deafness and vertigo; AICA stroke adds facial, trigeminal, and cerebellar signs. COMLEX: Sudden unilateral hearing loss with vertigo in a vascular-risk patient can herald posterior circulation stroke and needs urgent evaluation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 → Obj 6 — distinguishing posterior circulation syndromes, applied here to the AICA's labyrinthine branch","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q11","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize vascular syndromes involving paramedian, circumferential, and cerebellar/brainstem arteries.","batch":2,"stem":"A 59-year-old woman has sudden right-sided facial weakness including the forehead, decreased hearing on the right, right-sided limb and gait ataxia, and decreased pain and temperature sensation over the left trunk and limbs, with normal strength throughout. A colleague asks whether this reflects paramedian or circumferential artery involvement, and which specific vessel. Which answer is correct?","choices":{"A":"Circumferential involvement, from the anterior inferior cerebellar artery","B":"Paramedian involvement, from short perforating basilar branches","C":"Circumferential involvement, from the posterior inferior cerebellar artery","D":"Paramedian involvement, from the anterior spinal artery territory","E":"Circumferential involvement, from the superior cerebellar artery"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Facial weakness, hearing loss, limb and gait ataxia, and contralateral body pain and temperature loss with preserved strength together indicate a lateral pontine syndrome from the anterior inferior cerebellar artery, a long circumferential vessel that wraps around the brainstem to supply the labyrinthine circulation, facial nucleus, middle cerebellar peduncle, and lateral tegmentum while sparing the medial corticospinal fibers. Paramedian basilar branches instead spare hearing and cerebellar function while producing contralateral hemiparesis, which is absent here. Posterior inferior cerebellar artery supplies the medulla, not the pons, and would not produce facial weakness or hearing loss. Anterior spinal artery territory is medial and medullary, not lateral and pontine. Superior cerebellar artery occlusion instead spares hearing and facial strength while producing more severe deep cerebellar signs and ipsilateral Horner syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Preserved strength alongside ipsilateral face, ear, and cerebellar findings is the hallmark circumferential, lateral pontine, AICA pattern, distinguished from paramedian syndromes by which functions are spared rather than lost. COMLEX: Asking whether strength is preserved is often the fastest bedside step to separate a paramedian from a circumferential brainstem stroke.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 → Obj 4 — distinguishing medial pontine syndromes from lateral pontine syndromes, extended here to explicitly classify the responsible vessel as paramedian or circumferential","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q12","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize vascular syndromes involving paramedian, circumferential, and cerebellar/brainstem arteries.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old man with atrial fibrillation develops sudden severe left-sided limb ataxia and dysmetria that fails to improve over several weeks, along with a left miotic pupil, mild ptosis, and decreased pain and temperature sensation over the right face and body. Strength is normal throughout. A cardiac embolus is suspected. Which vessel occlusion and mechanism best explain both the severity and persistence of his cerebellar deficit?","choices":{"A":"Anterior inferior cerebellar artery, infarcting only cerebellar cortex","B":"Posterior inferior cerebellar artery, sparing the deep cerebellar nuclei","C":"Paramedian basilar branches, sparing all cerebellar tissue entirely","D":"Superior cerebellar artery, infarcting the deep nuclei directly","E":"Superior cerebellar artery, infarcting only the cerebellar cortex surface"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The superior cerebellar artery supplies the superior cerebellar peduncle and most of the deep cerebellar nuclei directly, along with the rostral lateral pontine tegmentum containing descending sympathetic fibers and the anterolateral system, so its occlusion produces severe and lasting cerebellar deficits rather than the milder, more reversible ataxia seen with cortex-only infarcts, together with ipsilateral Horner syndrome and contralateral face-and-body pain and temperature loss, exactly matching this presentation and its embolic source. AICA and cortex-only lesions would instead produce a milder, more recoverable ataxia rather than this severe persistent pattern. PICA territory is the lateral medulla and inferior cerebellum; although PICA infarcts can also cause Horner syndrome, they would add hoarseness and dysphagia and produce ipsilateral rather than contralateral facial pain and temperature loss. Paramedian basilar branches would instead spare cerebellar tissue and produce contralateral hemiparesis, which is absent here. Restricting SCA territory to cortex alone would not explain the deficit's unusual severity and poor recovery.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cardioembolic stroke from atrial fibrillation is a classic mechanism for posterior circulation events, and SCA infarcts are distinctively severe and persistent because the artery supplies the deep cerebellar nuclei rather than cortex alone. COMLEX: The combination of ipsilateral Horner syndrome, severe ataxia, and contralateral facial and body pain-temperature loss should prompt a cardiac source workup alongside vascular imaging.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 → Obj 7 — differentiating AICA, basilar paramedian, and SCA-related patterns, now extended by tying the severity-and-persistence distinction to the cerebellar peduncle and deep nuclear territory specifically supplied by SCA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"AICA and cortex-only lesions w","B":"PICA territory is the lateral ","E":"Restricting SCA territory to c"}},{"id":"sdl24_bloom_q01","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize vascular syndromes involving paramedian, circumferential, and cerebellar/brainstem arteries.","batch":3,"stem":"A 57-year-old smoker has a 1-hour history of sudden vertigo, hoarseness, and difficulty swallowing. Exam findings:\n- Absent gag reflex and palatal droop on the right\n- Decreased pain and temperature sensation over the right face\n- Decreased pain and temperature sensation over the left trunk and limbs\n- Right-sided limb ataxia and a right miotic pupil with mild ptosis\nThis patient's condition is most likely caused by ischemia in a region supplied by the ___.","choices":{"A":"anterior inferior cerebellar","B":"superior cerebellar artery","C":"basilar paramedian perforators","D":"posterior spinal artery","E":"posterior inferior cerebellar"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The combination of ipsilateral nucleus ambiguus, gag and palatal, and spinal trigeminal findings with contralateral body pain and temperature loss, plus ipsilateral cerebellar ataxia and Horner syndrome, is the full lateral medullary pattern supplied by the posterior inferior cerebellar artery. The anterior inferior cerebellar artery instead supplies the lateral pons and would add ipsilateral facial weakness and hearing loss, absent here. The superior cerebellar artery supplies the rostral cerebellum and superior cerebellar peduncle, producing ataxia without the lower cranial nerve or crossed sensory findings seen here. Basilar paramedian perforators supply the medial pons or medulla, midline structures, producing crossed motor rather than crossed sensory findings. The posterior spinal artery supplies the dorsal columns of the spinal cord, not the lateral medulla, and would not explain any of these brainstem findings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The lateral medullary pattern, crossed facial and body sensory loss plus ipsilateral bulbar, cerebellar, and Horner findings, is the single most tested brainstem vascular syndrome and is always linked to PICA. COMLEX: Absent gag with normal tongue protrusion is the detail that confirms a lateral, not medial, medullary process.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 7 — the tract-level deficits from corticospinal, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic, and sympathetic fiber injury, mapped here onto their responsible cerebellar and brainstem vessels","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl24_bloom_q02","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize vascular syndromes involving paramedian, circumferential, and cerebellar/brainstem arteries.","batch":3,"stem":"A 64-year-old woman has a right oculomotor palsy with a dilated pupil and contralateral ataxia and tremor. MRI shows a small medial midbrain infarct involving the exiting oculomotor fibers and the red nucleus. Medial midbrain structures, including the oculomotor, trochlear, and Edinger-Westphal nuclei and the red nucleus, are supplied by paramedian branches arising from the ___","choices":{"A":"superior cerebellar artery's medial branches","B":"P1 segment and posterior communicating artery","C":"anterior choroidal branch of the carotid","D":"quadrigeminal artery","E":"short circumferential basilar branches"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Medial regions of the midbrain receive numerous small paramedian branches from the P1 segment of the posterior cerebral artery and from the posterior communicating artery, the posteromedial group of branches from the circle of Willis; their territory includes the oculomotor, trochlear, and Edinger-Westphal nuclei, the exiting oculomotor fibers, the red nucleus, and the medial substantia nigra and crus cerebri. The superior cerebellar artery adds supply near the trochlear exit and inferior colliculus. The anterior choroidal artery supplies ventrolateral midbrain regions. The quadrigeminal artery supplies the posterior midbrain, and short circumferential basilar branches supply the lateral basilar pons.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Medial midbrain syndromes (Weber, Claude, Benedikt) arise from paramedian P1 and posterior communicating artery branches. COMLEX: A third nerve palsy with contralateral motor or cerebellar signs is a stroke, not a compressive palsy, and needs stroke workup.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 7 — the tract-level deficits from cranial nerve nucleus and red nucleus injury, mapped here onto their responsible paramedian vessels","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl24_bloom_q03","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize vascular syndromes involving paramedian, circumferential, and cerebellar/brainstem arteries.","batch":3,"stem":"Ventrolateral regions of the midbrain, including the lateral crus cerebri, the lateral substantia nigra, and the medial lemniscus, receive penetrating branches from three vessels: the quadrigeminal artery, the medial posterior choroidal artery, and a branch of the internal carotid artery that follows the course of the optic tract. That internal carotid branch is the ___","choices":{"A":"ophthalmic artery","B":"posterior communicating artery","C":"middle cerebral artery","D":"anterior choroidal artery","E":"anterior cerebral artery"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The anterior choroidal artery is a branch of the internal carotid artery that follows the general route of the optic tract and sends branches to the lateral portions of the midbrain; together with penetrating branches of the quadrigeminal artery and the medial posterior choroidal artery, it supplies the ventrolateral midbrain, including the lateral parts of the crus and substantia nigra and the medial lemniscus. The ophthalmic artery enters the orbit with the optic nerve. The posterior communicating artery contributes paramedian branches to the medial midbrain. The middle and anterior cerebral arteries are terminal branches supplying the hemispheres, not the midbrain.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The anterior choroidal artery serves the optic tract, posterior limb of the internal capsule, and parts of the lateral midbrain; its stroke classically causes contralateral hemiplegia, hemianesthesia, and hemianopia. COMLEX: An anterior circulation vessel can therefore contribute to brainstem supply, which matters when interpreting carotid territory strokes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 7 — tract-level deficits from corticospinal and medial lemniscus injury, mapped here onto the circumferential vessels of the lateral midbrain","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)","whyNot":{"E":"The middle and anterior cerebr"}},{"id":"sdl24_bloom_q04","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Predict deficits from lesions affecting the corticospinal tract, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic tract, sympathetic fibers, cerebellar peduncles, and cranial nerve nuclei.","batch":3,"stem":"A 52-year-old man develops acute left-sided limb ataxia and dysmetria. MRI shows an infarct in a left cerebellar white matter bundle just proximal to its decussation in the midbrain tegmentum. This peduncle, whose efferent fibers cross before reaching the contralateral red nucleus and thalamus, is the ___.","choices":{"A":"inferior cerebellar peduncle","B":"superior cerebellar peduncle","C":"middle cerebellar peduncle","D":"medial longitudinal fasciculus","E":"vestibulospinal tract"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The superior cerebellar peduncle carries the major efferent output of the cerebellum, the dentatorubrothalamic pathway, which decussates in the caudal midbrain before reaching the contralateral red nucleus and thalamus; a lesion proximal to this decussation therefore produces ataxia ipsilateral to the cerebellar hemisphere of origin, explaining the left-sided ataxia from this left-sided peduncular lesion. The inferior cerebellar peduncle (restiform body) carries mostly afferent fibers into the cerebellum and does not carry the crossing efferent output described here. The middle cerebellar peduncle carries pontocerebellar afferent fibers into the cerebellum, unrelated to the efferent decussation in question. The medial longitudinal fasciculus links the vestibular and ocular motor nuclei and carries no cerebellar efferents to the red nucleus or thalamus. The vestibulospinal tract descends from the vestibular nuclei to the spinal cord and plays no role in this ascending cerebellar efferent pathway.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The superior cerebellar peduncle is the only cerebellar peduncle whose major fiber pathway decussates, a detail that determines whether ataxia from a peduncular lesion appears ipsilateral or, after the crossing point, effectively contralateral. COMLEX: This decussation point is a favorite detail for distinguishing superior from inferior and middle peduncle lesions on imaging-based questions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 8 — the same cerebellar peduncle anatomy applied there to AICA/PICA vascular territories, here isolated to its tract-level decussation instead","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl24_bloom_q05","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Predict deficits from lesions affecting the corticospinal tract, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic tract, sympathetic fibers, cerebellar peduncles, and cranial nerve nuclei.","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has right-sided ptosis and miosis. Sweating is absent over the right face, arm, and trunk. This distribution of anhidrosis, together with the ptosis and miosis, most likely results from a lesion of the ___.","choices":{"A":"second-order neuron","B":"third-order neuron","C":"oculomotor parasympathetic fibers","D":"first-order neuron","E":"ciliary ganglion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The sympathetic pathway to the face and body runs through three neurons in series: first-order neurons descend from the hypothalamus through the lateral brainstem tegmentum and cervical cord to the ciliospinal center at T1-T2; a lesion anywhere along this long first-order path, such as a lateral medullary or lateral pontine stroke, produces anhidrosis over the entire ipsilateral face, arm, and trunk, exactly as seen here, in addition to ptosis and miosis. A second-order (preganglionic) neuron lesion, for example from a Pancoast tumor near the sympathetic chain, produces anhidrosis limited to the face only, since sweat fibers to the arm and trunk have already branched off by that level. A third-order (postganglionic) neuron lesion, such as from carotid artery dissection, characteristically spares sweating altogether, since facial sweat fibers travel with the external, not internal, carotid artery beyond that point. Oculomotor parasympathetic fibers and the ciliary ganglion constrict the pupil and have no role in eyelid elevation or sweating, so their lesions would give a dilated pupil rather than miosis with anhidrosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The extent of anhidrosis, whole-body versus face-only versus none, is the single feature that localizes a Horner syndrome to a first-, second-, or third-order neuron lesion, one of the most consistently tested localization ladders. COMLEX: Widespread ipsilateral anhidrosis in Horner syndrome should prompt evaluation for a central (first-order) lesion such as brainstem stroke, not a peripheral cause.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 8 — the same descending sympathetic pathway tested there via a lateral medullary vessel vignette, here separated into its three-neuron localization ladder","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)","whyNot":{"A":"A second-order (preganglionic)"}},{"id":"sdl24_bloom_q06","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Predict deficits from lesions affecting the corticospinal tract, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic tract, sympathetic fibers, cerebellar peduncles, and cranial nerve nuclei.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has vertical diplopia worse on downgaze and head tilt, with a weak left superior oblique muscle on exam. The trochlear nerve is the only cranial nerve whose fibers fully decussate before exiting the brainstem. Given this, a lesion confined to the trochlear nucleus itself, rather than to the nerve fascicle or nerve after it exits, would be expected to lie on the ___ side.","choices":{"A":"right","B":"left","C":"bilateral","D":"either","E":"neither"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because trochlear nerve fibers decussate completely within the midbrain before exiting dorsally, a nuclear lesion produces a contralateral superior oblique palsy, opposite to what would occur with a fascicular or peripheral nerve lesion after the decussation, both of which cause ipsilateral weakness; since this patient's weak muscle is on the left, a nuclear-level lesion would have to be on the right. A left-sided nuclear lesion would instead be expected to cause a right, not left, superior oblique weakness, the opposite of what is observed. A bilateral lesion would be expected to cause bilateral superior oblique weakness, not the isolated unilateral finding described. Either side is incorrect because the decussation makes the nuclear lesion side specifically predictable, not ambiguous. Neither side is incorrect because a nuclear lesion, though rare, remains anatomically capable of producing this exact contralateral pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CN IV is the only cranial nerve with a fully decussating nucleus, making it the single exception where a nuclear lesion causes a contralateral, not ipsilateral, deficit, a classic testable exception. COMLEX: This decussation point is also why CN IV is the most vulnerable cranial nerve to shearing trauma at the dorsal midbrain exit site.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 5 — the same dorsal midbrain anatomy tested there for the pretectal light-near dissociation vignette, here applied to the trochlear nucleus decussation exception","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)","whyNot":{"B":"A left-sided nuclear lesion wo","D":"Either side is incorrect becau","E":"Neither side is incorrect beca"}}]};
